{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,180p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-floatkind/test/yang/clojure_float_kind_test.cljc; rg -n 'defn.*run-ast|defn.*repl-session|defn.*read-one|floatkind-signoff|java -XX|syntax' collab/yang-clojure/1795714268000-architect-floatkind-signoff-r2.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\"","aggregated_output":"(ns yang.clojure-float-kind-test\n  \"Float-kind portability r1 (architect ruling 1795693415000, RULING 5):\n   the seven acceptance gates of Clojure floating-literal kind preservation\n   from source to Data/UAST - real-source kind through the frontend source\n   entry and the standard REPL/catalog route, structural and lexical\n   preservation, the runtime semantics oracle independent of digests, the\n   API/build carrier boundary, cross-host artifact and legacy custody, and\n   the profile/revision claims.\n\n   The reader under test is the frontend's own source entry\n   (yang.clojure/read-forms), the same one the standard catalog installs,\n   so a green gate here is a claim about the shipped frontend, not about a\n   test-private reader. The REPL-route gates run where the shell runs\n   (JVM and Node); the artifact gates run on every host that can load this\n   namespace.\"\n            (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\n            [dao.host.bytes :as bytes]\n            [dao.host.integer :as integer-host]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [dao.stream.base64 :as base64]\n            [yang.clojure :as yang]\n            [yang.clojure.numeric :as clj-numeric]\n            [yang.clojure.prelude]\n            [yang.frontend :as frontend]\n            [yin.repl :as repl]\n            [yin.repl.frontends :as frontends]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.ast-walker :as ast-walker]\n            [yin.vm.data :as data]\n            [yin.vm.module :as module]\n            [yin.vm.test-utils :as tu]\n            #?@(:jolt []\n                :cljd [[\"dart:io\" :as dart-io]]\n                :clj [[clojure.java.io :as io]\n                      [clojure.tools.reader :as tools-reader]\n                      [clojure.tools.reader.reader-types :as tools-rt]\n                      [clojure.walk :as walk]\n                      [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ringbuffer]\n                      [yang.clojure.core-impl]\n                      [yang.python.antlr.clj-runtime]\n                      [yin.vm.semantic :as sr]\n                      [yin.vm.semantic.code :as semantic-code]\n                      [yin.vm.semantic.linearize :as semantic-linearize]\n                      [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as ucf-rows]]\n                :cljs [[cljs.tools.reader :as tools-reader]\n                       [cljs.tools.reader.reader-types :as tools-rt]\n                       [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ringbuffer]\n                       [yin.vm.semantic :as sr]\n                       [yin.vm.semantic.code :as semantic-code]\n                       [yin.vm.semantic.linearize :as semantic-linearize]\n                       [yin.vm.ucf.rows :as ucf-rows]])))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Shared helpers\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def ^:private data-equiv\n  \"The exported kind-strict Data equivalence predicate.\"\n  (get data/data-module 'equiv))\n\n(declare run-library)\n\n\n(defn- read-one\n  \"The single form of `source` through the frontend source entry.\"\n  [source]\n  (first (yang/read-forms source)))\n\n\n(defn- compiled-value\n  \"The literal :value of the compiled single form of `source`.\"\n  [source]\n  (:value (yang/compile (read-one source))))\n\n\n(defn- run-ast\n  \"Run compiled `ast` on the ast-walker with the plain registry.\"\n  [ast]\n  (-> (tu/create-vm {:primitives vm/primitives\n                     :make-stream tu/make-stream\n                     :capability-secret tu/secret})\n      (vm/eval ast)\n      vm/value))\n\n\n(defn- semantic-root\n  \"The canonical semantic-bytecode root of the compiled form of `source`.\"\n  [source]\n  (:root (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode (yang/compile (read-one source)))))\n\n\n(defn- host-read-file\n  [path]\n  #?(:jolt nil\n     :cljd (.readAsBytesSync (dart-io/File. path))\n     :clj (java.nio.file.Files/readAllBytes (.toPath (io/file path)))\n     :cljs (.readFileSync (js/require \"fs\") path)\n     :default nil))\n\n\n(def ^:private repl-route?\n  \"The REPL/catalog source-reading route runs where the source reader ships\n   (JVM and Node); the Dart and Jolt legs exercise carrier compilation,\n   cross-host artifact loading/execution, legacy custody, and capability claims.\"\n  #?(:jolt false :cljd false :clj true :cljs true :default false))\n\n\n(defn- drain-result\n  \"The :repl/result value of a shell's output medium, oldest first.\"\n  [state]\n  (let [output (:output-stream state)\n        cursor (:dao.stream/cursor (stream/cursor output :dao.stream/oldest))]\n    (loop [cursor cursor, value ::none]\n      (let [step (stream/next output cursor)]\n        (if (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome step))\n          (let [v (:dao.stream/value step)]\n            (if (= :repl/result (:type v))\n              (recur (:dao.stream/cursor step) (:value v))\n              (recur (:dao.stream/cursor step) value)))\n          value)))))\n\n\n(defn- repl-session-result\n  \"The result value of evaluating `line` through the standard REPL/catalog\n  route (the frontends the shell installs), semantic VM.\"\n  [line]\n  #?(:jolt nil\n     :cljd nil\n     :clj (drain-result (first (repl/eval-input (frontends/create-state {:vm-type :semantic}) line)))\n     :cljs (drain-result (first (repl/eval-input (frontends/create-state {:vm-type :semantic}) line)))\n     :default nil))\n\n\n(defn- repl-text\n  \"The shell text of evaluating `line` through the standard route.\"\n  [line]\n  #?(:jolt nil\n     :cljd nil\n     :clj (second (repl/eval-input (frontends/create-state {:vm-type :semantic}) line))\n     :cljs (second (repl/eval-input (frontends/create-state {:vm-type :semantic}) line))\n     :default nil))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; GATE 1 - real source strings, every supported numeric category\n;; =============================================================================\n\n\n(def ^:private float-tokens\n  \"Every binary-floating token class the ruling names: integral floats at\n   0, 1, -1, at and beyond 2^53, exponent spellings, negative zero, a\n   small positive token that rounds to zero (underflow), and a value at\n   the top of the range. [token expected-double-bits-hex].\"\n  [[\"0.0\" \"0000000000000000\"]\n   [\"1.0\" \"3ff0000000000000\"]\n   [\"-1.0\" \"bff0000000000000\"]\n   [\"1e0\" \"3ff0000000000000\"]\n   [\"1.5\" \"3ff8000000000000\"]\n   [\"-0.0\" \"8000000000000000\"]\n   [\"9007199254740992.0\" \"4340000000000000\"]\n   [\"9007199254740994.0\" \"4340000000000001\"]\n   [\"1e19\" \"43e158e460913d00\"]\n   [\"1.5e-5\" \"3eef75104d551d69\"]\n   [\"5e-324\" \"0000000000000001\"]\n   [\"1e-400\" \"0000000000000000\"]\n   [\"2.\" \"4000000000000000\"]\n   [\"+1.5\" \"3ff8000000000000\"]\n   [\"1E5\" \"40f86a0000000000\"]])\n\n\n(def ^:private integer-tokens\n  \"Integer controls: plain, signed, radix, octal, and the N-suffixed big\n   integer (the exact carrier). [token expected-value].\"\n  [[\"0\" 0] [\"1\" 1] [\"-1\" -1] [\"+7\" 7] [\"42\" 42]\n   [\"0x10\" 16] [\"017\" 15] [\"2r101\" 5] [\"36rZZ\" 1295]\n   [\"9007199254740991\" 9007199254740991]])\n\n5:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\n---\\n\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\n\\n## Core Philosophy\\n\\nEverything is data.\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\n\\n### Foundational Axioms\\n\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\n   At system scale, a \\\"system\\\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\n\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\n\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\\n\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\n\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\n\\n## Streams\\n\\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\n\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\nthe stream's own.\\n\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\n\\n### Host Boundaries\\n\\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\\n\\n## Architectural Commitments\\n\\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\\n\\n## Design Principles\\n\\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\\n\\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\\n\\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\\n  ordinary streams.\\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\\n  auto-index observer.\\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\\n\\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\nretired `:global` tag in\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\\n\\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\\n\\n## Code Style\\n\\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\\n- Avoid cleverness.\\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\\n\\n## Development Workflow\\n\\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\\n  leaves to review.\\n\\n### Problem Decomposition\\n\\nIf a problem appears complex:\\n1. Decompose it into streams.\\n2. Identify invariants.\\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\\n4. Make causality explicit.\\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\\n\\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\\n\\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n9:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"git status --short; git log -4 --oneline; rg -n '1795709372000|RULING|4-vs-12|cached|classpath' collab/yang-clojure/*float* docs/agents/build-n-test.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\" M collab/jolt/1795251000000-engineer-p2b-resume.gpt-6.2-sol.stdout.log\\n M collab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md\\n m r3-fix-wt\\n?? collab/general/1795750000000-orchestrator-splits-weight-refresh-sequencing.md\\n?? collab/general/1795760000000-qa-engineer-dart-wrapper-proof.prompt.md\\n?? collab/general/1795765000000-qa-engineer-include-test-ab.prompt.md\\n?? collab/general/1795770000000-orchestrator-dart-wrapper-r2-decision.md\\n?? collab/general/1795780000000-orchestrator-dart-wrapper-r3-continue.md\\n?? collab/jolt/1795245000000-engineer-p2final-consolidation.gpt-6.2-sol.stdout.log\\n?? collab/jolt/1795245000000-engineer-p2final-consolidation.prompt.md\\n?? collab/jolt/1795250000000-engineer-p2b-compiled-session-boot.gpt-6.2-sol.findings.md\\n?? collab/jolt/1795254000000-reviewer-p2b-agyflash.gemini-3.8-flash.findings.md\\n?? collab/jolt/1795254000000-reviewer-p2b-agyflash.gemini-3.8-flash.stdout.log\\n?? collab/jolt/1795254000000-reviewer-p2b-agyflash.prompt.md\\n?? collab/jolt/1795309000001-engineer-metrics-allow-prune.gpt-6.2-sol.stdout.log\\n?? collab/jolt/1795309000001-engineer-metrics-allow-prune.prompt.md\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795697994000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.prompt.md\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795714268000-architect-floatkind-signoff-r2.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795714268000-architect-floatkind-signoff-r2.prompt.md\\n685d47bd docs(agents): deepseek cost accounting — wrapper costUSD is a list-price estimate; reconcile against the platform dashboard (measured 8.64-estimate vs 0.21-actual, cache-heavy resumed review)\\n255b1024 docs(blog): center yin-yang pipeline diagram on dao.stream\\nbab7640f docs(ledger): GREEN DECLARATION — master green at 9bd665fe; tri-leg confirmation matrix all rc=0 (jupiter JVM 7.9h, shiva node 12.1h, shiva dart 10.1h); every 20261123 red family closed with landed countersigned fixes\\nd49ff4b7 docs(agents): correct the dispatch recipe — zsh -ic (interactive subshell loads .zshrc: mise activate + nvm), replacing the hand-built PATH line (owner correction 2026-11-26: codex is already on the user's PATH)\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md:15:Write the FAILING tests first — RULING 5's seven acceptance gates in test form — run them RED, record the red evidence, THEN implement. Every acceptance gate lands as a test before the code that makes it pass. The red run is itself deliverable evidence (attach logs). Do not implement first and backfill tests.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md:19:1. **The ruling (binding, normative)**: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795693415000-architect-float-kind-portability-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md — RULING 1 (impact), RULING 2 (option b: frontend-owned lexical preservation; carrier admission; execution reboxing), RULING 3 (revision bump, migration, legacy custody), RULING 4 (honest portability statement + doc placements), RULING 5 (the complete fix-round contract: file boundaries + seven acceptance gates + re-pin protocol). Read ALL of it.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md:29:1. **RED — write the seven acceptance gates as portable tests** (new namespace per RULING 5's test boundaries, e.g. test/yang/clojure_float_kind_test.cljc + the corpus/semantic-oracle extensions it specifies). Gate 1 first (real identical source strings on JVM and Node through the frontend source entry + REPL/catalog route: 0.0, 1.0, -1.0, 1e0, 1.5, -0.0, integral floats at/beyond 2^53, small positive float rounding to zero; integer controls). Run RED on JVM + Node, save logs to collab/yang-clojure/<new-ts>-floatkind-evidence/.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md:31:3. **The Clojure ingestion stage** (the hard piece — the ruling REQUIRES a lexical-coverage demonstration BEFORE selecting the minimal mechanism): demonstrate coverage of comments/strings/chars/quote/metadata/dispatch/discarded forms/reader conditionals + numeric tokens with the retained-kind mechanism, THEN implement. No regex-over-source, no postwalk of read Numbers, no print-round-trip, no process-wide reader monkeypatch. Scope per RULING 5's file boundaries (src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc + a focused module under src/cljc/yang/clojure/; repl.cljc/frontends.cljc routing; the audit set).\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md:32:4. **Carrier admission + execution reboxing** per RULING 2 (forms compiler admits float64 carriers incl. nested/quoted; the portable profile routes floating ops carrier-aware with results reboxed; do not overhaul legacy arithmetic).\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md:34:6. **Migration per RULING 3**: new Clojure frontend revision + lowering/support profile (retaining the constructed-collection ordering contract in the identity), legacy custody tests (archive pre-fix JS integer-bearing + JVM float-bearing bytes, load/execute under the new system without recompilation), doc placements per RULING 4.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md:35:7. **Re-pin** per RULING 5's protocol — only after the gates explain the change: complete corpus key/C1/provenance references, pre-fix archives preserved, historical records untouched.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md:39:RULING 5's file boundaries exactly. Outside them (codec, VM equality, upstream reader): report as dependencies requiring separate architectural decision — do NOT fix incidentally. No unrelated arithmetic redesign, no float-key policy expansion, no new persistent annotation, no global reader monkeypatch. kondo 0 errors on touched files.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795693415000-architect-float-kind-portability-ruling.prompt.md:22:## Rule on (deliver RULING entries with normative text; READ-ONLY, no implementation authorized)\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795697994000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md:16:1. **The ruling (binding, normative)**: collab/yang-clojure/1795693415000-architect-float-kind-portability-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md — all five rulings. RULING 5 is your contract: file boundaries, the seven acceptance gates, the re-pin protocol.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795697994000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md:27:1. RED: the seven gates as portable tests (RULING 5's gate list is your test plan; gate 1 first: identical source strings JVM vs Node through the frontend entry + REPL/catalog route; the token list, controls, and refusal categories are specified there). Run RED on JVM and Node locally on jupiter (48 cores; single-namespace + targeted runs are fine; one Dart lane at a time; Jolt via jolt_j2_lane with --jobs 16 per build-n-test). RED evidence: collab/yang-clojure/<new-ts>-floatkind-evidence/.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795697994000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md:29:3. Migration per RULING 3: new Clojure frontend revision + profile (retaining the collection-ordering contract), legacy custody (the committed capture pair is your gate-6 seed — extend per RULING 5 gate 6), doc placements per RULING 4.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795697994000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md:30:4. Re-pin per RULING 5's protocol, only after the gates explain the change.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795697994000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md:34:RULING 5's file boundaries exactly; outside them (codec, VM equality, upstream reader) report as dependencies — never fix incidentally. kondo 0 errors on touched files. Deliverable: collab/yang-clojure/<new-ts>-engineer-float-kind-portability-r1.gemini-4-neon.findings.md (write in the clone; the orchestrator mirrors). RED evidence + per-gate commands/counts per host are mandatory. Do not end the turn with runnable work remaining. Commit NOTHING — the orchestrator commits after review.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:11:The requested darkstar mirror is NOT the landed tip: `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-floatkind` has HEAD and its locally cached origin branch at `8214a3ed8d1e6627cf9a4eb6fdb896076ba22316`, plus an uncommitted change to `test/yang/clojure_float_kind_test.cljc`. It lacks the engineer/reviewer findings and cited evidence directory. The named engineer findings also do not exist in the main darkstar checkout. `git cat-file -t 6a7fb586` there fails.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:19:2. **B-2 / P1 — The source set reader silently drops false/nil and every later occurrence.** `src/cljc/yang/clojure/reader.cljc:374–384`, specifically `(if-let [x (first xs)] ...)`, treats a false/nil element as exhaustion. Live results: `#{false 1.0 2}` reads as `[#{}]`; `#{nil 1.0}` also reads as `[#{}]`; `#{1 false 2}` reads as `[#{1}]`. The standard catalog evaluates `#{false 1.0 2}` to `#{}`. This directly violates RULING 2 and Gate 2 occurrence preservation, including loss of the floating occurrence. Test sequence presence, not element truthiness; cover false/nil at all positions and duplicate checking after them on JVM and Node.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:35:| RULING 1: semantic oracle | `gate4-data-oracle` calls exported Data operations and checks scalar/nested integer-vs-float inequivalence, float equivalence, admission, signed zero and NaN. `gate4-unboxed-control-distinguishes` executes boxed/unboxed forms and compares both semantic equivalence and roots. | Substantive oracle exists; B-1 prevents loading it as landed, and B-3 shows missing variadic source-equality coverage. |\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:36:| RULING 2: option b | `reader.cljc:648–654` materializes the original token through `numeric-token/materialize :clojure` before collection construction. No Number-value reconstruction, source-wide regex substitution, print round-trip, global reader replacement, or postwalk of lost numeric values is used. Lexical coverage is described in reader source and engineer findings; Gate 2 supplies actual tests. | Correct architectural layer; coverage is insufficient to certify the replacement grammar and occurrence preservation (B-2, B-4, B-5, B-6). |\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:38:| RULING 3: identity | `frontends.clojure-manifest` declares `c1-3`, `:yang.clojure/constructed-collections-float-kind-v1`, and `:yang.clojure/c1-float-kind`. Grammar identifies `yang.clojure.reader`, with `:source-text` on JVM/Node and `:forms-only` on Dart/Jolt. | Names and capability distinction match the landed contract; no c1-2 relabeling. |\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:39:| RULING 3: custody/history | Both archived c1-2 payloads match the recorded SHA-256 values; independent loading, execution and whole-payload decode/re-encode succeeded. Historical c1-1 and cc4 paths are absent from the change set. | Verified, with details below. |\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:40:| RULING 4: documentation | Codec cross-reference, `yang.antlr.md` §8.8.1, implementation documentation, prelude producer obligations, API/REPL docstrings, and collection-corpus commentary are present. They distinguish portable Data from source/forms and state the JS bare integral Number contract honestly. | Required locations substantially covered. Claims of completed source preservation cannot override the confirmed counterexamples; reconcile after fixes. |\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:41:| RULING 5: boundaries | No production codec, VM equality, base VM primitive table, C1, UAST or bytecode format change. Shared factory and narrow PHP/Python/TS adapters follow the owner's round-brief addition. `yang.io` is an additional actual source-ingestion route. Shared prelude/module publication sources remain unchanged. | No forbidden codec/global-reader shortcut. The 700-line replacement reader has demonstrated compatibility defects; its scope cannot be accepted merely because the path is authorized. |\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:42:| RULING 5: pins | Existing corpus reference rows remain; the integral-float row adds one common root/key/C1/instruction-provenance reference and an unmodified-source comparison. Shared runtime closure pins and other frontend revisions are not reminted. | Bounded pin changes are explained; preserve history and rerun relevant gates after fixes. Do not repin away these failures. |\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:61:Probes ran on jupiter from its existing cached classpath, without dependency resolution or rebuilding:\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795714268000-architect-floatkind-signoff-r2.prompt.md:8:Role: Architect (numeric semantics). Your sign-off r1 (1795709372000) blocked merge on B-1..B-7. The author resolved all seven (r2 commit f091597e on fix/float-kind-portability; deepseek re-reviewed APPROVE). Verify the corrected tip AS LANDED with your execution-probe method — it caught what the log review missed last time.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795714268000-architect-floatkind-signoff-r2.prompt.md:16:- B-5: capture-avoiding FnArgRef placeholders + fresh-param-sym (4-vs-12 fixed, deterministic canonical output).\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795714268000-architect-floatkind-signoff-r2.prompt.md:24:jupiter ~/ci/datomworld-floatkind, tip f091597e (verify via ls-remote). Your prior probe recipe (cached classpath + test) should work.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795714268000-architect-floatkind-signoff-r2.prompt.md:28:Re-run your counterexample probes: B-2 (#{false 1.0 2}, #{nil 1.0}), B-3 (clj-= 1 1 false), B-4 (:yang.clojure.reader/discard in all positions), B-5 (the 4-vs-12 case), B-6 (x', scalar backtick). Confirm B-7's test exists and crosses the boundary pre-pause. Confirm r2 diff touches only the RULING 5 files. If clean: SIGN-OFF with the merge instruction (one merge, fix+custody+reviews together; master recheck at landing).\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795714268000-architect-floatkind-signoff-r2.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:8:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_3\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"git status --short; git log -4 --oneline; rg -n '1795709372000|RULING|4-vs-12|cached|classpath' collab/yang-clojure/*float* docs/agents/build-n-test.md\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.prompt.md:24:1. RULING 1: the semantics oracle is real (kind-strict data/equiv asserted, mutation control present).\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.prompt.md:25:2. RULING 2: option b implemented — reader.cljc consumes lexical info at token time; the banned shortcuts absent; lexical-coverage demonstration exists.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.prompt.md:26:3. RULING 3: revision c1-3 (not c1-2), profile names per contract, legacy custody pairs load/execute without recompilation, historical records untouched (c1-1 fixture, cc4 snapshots).\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.prompt.md:27:4. RULING 4: docs placed in the four named locations; the honest portability statement present.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.prompt.md:28:5. RULING 5: file boundaries respected (diff against origin/master); seven gates present and green; re-pin per protocol.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.prompt.md:33:READ-ONLY except your findings: collab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.findings.md in /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/. Verdict: SIGN-OFF (state the merge instruction — this branch folds to master as one merge; note any landing-order constraints vs current master) or REMAINING-BLOCKERS with dispositions.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795693415000-architect-float-kind-portability-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:36:## RULING 1 — Impact and responsibility\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795693415000-architect-float-kind-portability-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:53:## RULING 2 — One fix layer: frontend-owned lexical preservation (option b)\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795693415000-architect-float-kind-portability-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:78:## RULING 3 — Migration, legacy loading, and revisions\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795693415000-architect-float-kind-portability-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:92:## RULING 4 — Honest portability statement pending the fix\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795693415000-architect-float-kind-portability-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:113:## RULING 5 — Future fix-round contract; not dispatched\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795693415000-architect-float-kind-portability-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:123:- Documentation changes are the locations in RULING 4. Production codec/VM equality changes and upstream toolchain patches are outside this contract; a newly discovered need for them requires a separate architectural decision.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795693415000-architect-float-kind-portability-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:142:This is a read-only architectural investigation plus this findings file. I inspected current source, locally installed upstream reader source, the r3 raw failure log, and the r2 ruling. I ran bounded JVM expressions using the existing cached classpath, without rebuilding: `yang.clojure/read-forms` returns Long for `1`, Double for `1.0`, `0.0`, `1e0`, `-0.0`, `1.5`, and `9007199254740992.0`. For `1.0`, exported `data/equiv` against integer 1 is false, `admit-key?` is false, and `content=` against integer 1 is false; integer 1 yields true for those three checks. These establish the live JVM side of the defect.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795693415000-architect-float-kind-portability-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md:146:I also loaded only the existing cached Node bootstrap dependencies in memory, stopping before the REPL main entry. Both `cljs.tools.reader` and `cljs.reader` return the same bare Number for `1`, `1.0`, and `1e0`; cached Jing classifies them as integer and gives them equal content. Negative zero remains floating; `1.5` remains floating. The unsafe integral float probe reaches the expected unsupported-value refusal. These cached JS artifacts are older than current source: an attempted exported `data/equiv` probe found that export absent in the cache. Consequently I do **not** claim a freshly built Node VM-semantic suite or current four-host execution result. The current JS semantic consequence is derived from the checked-in portable Data/dispatch code, not represented as a green Node test. The earlier probe's refusal terminated its final row; it was not a complete test run.\\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:214:classes and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:232:builds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\\ndocs/agents/build-n-test.md:766:git diff --cached | grep -E '^\\\\+(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' && echo CONFLICT-MARKERS-STAGED\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:5:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'pwd && git status --short && cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld\\\\n M collab/jolt/1795251000000-engineer-p2b-resume.gpt-6.2-sol.stdout.log\\\\n M collab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md\\\\n m r3-fix-wt\\\\n?? collab/general/1795750000000-orchestrator-splits-weight-refresh-sequencing.md\\\\n?? collab/general/1795760000000-qa-engineer-dart-wrapper-proof.prompt.md\\\\n?? collab/general/1795765000000-qa-engineer-include-test-ab.prompt.md\\\\n?? collab/jolt/1795250000000-engineer-p2b-compiled-session-boot.gpt-6.2-sol.findings.md\\\\n?? collab/jolt/1795254000000-reviewer-p2b-agyflash.gemini-3.8-flash.findings.md\\\\n?? collab/jolt/1795254000000-reviewer-p2b-agyflash.gemini-3.8-flash.stdout.log\\\\n?? collab/jolt/1795254000000-reviewer-p2b-agyflash.prompt.md\\\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795697994000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md\\\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\\\n?? collab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.prompt.md\\\\n---\\\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\\\n\\\\n## Core Philosophy\\\\n\\\\nEverything is data.\\\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\\\n\\\\n### Foundational Axioms\\\\n\\\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\\\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\\\n   At system scale, a \\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\\\\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\\\\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\\\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\\\n\\\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\\\n\\\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\\\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\\\\n\\\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\\\n\\\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\\\n\\\\n## Streams\\\\n\\\\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\\\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\\\\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\\\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\\\n\\\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\\\nthe stream's own.\\\\n\\\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\\\n\\\\n### Host Boundaries\\\\n\\\\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\\\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\\\\n\\\\n## Architectural Commitments\\\\n\\\\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\\\\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\\\\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\\\\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\\\\n\\\\n## Design Principles\\\\n\\\\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\\\\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\\\\n\\\\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\\\\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\\\\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\\\\n\\\\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\\\\n  ordinary streams.\\\\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\\\\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\\\\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\\\\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\\\\n  auto-index observer.\\\\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\\\\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\\\\n\\\\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\\\\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\\\\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\\\\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\\\\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\\\\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\\\nretired `:global` tag in\\\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\\\\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\\\\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\\\\n\\\\n## Code Style\\\\n\\\\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\\\\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\\\\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\\\\n- Avoid cleverness.\\\\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\\\\n\\\\n## Development Workflow\\\\n\\\\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\\\\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\\\\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\\\\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\\\\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\\\\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\\\\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\\\\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\\\\n  leaves to review.\\\\n\\\\n### Problem Decomposition\\\\n\\\\nIf a problem appears complex:\\\\n1. Decompose it into streams.\\\\n2. Identify invariants.\\\\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\\\\n4. Make causality explicit.\\\\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\\\\n\\\\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\\\\n\\\\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\\\\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\\\\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\\\\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\\\\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:12:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_5\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short && git log -7 --oneline && git rev-parse HEAD origin/master origin/fix/float-kind-portability && git diff --stat origin/master...HEAD && cat collab/yang-clojure/1795693415000-architect-float-kind-portability-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md && cat collab/yang-clojure/1795706698000-reviewer-float-kind-r1.deepseek-v4-pro.findings.md'\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\" M test/yang/clojure_float_kind_test.cljc\\\\n8214a3ed docs(collab): the binding authority documents for the float-kind round — architect ruling + round brief (with the darkstar-session supersession event)\\\\n09401506 wip(floatkind): r1 in-flight state — legacy-custody capture (gate 6 pre-fix evidence) at migration point\\\\n7f4898f4 docs(agents): harden orchestrator branch discipline per owner instruction — main checkout always on master, all work in worktrees; per-operation branch check is a hard abort gate; bare push publishes nothing when the checkout silently sits on another branch (2026-11-26 s0 incident provenance)\\\\n8c71ce86 Merge remote-tracking branch 'origin/master'\\\\nd17c59e1 merge s0: restore the session's landing continuity — LPT ordering (752a35e9), ledger addendum 3 (208b7c01), quick work-stealing (b7d39397) + findings (dcfb8b7d); the darkstar checkout was on branch s0 so these commits did not reach origin/master until now\\\\n5f9eabda fix(node-shard): raise the node worker cap — min(requested, 12, cores/2) was min(requested, 8, cores/3); shiva 24-core: 8->12 concurrent node workers, ~1-1.5GB RSS each within the 62GB host alongside the dart co-tenant; orchestrator config-level edit\\\\ndcfb8b7d collab: quick work-stealing round 1795740000000 record (landed b7d39397) — weighted shared queue + LPT + workers 16 + concurrency demonstration (4 JVMs sampled)\\\\n8214a3ed8d1e6627cf9a4eb6fdb896076ba22316\\\\nbab7640f0b162444285ab622469de1abbefbb668\\\\n8214a3ed8d1e6627cf9a4eb6fdb896076ba22316\\\\n ...kind-portability-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md | 148 ++++++\\\\n ...31000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md |  47 ++\\\\n .../yang/clojure/float-kind-legacy-jvm-float.cbor  | Bin 0 -> 1415 bytes\\\\n test/yang/clojure_float_kind_test.cljc             | 541 +++++++++++++++++++++\\\\n test/yang/floatkind_legacy_capture_test.cljc       |  54 ++\\\\n 5 files changed, 790 insertions(+)\\\\nRole: Architect (compiler provenance / numeric semantics)\\\\nCoding-Agent: codex\\\\nDate: 2026-11-26\\\\nInspected HEAD: `7f4898f474d02dc127e5e484254bc44ae7ff88f6`\\\\nAuthority: architectural ruling only; no implementation, dispatch, re-pin, or publication authorized.\\\\n\\\\n# Integral-float kind preservation on JavaScript\\\\n\\\\n**Decision: this is a runtime correctness defect in the source-to-VM portability claim. Fix it in the source frontend, before numeric token kind is lost. Select option (b): frontend-owned, token-aware source ingestion and lowering. Preserve Jing's existing numeric contract and representation. Old artifacts retain their recorded meaning; repairing their compilation requires new artifacts.**\\\\n\\\\nThe r2 acceptance of a narrowed collection-order corpus was a scope boundary, not acceptance of the numeric defect. This ruling supersedes any reading of “encoder/float-carrier territory” as a diagnosis that the encoder should infer the lost kind. It does not retroactively reopen that bounded collection-order decision or authorize work now.\\\\n\\\\n## Evidence re-derived\\\\n\\\\n1. `src/cljc/dao/host/integer.cljc:66` classifies safe integral JS Numbers, except negative zero, as exact integers. `src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:201–220` also recognizes unsafe integral Numbers for refusal and distinguishes floating content. `number-bytes` at line 748 emits integer content or refuses an unsafe integral Number before its float branch. JVM `Double` is float content, even at `1.0`. `cbor/float64` at line 242 delegates to `dao.host.number/float64-of`, whose JS carrier preserves the distinction. This matches `docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:387–425`; the codec is conforming.\\\\n2. `src/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:513–536` makes float and exact-integer `data/equiv` mutually exclusive, recursively. Its `admit-key?` at line 538 refuses float content and admits exact integers. `data/content=` and Jing `content=` are kind-strict. `src/cljc/yang/clojure/core_impl.cljc:256–261` dispatches `inc`/`dec` to `float/add`/`float/sub` or integer operations according to `data/float64?`. `clj/collection-key` in `src/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc:225` applies the key admission policy. These are observable semantics, independently of hashing.\\\\n3. `yang.clojure/compile-literal` at line 766 merely puts its argument in `{:type :literal :value ...}`. `compile-quote` does the same for quoted Data. There is no lexical provenance from which either can distinguish two bare JS Numbers with value 1.\\\\n4. The explicit source entry `yang.clojure/read-forms`, at line 2135, uses **`cljs.tools.reader`**, not an in-repository Clojure lexer. JVM uses `clojure.tools.reader`. These are upstream `org.clojure/tools.reader` code; `deps.edn` declares 1.5.0. I inspected the locally available 1.5.0 and 1.5.2 jars: `cljs/tools/reader.cljs` reads a token and calls `match-number`; `cljs/tools/reader/impl/commons.cljs:79–95` selects float syntax and returns `js/parseFloat`. It knows the distinction temporarily and discards it in its result. A general tagged-literal hook does not automatically intercept ordinary numeric literals.\\\\n5. **There is another source entry.** `yin.repl/read-forms`, `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:1333`, uses `cljs.reader` on JS and `clojure.core/read-string` on JVM. The standard Clojure binding in `src/cljc/yin/repl/frontends.cljc:135` is `:input :forms`, with `:parse identity`. `compile-input`/`compile-source` therefore pass already-read forms. Fixing only `yang.clojure/read-forms` misses the normal REPL. `yin.repl.adapter` also reads for command detection, but forwards ordinary remote input as its original string; its command sniffing must not be confused with compilation.\\\\n6. **There is a carrier-admission gap.** `yang.clojure/literal?` and `constant-form?`, lines 270–301, recognize numeric scalars through host `number?`. Jing's JS Float64 is a `deftype` carrier, not a JS Number. Directly supplying that carrier to the normal forms compiler can reach “Cannot compile unknown form type” at line 1840. Carrier insertion therefore requires bounded compiler admission work as well. The VM Data API, raw UAST literal constructors, and the forms compiler are distinct APIs; a working carrier in the first two does not prove acceptance by the third.\\\\n7. Python already boxes float tokens in `src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:350–363`; PHP does so in `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:310–315`; TypeScript boxes Number tokens in `src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/lower.cljc:428–440`. TypeScript BigInt is a separate case: “every numeric literal” here means Number literals, not BigInt literals. Python/PHP distinguish integer syntax from float syntax; TypeScript Number `1` is intentionally float64 content. Uniform representation does not mean uniform language rules for spelling `1`.\\\\n8. Host-compiled quoted `.cljc` prelude data can lose kind independently of the runtime reader. The JVM build reader can retain Double initially, but the emitted JS literal is still a bare Number. `yang.core.defquoted` is not a general numeric-preservation pass. TypeScript's explicit `(data/float64 1)` convention and Python's existing float-address discipline address that producer boundary; changing a runtime source reader cannot retroactively repair already-compiled quoted constants.\\\\n9. **Carrier-aware arithmetic is profile-specific.** `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:420–440` still binds the base `+`, `-`, `*`, comparison and equality primitives to host functions. The `inc`/`dec` evidence above is the Clojure library path, not a claim that every base primitive dispatches by Jing kind. `core-source-name` in `yang.clojure.cljc:868` is conditional on the outcome/library convention. Boxing a literal does not by itself make bare host arithmetic portable; on JS the carrier deliberately refuses numeric coercion. This is a necessary integration gate, not a reason to erase kind again.\\\\n\\\\nThe archived r3 failed comparison confirms different roots **and** occurrence/provenance digests for the former float row:\\\\n\\\\n| Field | JVM reference | Node actual |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| Root suffix | `149c168e3b766d703fc230365be4ad940bbcf487e0bff36efff9cf6e3bf08958` | `d4ee0ca368a18d7cf8010e211e50ed96df6f4ecc39befad11dd9aad01c57d5b1` |\\\\n| Keys digest | `cb7365d53d55ad30e90e700206caff405d3fe1d5faf62566df91f4f1dcd4afb7` | `f6e32612f78e3299c469e14c953e8ee53cf3226ed825f3c12b69b5080d1f42da` |\\\\n| C1 digest | `f181b911a60e6e66217fecbf93bd6623d83354c41aa0afdb9e49f10f45f69fdd` | `d8058ae46577532bdbc709df329d5c324b731abb7962ad29cf326a084ef99906` |\\\\n| Instruction provenance | `e25482dac0a59eaa0801c1faea000ad518a5ab1d7e53b704d883ff6ecf495735` | `4aaeac91b2a2a1af5e3b980c325ac60b0534913df06ee069af2f03c89224456e` |\\\\n\\\\nSource: `1795104744000-fixorder-r3-evidence/fixorder-r3-node-order-floats-divergence.log`, line 11. The boundary note has the already-reported transcription error in the JVM provenance digest; the raw log above is authoritative. The r2 findings are `1795119753581-architect-fixorder-signoff-r2.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`.\\\\n\\\\n## RULING 1 — Impact and responsibility\\\\n\\\\n**Normative:** yin.vm's source portability claim MUST treat loss of intended numeric kind as a correctness defect, not merely an address or traceability defect. Identical source text MUST NOT silently acquire integer semantics on one supported compiling host and floating semantics on another. A portable VM interpreting correctly encoded Data does not absolve a frontend that supplies the wrong Data.\\\\n\\\\n| Surface | Concrete impact and boundary |\\\\n| --- | --- |\\\\n| Source text and source forms | Bare `0.0`, `1.0`, `1e0`, negative integral floats, and floats rounded/underflowed to integral values can become integers on JS. Unsafe integral results can be refused instead of compiled. This affects top-level literals, arguments, definitions, quoted data, and nested collection contents. Once an external reader has supplied only Number 1, the forms API cannot know whether it originally read `1` or `1.0`. |\\\\n| Public host-Data APIs | An unboxed JS Number 1 already denotes integer content under the contract. A caller intending float content must supply `cbor/float64`/`data/float64` before crossing the boundary. Correctly boxed VM inputs are representable today. The forms compiler's additional carrier-admission gap must be fixed; do not advertise direct carrier forms as working everywhere today. |\\\\n| Runtime equality and dispatch | For intended float `f = 1.0`, `data/equiv(f, 1)` is false; after collapse it is true. The same distinction recurses through vectors and maps. `inc(f)` takes the float branch and returns float content; collapsed 1 takes the integer branch. A matching printed result, such as 2, is not a sufficient oracle. |\\\\n| Collections | Key admission can change from float refusal to integer acceptance. Lookup, membership, collision behavior, and constructor operand ordering can consequently differ. A host reader may already coalesce integer/float keys or set members before compilation. This ruling does not grant float-key support to Clojure-family collections: existing refusal policy remains. Do not assert that all maps universally support both keys merely because Jing distinguishes the scalar contents. |\\\\n| Prelude/library code | Host-quoted numeric constants, macro-generated forms, and library constructors are producers too. A runtime reader fix does not reach them automatically. Explicit float constructors remain necessary where those producers otherwise lose information. Float-producing arithmetic must rebox before a value is exposed, stored, captured, or resumed. |\\\\n| Stored artifacts and cross-host reload | A JS-produced artifact containing integer 1 reloads as integer 1 on JVM. It is not healed by the destination reader. A JVM-produced float artifact decodes to a Float64 carrier on JS and retains float kind. The defect is usually different artifacts produced from the same source, not the decoder randomly changing a fixed artifact. Existing profile/ABI compatibility requirements still apply. |\\\\n| Canonical addresses and provenance | Integer and float literal bytes differ; ancestor UAST roots, compiled-content ordering, call-site keys/C1 relations, format projections, modules, and addressed state can differ. Source-text identity can remain identical while derived forms differ. Sorting cannot repair wrong child content. |\\\\n| `content=` | `content=(float64(1), 1)` is false by contract. A collapsed source `1.0` produces true against integer 1 on JS. Canonical equality must remain kind-strict; weakening it would hide the defect and alter valid programs. |\\\\n\\\\nThe distinction between `data/equiv` and `content=` also remains: floating positive and negative zero are `data/equiv`, but not `content=`; NaN is not `data/equiv` to itself, while canonical NaN content has one identity. The repair MUST NOT conflate these relations.\\\\n\\\\n## RULING 2 — One fix layer: frontend-owned lexical preservation (option b)\\\\n\\\\n**Normative:** the Clojure source frontend MUST preserve the original numeric token's kind while source text is available and produce Jing float64 Data for every supported binary floating literal, including integral-valued ones. On JS, all such float literals MUST use the existing carrier, including non-integral values. Integer literals MUST remain exact integers under their existing supported range/carrier rules. No test of a bare Number's value can reconstruct the missing distinction.\\\\n\\\\nChoose **(b), frontend-owned token-aware source ingestion**, generalized from the ANTLR frontends' producer rule. “Reconstruction” means interpreting the retained original token, not guessing from the output of an ordinary reader. It MUST occur before host maps/sets or downstream lowering can destroy occurrences. A value-keyed table, postwalk of already-read Numbers, numeric printing round-trip, or textual regex substitution over arbitrary source is insufficient.\\\\n\\\\nThe frontend may retain token spans/kinds in a transient parse structure and materialize carrier Data from them. It need not adopt ANTLR or replace the whole Clojure grammar. A scoped source-reading adapter can be the implementation of this frontend-owned stage, but it MUST consume the original lexical information; changing the meaning of the process-wide host reader is not the selected architecture. The engineer must demonstrate lexical coverage and occurrence preservation before selecting a minimal mechanism. The ruling does not pre-authorize a full parser rewrite.\\\\n\\\\nAll source compilation routes, including REPL compilation, MUST reach this stage or supply equivalent already-kind-preserving forms. Retain the original source text and source locations for provenance. Reader support and refusal behavior must be explicit for comments, strings, character literals, quote, metadata, dispatch forms, discarded forms, and any currently supported conditional syntax. No evaluation during reading and no ambient mutable reader registration. Token kind is derivable from source and need not become a new persisted UAST field or Jing tag.\\\\n\\\\nAlternatives:\\\\n\\\\n| Option | Ruling and reasons |\\\\n| --- | --- |\\\\n| (a) Change the upstream cljs reader/toolchain to return Float64 | Rejected as the primary repair. The readers are not ours; changing ordinary numeric results across upstream `cljs.tools.reader`, `cljs.reader`, and host compilation affects unrelated application code, host arithmetic, and macros. A change to one reader misses the others and precompiled quotes. A future upstream token-preservation API could help implement (b), but is not a dependency or authorization for global reader semantics changes. |\\\\n| (b) Frontend owns lexical preservation and emits the carrier | Selected. It acts where syntax still identifies the kind, is independently versionable, follows the existing Python/PHP/TS pattern, and leaves the codec and consumers' Data interpretation intact. It includes the source-ingestion boundary, not just `compile-literal`. |\\\\n| (c) Change canonical CBOR for integral JS Numbers | Rejected for this defect. A bare Number supplies no fact distinguishing intended integer from float. Encoding all such Numbers as float breaks legitimate integers; encoding all integral floats as integers breaks the established four-kind model and JVM/Dart meaning. A new explicit marker would merely duplicate the existing Float64 carrier. A deliberate future codec round is possible, but cannot recover absent source information and has an unnecessary consumer-wide migration radius here. |\\\\n| (d) Source discipline only | Rejected as closure of source portability. Requiring every user to rewrite `1.0` is an honest temporary restriction, not a working frontend for supported float syntax. Explicit constructors remain the permanent, correct contract for already-materialized host Data and generated/precompiled code whose original syntax is unavailable. |\\\\n\\\\n**Cross-frontend uniformity:** all four frontends MUST meet the same producer invariant, while retaining their language's numeric syntax and selected semantic profile. Python's tagged native float payload must still be float64 internally; its Clojure profile can emit bare float64 content. PHP float tokens remain float64; TS Number tokens remain float64 and BigInt tokens remain integers. The new Clojure path brings its float tokens into the same representation. It does not promise that `1` means float in all languages or that unlike language profiles have identical program roots.\\\\n\\\\n**Forms and prelude obligation:** the forms compiler MUST admit existing numeric carriers as literal constants, including nested/quoted positions, and preserve them. It MUST NOT reinterpret an unboxed Number 1 as a float. Library/generated forms without token provenance must use explicit constructors or a kind-preserving source/build path. Audit host-quoted prelude constants on a host that still distinguishes Double from integer; a JS-only scan after loss cannot establish absence. Fixing only the source reader is not sufficient acceptance.\\\\n\\\\n**Execution obligation:** the repaired portable frontend/profile MUST route its supported floating operations through carrier-aware numeric operations/adapters, with results reboxed before becoming guest Data. The authorized round must inspect the default REPL and plain-compile bindings as well as linked/library paths. Do not feed newly boxed floats to raw host `+` and call a coercion exception a completed portability repair. Prefer the existing explicit float/integer modules and scoped frontend/runtime bindings; do not change the global legacy primitive table or Float64 coercion policy incidentally. Any raw-host profile retained unchanged must retain its explicit limitation and immutable identity. Supporting the affected floating operations under the new portable profile is part of acceptance; a broad legacy arithmetic overhaul is not implied.\\\\n\\\\n## RULING 3 — Migration, legacy loading, and revisions\\\\n\\\\n**Normative:** newly compiled affected JS artifacts MUST be re-minted under a new Clojure frontend revision and a revised lowering/support profile. The current `:yang/clojure` manifest is revision `\\\\\\\"c1-2\\\\\\\"`, lowering profile `:yang.clojure/constructed-collections-v1`, in `src/cljc/yin/repl/frontends.cljc:44–61`. Do not publish repaired semantics under those unchanged identities. The replacement profile must retain the constructed-collection ordering contract as well as add numeric-kind preservation; exact new names are for the authorized implementation round.\\\\n\\\\nThe revision is a portable frontend identity, not a Node-only variant. Publish the same revised frontend contract on supported hosts. Source-reader/tokenizer implementation identity belongs in the existing compilation derivation; expose accurate source-text versus forms-only capabilities. JVM/Node source reading and Dart/Jolt forms execution must not be mislabeled as four identical parser capabilities.\\\\n\\\\n- **Expected one-time bytes shift:** JS compilation of affected floating source changes literal content from INTEGER to FLOAT64 and changes its dependent address closure. Unsafe-integral-number failures can become valid float compilations. Correct JVM float literal bytes and already-correct Python/PHP/TS float bytes should remain unchanged. Unaffected integer-only and already-correct scalar artifacts must stay byte-stable unless a separately explained dependency or provenance record changes.\\\\n- **JVM changes are not categorically zero:** frontend manifests/derivations change with revision. Corrected source collection preservation, or a repaired shared prelude/library, may also change JVM artifacts. Inventory these explicitly; do not justify arbitrary churn by calling it a JS fix. Original source text is unchanged; a canonical read-form input address may change when its previous kind was wrong.\\\\n- **Legacy loading:** preserve old bytes and addresses. Continue loading/executing old integer-bearing and float-bearing artifacts with their recorded meaning and compatible pinned runtimes. Do not rewrite integer rows on load, alias old addresses to corrected bytes, or claim an old JS integer artifact was always a float. Recompilation from authoritative source under the new frontend yields a new artifact and a derivation relating input, interpreter revision, and output. If source is absent, recovery of intended kind is not generally possible.\\\\n- **Other frontends/profiles:** no blanket Python/PHP/TS revision bump. Their token float constructors already meet this obligation. Their native profiles and Clojure-family profiles need new immutable revisions only if this round actually changes their emitted content, descriptor/support promise, or pinned runtime closure. In particular, a change to shared `clojure.core.impl`/`clojure.core`/`clj` artifacts requires new pins for every consuming profile whose closure changes; never mutate an existing `clojure-core-v1` publication in place. Python's `py-v1` and already-correct native JS/PHP publications are not automatically re-minted merely because the Clojure source reader changes.\\\\n- **Version boundaries:** no Jing encoding-profile, CBOR tag, universal AST grammar, VM numeric-kind/equality, C1 marker, or bytecode-format version bump is warranted by this repair. Prelude/runtime revisions change only when their artifacts/contracts change. Keeping an old frontend revision selectable requires its actual old implementation/closure; retaining a label over new behavior is not legacy support.\\\\n\\\\nThis migration preserves artifact portability while correcting source portability. The new compiler cannot make past wrong and new correct artifacts have the same content address.\\\\n\\\\n## RULING 4 — Honest portability statement pending the fix\\\\n\\\\n**Normative text for the public claim:**\\\\n\\\\n> yin.vm on JS preserves the numeric kind carried by portable Data and canonically encoded artifacts, subject to the selected runtime/profile's supported operations. Its Clojure source-to-Data path does not currently preserve every floating literal: ordinary JS readers and host-compiled forms can collapse an integral-valued binary float such as `0.0`, `1.0`, or `1e0` into integer content before compilation. Therefore equal Clojure source containing those unmarked values is not currently guaranteed equal runtime behavior or canonical output across hosts. A bare integral JS Number denotes integer content; intended floating Data must use Jing's float64 carrier. The Clojure forms compiler also needs carrier-literal admission repaired before direct carrier forms are supported uniformly.\\\\n\\\\nSafe **with respect to this defect**, not a blanket certification of every language feature:\\\\n\\\\n- Supported exact integer forms whose values are representable exactly on the producing host; use supported exact carriers for large integers, never a rounded JS Number.\\\\n- Supported float source values that remain non-integral after reading retain initial kind today. Native negative zero and non-finite Numbers have float classification in Jing. This does not guarantee every reader/build route preserves their original spelling/sign, and does not certify subsequent bare host arithmetic: `1.5 + 0.5` can become an unboxed integral Number unless the operation reboxes it.\\\\n- Explicit executable guest construction, e.g. `(data/float64 1)` where the `data` module/profile is available, supplies float kind at runtime. It is an expression, not a substitute for a float literal inside quoted Data; changing quoted `1.0` into quoted `(data/float64 1)` changes the value into a list.\\\\n- Canonical float64 Data decoded from existing Jing artifacts, or explicitly boxed host values passed to VM/UAST interfaces that accept them, retain kind. They must continue through carrier-aware operations. Do not generalize this to `yang.clojure/compile`'s currently incomplete carrier recognition.\\\\n- Python/PHP/TS token-based float lowering already performs boxing. Its existing profile-specific tests and explicit prelude/runtime producer obligations continue to govern those paths; this Clojure defect is not evidence of a new defect in each of them.\\\\n\\\\n**Placement required in the future round:**\\\\n\\\\n1. Keep the authoritative numeric/producer contract in `docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md` and add a cross-reference to the Clojure source boundary; do not redefine codec meaning to accommodate the frontend.\\\\n2. Put the Clojure source/forms limitation, selected repair, revision, migration, and capability matrix in `docs/design/yang.antlr.md` §8.8, alongside §8.5.5's existing float-producer precedent. There is no current `docs/design/yang.clojure.md`; do not cite a nonexistent document as the authority.\\\\n3. Reconcile portability assertions in `src/cljc/yang/docs/yang_implementation.md`, relevant VM/REPL public documentation, and the `read-forms`/compile API docstrings, including the forms-only boundary. `docs/design/yang.preludes.md` must retain the producer and profile obligations for shared preludes.\\\\n4. Correct `test/yang/clojure_collection_order_test.cljc`'s corpus comment/docstring: green non-integral replacement rows establish only that narrowed coverage. Identify this as a source-producer correctness dependency, link the archived failed row and this ruling, and remove the pending limitation only after the restored source and semantics gates pass. A corpus comment alone is not adequate public disclosure.\\\\n\\\\n## RULING 5 — Future fix-round contract; not dispatched\\\\n\\\\n**Scope:** preserve Clojure floating literal kind from source to Data/UAST, admit the existing carrier in the forms compiler, integrate actual source entry paths, close the affected producer gaps, version the frontend, and establish both semantic and content portability. No unrelated arithmetic redesign, float-key policy expansion, new persistent numeric annotation, global reader monkeypatch, codec change, silent legacy repair, or broad frontend/prelude refactor.\\\\n\\\\n**File boundaries for the future dispatch:**\\\\n\\\\n- `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc`: token-aware reading integration, carrier-aware `literal?`/`constant-form?` and preservation through quoting/lowering; a focused source/token module under `src/cljc/yang/clojure/` may be added. Do not bury lexical reconstruction in `compile-literal` after loss.\\\\n- `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc` and `src/cljc/yin/repl/frontends.cljc`: original-source routing, matching manifest revision/profiles, shell/source separation, and equivalent REPL/preview paths. Audit `src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc` and the three demo readers (`src/cljs/datomworld/demo/{compilation_pipeline,continuation_stream,equation_plotter}.cljs`); migrate actual compilation callers or explicitly delimit their legacy raw-forms behavior. Do not gratuitously change an adapter that already forwards source intact.\\\\n- Audit `src/cljc/yang/core/{uast,defquoted}.cljc`, `src/cljc/yang/clojure/{prelude,core_impl,core_module}.cljc`, and Python/PHP/TS prelude/lowerer producers for retained kind. Changes there require a demonstrated producer gap. Their existing correct boxing is a control, not a reason to rewrite them.\\\\n- Tests: add a focused portable Clojure float/source test namespace; extend `test/yang/clojure_collection_order_test.cljc`, `test/yang/clojure_callsite_test.cljc`, relevant `test/yin/repl` reader/catalog/source tests, and `test/yang/clojure/stream_eval_test.cljc` as appropriate. Use `test/yin/vm/data_test.cljc`, `test/yin/vm/float_test.cljc`, Jing numeric tests, and Python/TS `float_address_test.cljc` as regression oracles; add the equivalent PHP producer check if missing.\\\\n- Documentation changes are the locations in RULING 4. Production codec/VM equality changes and upstream toolchain patches are outside this contract; a newly discovered need for them requires a separate architectural decision.\\\\n\\\\n**Required acceptance gates:**\\\\n\\\\n1. **Real source, not only hand-boxed fixtures.** Compile identical original strings on JVM and Node through both the frontend source entry and the standard REPL/catalog route. Cover `0.0`, `1.0`, `-1.0`, `1e0`, `1.5`, `-0.0`, integral floats at/beyond `2^53`, and a small positive floating token that rounds to zero. Integer `0`/`1`, exact-carrier large integers, and integer-shaped tokens are controls. Preserve source numeric categories; decimal `M`, ratio, or other unsupported categories must not be silently relabeled as binary floats to make this gate pass. Retain the existing supported grammar/refusal policy for those categories.\\\\n2. **Structural preservation.** Exercise top-level, binding/argument, vector, quoted/nested data, dynamic collection, and metadata paths. Test repeated equal-valued tokens at distinct positions; comments, strings, characters and discarded forms containing numeric-looking text must not be rewritten. Exercise macro-generated numeric Data. Prove that host map/set construction cannot silently discard a source occurrence because kind was erased. Where float keys/elements are prohibited by a profile, require the same explicit refusal on both hosts; this gate must not expand key support accidentally.\\\\n3. **Restore the corpus honestly.** Restore integral floats to the common cross-host collection corpus using explicit carriers for intentionally programmatic Data fixtures, and add an equivalent unmodified-source-string case through the fixed frontend. Merely wrapping the fixture while leaving source reading broken is not closure. Include integer/float distinctions in root, complete occurrence-key vector, complete C1 relation, and semantic/stack/register instruction-provenance comparisons. Keep permutation, metadata, duplicate-occurrence, effect-order and collision controls. Compare against one recorded common reference, not independently regenerated expectations on each host.\\\\n4. **Runtime semantics oracle, independently of digests.** Through exported VM Data operations and actual compiled execution, require `data/equiv(float64(1), 1) = false`, nested `[float64(1)]` versus `[1] = false`, and float/float equality = true. In the selected Clojure library profile, `inc`/`dec` of floating input must take floating behavior and return float kind even when the value is integral; integer input must retain integer behavior. Check `float64?`/integer predicates and result bits, not printed numbers alone. Verify integer key admission versus floating refusal, including nested key Data and guest collection-policy behavior. Preserve signed-zero and NaN distinctions for both `data/equiv` and `content=`. Add a test mutation/control that dropping boxing fails the semantic oracle as well as the digest gate.\\\\n   Also execute supported floating `+`/`-`/`*` and comparisons through the actual repaired default/catalog path and linked path, including a non-integral-to-integral result. Record the primitive/profile bindings used. Direct `float/add` tests alone cannot certify a source path still calling raw host `+`; source `=` must be tested against its selected language/profile contract rather than assumed synonymous with `data/equiv`.\\\\n5. **API/build boundary.** Compile explicit Float64 forms directly on Node, nested and quoted, and execute/serialize them; integer Number controls must remain integers. Exercise actual prelude emission and pin its contents across hosts. A JVM-side audit must detect forbidden integral Double constants in host-quoted prelude producers before JS can erase them. Test a float-producing operation whose result is integral crossing a snapshot/continuation boundary to ensure the producer obligation is not restricted to source literals.\\\\n6. **Cross-host artifacts and legacy custody.** Produce representative repaired float artifacts on JVM and Node; decode, re-encode and execute them across supported VM hosts (JVM, Node, Dart, Jolt), comparing kind, value, refusal behavior and canonical identity. Test compilation from explicit carrier forms on forms-capable hosts; do not label Dart/Jolt execution of relayed source as native source parsing. Archive genuine pre-fix JS integer-bearing bytes and pre-fix JVM float-bearing bytes, then load/execute those bytes under the new system without recompilation. Verify both keep their historical meanings and new compilation produces a distinct corrected JS artifact. An old artifact synthesized by the new compiler is not legacy evidence.\\\\n7. **Profiles and revisions.** Assert the repaired Clojure manifest revision/profile, unchanged Jing/UAST/bytecode/C1 versions, correct source/forms capability claims, and immutable old pins. Check that already-correct Python/PHP/TS numeric producers and unselected native profiles retain expected artifacts. If a shared runtime closure changes, require the impacted profile's descriptor/runtime/derivation pins to reconcile and cold linked execution to pass. Selected profiles must not silently receive a new runtime under an old pin.\\\\n\\\\n**Re-pin only after these gates explain the change:** new Clojure frontend/lowering/support identities and parser implementation provenance; changed parsed-input/UAST/bytecode roots; complete corpus key/C1/provenance references; affected module/prelude manifests and transitive dependency/profile pins; changed program/snapshot fixtures and any active cc4 measurement snapshot actually containing changed artifacts. Preserve old references and the r3 failed evidence as historical records. Do not overwrite the `c1-1` legacy fixture or relabel historical cc4 measurements. A new compiler's measurements need their own source/profile pins; unchanged budgets are not automatically re-certified by a new address.\\\\n\\\\nRun focused failing gates first, then affected compiler/REPL/VM and producer regressions according to the repository's test ladder. The four-host evidence must state which work was source reading, forms compilation, artifact loading, and execution. A skipped or unavailable host is an open acceptance item, not a pass. No full suite or remote job is launched by this ruling.\\\\n\\\\n## Verification performed and limits\\\\n\\\\nThis is a read-only architectural investigation plus this findings file. I inspected current source, locally installed upstream reader source, the r3 raw failure log, and the r2 ruling. I ran bounded JVM expressions using the existing cached classpath, without rebuilding: `yang.clojure/read-forms` returns Long for `1`, Double for `1.0`, `0.0`, `1e0`, `-0.0`, `1.5`, and `9007199254740992.0`. For `1.0`, exported `data/equiv` against integer 1 is false, `admit-key?` is false, and `content=` against integer 1 is false; integer 1 yields true for those three checks. These establish the live JVM side of the defect.\\\\n\\\\nA second bounded JVM probe returned `{:integer-equiv-float false, :nested-equiv false, :integer-key true, :float-key false, :signed-zero-equiv true, :signed-zero-content false}` through the exported Data module. Both JVM probes exited successfully; no suite was run.\\\\n\\\\nI also loaded only the existing cached Node bootstrap dependencies in memory, stopping before the REPL main entry. Both `cljs.tools.reader` and `cljs.reader` return the same bare Number for `1`, `1.0`, and `1e0`; cached Jing classifies them as integer and gives them equal content. Negative zero remains floating; `1.5` remains floating. The unsafe integral float probe reaches the expected unsupported-value refusal. These cached JS artifacts are older than current source: an attempted exported `data/equiv` probe found that export absent in the cache. Consequently I do **not** claim a freshly built Node VM-semantic suite or current four-host execution result. The current JS semantic consequence is derived from the checked-in portable Data/dispatch code, not represented as a green Node test. The earlier probe's refusal terminated its final row; it was not a complete test run.\\\\n\\\\nNo production code, tests, dependency configuration, published pins, Git index/refs, or historical evidence were changed. Existing unrelated working-tree files were left alone. The owner/orchestrator must authorize a future fix round before implementation begins.\\\\ncat: collab/yang-clojure/1795706698000-reviewer-float-kind-r1.deepseek-v4-pro.findings.md: No such file or directory\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":1,\\\"status\\\":\\\"failed\\\"}}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:16:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_7\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"git cat-file -t 6a7fb586; git remote -v; git log -1 --format=fuller origin/master; rg --files collab/yang-clojure | rg '179570|floatkind|float-kind'; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md; cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"fatal: Not a valid object name 6a7fb586\\\\norigin\\\\tgit@github.com:sonwh98/datomworld.git (fetch)\\\\norigin\\\\tgit@github.com:sonwh98/datomworld.git (push)\\\\ncommit bab7640f0b162444285ab622469de1abbefbb668\\\\nAuthor:     Sonny To <lane-b@datom.world>\\\\nAuthorDate: Thu Nov 26 21:38:07 2026 +0700\\\\nCommit:     Sonny To <lane-b@datom.world>\\\\nCommitDate: Thu Nov 26 21:38:07 2026 +0700\\\\n\\\\n    docs(ledger): GREEN DECLARATION — master green at 9bd665fe; tri-leg confirmation matrix all rc=0 (jupiter JVM 7.9h, shiva node 12.1h, shiva dart 10.1h); every 20261123 red family closed with landed countersigned fixes\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.prompt.md\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795697994000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795693415000-architect-float-kind-portability-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795693415000-architect-float-kind-portability-ruling.prompt.md\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795693415000-architect-float-kind-portability-ruling.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log\\\\n---\\\\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# BUILD & TEST\\\\n\\\\n## Commands\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\\\\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\\\\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\\\\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\\\\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\\\\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\\\\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\\\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\\\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\\\\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\\\\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\\\\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\\\\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\\\\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\\\\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\\\\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\\\\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\\\\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\\\\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\\\\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \\\\\\\"Remote test servers\\\\\\\" below for\\\\n# which lanes may overlap.\\\\n#\\\\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\\\\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\\\\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\\\\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\\\\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\\\\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\\\\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\\\\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\\\\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\\\\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\\\\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\\\\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\\\\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\\\\n# When a test fails, reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the failing test\\\\n# (`clojure -M:test -n <ns> -v <ns>/<var>`, Node `--test=<ns>`, Dart `--only <ns>`) --\\\\n# never the whole suite (owner ruling 2026-11-01; see Gate Ladder G0).\\\\n# Individual tests run fine on the laptop; suite-scale runs (G1 subsystem sets,\\\\n# G2 changed sets, full suites, slow tier, nightly) run on shiva/jupiter --\\\\n# the suite has outgrown the laptop (owner ruling 2026-11-04).\\\\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\\\\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\\\\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` runs once daily on the remote hosts (04:00 VN);\\\\n# a MERGE needs only the affected tests (bb test:changed) -- owner ruling 2026-11-01;\\\\n# for a fix branch, the previously-failing tests green IS the merge bar\\\\n# (owner ruling 2026-11-07; see Gate Ladder G0).\\\\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\\\\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\\\\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\\\\n# overlap lanes, see \\\\\\\"Remote test servers\\\\\\\" below).\\\\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\\\\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\\\\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\\\\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\\\\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\\\\n\\\\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\\\\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\\\\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\\\\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\\\\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\\\\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\\\\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\\\\n\\\\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\\\\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\\\\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\\\\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\\\\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\\\\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\\\\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\\\\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\\\\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\\\\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\\\\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\\\\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\\\\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\\\\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\\\\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\\\\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\\\\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\\\\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\\\\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\\\\n\\\\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\\\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\\\\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\\\\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\\\\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\\\\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\\\\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\\\\n`--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\\\\n`affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\\\\nthe full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\\\\nsemantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\\\\n\\\\nShard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\\\\n`affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\\\\n`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\\\\nin an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\\\\ntherefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\\\\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\\\\nPrerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\\\\nserially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\\\\n(`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\\\\n\\\\nEach invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\\\\n`selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\\\\nand `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\\\\nnamespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\\\\nAny uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\\\\nThe final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\\\\nJVM wall time from total time including selection/builds. A nonzero child exit,\\\\nfailure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\\\\nDry plans write namespace evidence but execute no builds or tests. An empty\\\\nselection prints `0 JVM namespaces selected` and exits 1, including dry plans.\\\\nThe final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\\\\nrecord is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\\\\n`:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\\\\n`:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\\\\n\\\\n`test:routed <changed-files...>` applies this priority table to the whole change\\\\nset, prints the exact dispatch, executes it and propagates its result:\\\\n\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Change class (first match wins)                                                              | Dispatch                                                              |\\\\n+==============================================================================================+=======================================================================+\\\\n| Existing wide inputs (`deps.edn`, `bb.edn`, `src/dev/`, ANTLR, package locks, runner         | `bb test` — full quick tri-host; success required                     |\\\\n| configs, etc.), toolchain manifests, CI workflows, build scripts                             |                                                                       |\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Only `.md`, `.txt`, `.rst` documentation, excluding test resources                           | `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>` — documentation whitespace/format  |\\\\n|                                                                                              | lint only                                                             |\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Foundation code owned by a layer-0 entry in `subsystems.edn`, or `yang.clojure`, `yang.php`, | `bb test:quick-sharded --changed <paths>` — broad selected JVM        |\\\\n| `yang.typescript`, `yang.python` code                                                        | feedback                                                              |\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Other code/tests/fixtures with a nonempty affected selection                                 | `bb test:changed --changed <paths>` — existing quick tri-host         |\\\\n|                                                                                              | selection                                                             |\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` — fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n\\\\nMixed changes take the widest matching route. Missing paths, an empty git change\\\\nset, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\\\\nthe base tree. If the leaf command returns exit 2, routing immediately dispatches\\\\n`bb test` and requires its success. `--dry-run`/`--list` only reports the route;\\\\nits zero exit is **not** a verification result. Documentation checks inspect\\\\nstaged and unstaged changes together against HEAD. Paths absent from\\\\n`git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\\\\nunder `:unchecked`; they cannot complete the docs gate. These checks do not lint\\\\nexecutable examples or validate documentation links.\\\\n\\\\nRouting prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\\\\nmeans the dispatched gate completed successfully, not that every landing gate\\\\nis satisfied. It is false for foundation/JVM feedback, dry runs, unchecked docs,\\\\nand failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\\\\nquick tri-host routes report `:docs-whitespace`, `:selected-quick-tri-host`, and\\\\n`:full-quick-tri-host` respectively. Exit codes continue to propagate child\\\\nresults, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\\\\n\\\\n**Gate requirements are unchanged.** Sharded JVM success alone is not G2/G3\\\\ntri-host success: for foundation/compiler changes also run\\\\n`bb test:changed:cljs --changed <paths>` and\\\\n`bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\\\\nserialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\\\\ndemo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\\\\nlanes retain their current commands. The original unsharded tasks remain\\\\navailable. **Individual tests may run on the laptop; suite-scale runs may\\\\nnot (owner ruling 2026-11-04).** A single failing test or one namespace\\\\nreproduces quickly and is fine locally. Everything suite-scale — subsystem\\\\nlane sets (G1), changed-set runs (G2), the full quick suite, the slow tier,\\\\n`bb test:all`, and `nightly.clj` — runs on the remote hosts: the suite has\\\\noutgrown the laptop. The slow tier is 52 long namespaces whose JVM\\\\nprocesses each multi-thread (test workers, JIT, GC threads sized by core\\\\ncount); four of them GC-thrashing at once drove a laptop load average over\\\\n100 on 2026-11-01 while making no progress (the remote hosts run this tier:\\\\njupiter 5,645 s at 12 workers, shiva comparable). Do not run another lane\\\\nset concurrently on the laptop either.\\\\n\\\\n#### Worktree-local prerequisite caches\\\\n\\\\n`bb gen:antlr` now checks a content key over all grammar/manifests (including\\\\nrecorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\\\\n`deps.edn`, `mise.toml`, actual Java/javac versions and canonical worktree path.\\\\nIt also verifies the names and SHA-256 bytes of all generated targets, compiled\\\\nclasses and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\\\\nremoved or corrupted outputs trigger a full generation, retaining the generator's\\\\ndigest checks. Filtered/discovery invocations retain their CLI behavior and\\\\ninvalidate the complete-build stamp.\\\\n\\\\n`bb deps:npm` wraps `npm ci` with a key covering `package-lock.json`,\\\\n`package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\\\\nenvironment and worktree identity; it verifies the installed file tree before\\\\nreuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\\\\nensure this dependency too. Direct `npm ci` is still available.\\\\n\\\\nStamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\\\\nlock only after confirming its owning process died. Symlinked output parents\\\\nthat escape the worktree are refused. This is reuse **within one worktree**,\\\\nnot a shared artifact store: never share mutable `build/`, `lib/`, `target/` or\\\\n`node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\\\\nstill require the existing worktree isolation/serialization rules; the cache\\\\ndoes not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\\\\nbuilds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\\\\n\\\\nLaptop pilot (2026-10-29, 8 cores, 4 workers, `yang.clojure.cljc` changed):\\\\nthe unchanged selector and the sharded plan both covered **61 JVM namespaces**;\\\\nboth completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\\\\nSharded JVM wall time was **177.165 s** (total including prerequisites **257.207 s**;\\\\nouter command **258.27 s**). The original `test:changed:clj` command completed\\\\nin **376.79 s** overall and reported **298 s** for its JVM lane (that runner\\\\nrounds to whole seconds). The investigation's earlier unsharded command had\\\\ntimed out at 300 s without a summary. These are single-run observations, not\\\\na median/p95 claim or a replacement for tri-host landing verification.\\\\n\\\\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\\\\n\\\\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\\\\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\\\\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\\\\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\\\\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\\\\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\\\\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\\\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\\\\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\\\\n\\\\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\\\\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\\\\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\\\\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\\\\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\\\\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\\\\n\\\\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:antlr`)\\\\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\\\\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\\\\n\\\\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\\\\n\\\\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\\\\n   **Failure-reproduction rule (owner ruling 2026-11-01): when a test fails anywhere — a\\\\n   gate, the nightly, or a delegate report — reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the\\\\n   failing test, never the whole suite.** JVM: `clojure -M:test -n <ns>` (a single\\\\n   deftest: add `-v <ns>/<var-name>`). Node: `node target/node-tests.js --test=<ns>`\\\\n   (built by `bb build:yin-repl-node` first only if that test needs the Node REPL).\\\\n   Dart: `bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only <ns>[/<test-name>]` (owner ruling\\\\n   2026-11-07: the focus rule runs the failing TEST, not the namespace —\\\\n   `NS/test-name` entries become flutter `--plain-name` filters so only the\\\\n   named deftests execute; fall back to bare `<ns>` only when the failing\\\\n   var inside the namespace is not yet known, and narrow as soon as it is).\\\\n   Fix against the failing test alone, re-run it green, then climb back up\\\\n   (G1 for the home subsystem, G2 for dependents).\\\\n   Dart bug-chases may set `DATOM_FAIL_FAST=1` (owner ruling 2026-11-07): the\\\\n   scoped run stops at the first failure so the report arrives in minutes instead\\\\n   of after the full scoped set. Gate runs never set it — the merge bar needs the\\\\n   complete pass/fail picture. All dart runs use `--reporter expanded`, which\\\\n   streams failure names and positions inline so an interrupted run still retains\\\\n   them (the nightly's compact reporter lost 7 failure names to its 30000s wall,\\\\n   2026-11-06).\\\\n   Merge bar for a fix branch (owner ruling 2026-11-07, sharpening the 2026-11-01\\\\n   ruling): when the work is fixing already-failing tests, the previously-failing\\\\n   tests re-run green IS the merge bar — a fix branch merges on that alone; the\\\\n   G1/G2 climb applies to new feature slices, not to fixes. The full suite still\\\\n   gets its end-to-end confirmation from the daily nightly (04:00 VN), which is\\\\n   what catches collateral damage, not a pre-merge suite run.\\\\n   Never reproduce or chase a failure with the full suite or the slow tier — the full\\\\n   suite is not a debugging tool, and running it locally for one failing test is the\\\\n   exact misuse that drove a laptop load average over 100 (2026-11-01). A failure that\\\\n   only reproduces under the full suite's concurrency is diagnosed with the suite's own\\\\n   logs (its per-shard/per-test output), not by re-running the suite repeatedly.\\\\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\\\\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite). Gate claims name the tier (see the citation rule above): a fast-lane \\\\\\\"0 failures\\\\\\\" never asserts the slow tier ran.\\\\n4. **G3 Master (merged in place by the 2026-11-01 owner ruling)**: The landing gate\\\\n   for a change is the **tests that cover the feature being built** — `bb test:changed`\\\\n   (G2) already includes the reverse-require closure of the changed files, and a wide\\\\n   change still routes to the full quick lane. The complete suite is no longer a\\\\n   per-merge gate: it runs twice a day on the remote hosts (see \\\\\\\"Cadence\\\\\\\" below), so a\\\\n   break is caught within hours without charging every merge ~400 minutes of slow tier.\\\\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge\\\\n   (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\\\\n   (owner decision 2026-10-12), and implicitly twice daily by the scheduled run below.\\\\n   G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\\\\nslice L5) is two tests:\\\\n\\\\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\\\\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\\\\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\\\\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\\\\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\\\\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\\\\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\\\\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\\\\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\\\\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\\\\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\\\\n  REPL.\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\\\\n\\\\n# ClojureDart\\\\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\\\n\\\\n# Linting\\\\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\\\\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\\\\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\\\\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\\\\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\\\\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\\\\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\\\\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\\\\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\\\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\\\\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\\\\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\\\\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\\\\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\\\\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\\\\n\\\\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\\\\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\\\\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\\\\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\\\\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\\\\n\\\\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\\\\n\\\\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\\\\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\\\\n\\\\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\\\\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\\\\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\\\\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\\\\\\\"When to tag a test slow\\\\\\\"): a deftest that\\\\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\\\\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\\\\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\\\\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\\\\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\\\\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\\\\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\\\\n- **Cadence (owner ruling 2026-11-05):** a merge needs only the tests that cover the\\\\n  feature being built — the affected/dependent set from `bb test:changed` (or\\\\n  `bb test:sub <own> --slow` when the change touches slow-tagged code). The FULL suite,\\\\n  including the slow tier, runs **once a day at 04:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh** as a\\\\n  disjoint lane pair (`src/dev/nightly.clj`): **jupiter takes the jvm lane, shiva\\\\n  takes node and dart** (each lane in its own clone; shiva publishes the run pin,\\\\n  jupiter reads it and falls back to its own current master as an advisory run).\\\\n  The measured long set is ~400 minutes of serial slow-tier work, so it cannot be\\\\n  a per-merge cost; a break is instead caught by the next scheduled run.\\\\n  The schedule is an **hourly cron plus a Vietnam-time guard** in\\\\n  `~/ci/nightly-cron.sh` on each host (the guard fires only at the 04 VN hour and\\\\n  `flock` skips a slot whose previous run is still active), so it is immune to the\\\\n  DST shifts of either host's local timezone. **The cron entries are currently\\\\n  REMOVED (owner instruction 2026-11-06)**: the wrappers remain installed on both\\\\n  hosts and re-arming is one crontab line each\\\\n  (`0 * * * * ~/ci/nightly-cron.sh >> ~/ci/nightly-cron.log 2>&1`). Logs:\\\\n  `~/ci/nightly-cron.log`; results: `~/ci/nightly-results/<stamp>/summary.txt`.\\\\n\\\\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \\\\\\\"Remote test\\\\nservers\\\\\\\" for how to run them):\\\\n\\\\n| Run | Time |\\\\n|---|---|\\\\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\\\\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\\\\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\\\\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\\\\n\\\\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\\\\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\\\\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\\\\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\\\\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\\\\n\\\\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\\\\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\\\\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\\\\\\\"test\\\\\\\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\\\\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\\\\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\\\\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\\\\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\\\\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\\\\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\\\\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\\\\nset -u\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\"\\\\ncd \\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\nPAR=\\\\\\\"${2:-8}\\\\\\\"\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\neval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\"\\\\nOUT=\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci/shards\\\\\\\"; rm -rf \\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\"\\\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\\\necho \\\\\\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\\\\\" | tee \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\\\\\"$OUT/npm.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1                   && echo \\\\\\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\\\\\"$OUT/build-node.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1     && echo \\\\\\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\\\\\"$OUT/antlr.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1             && echo \\\\\\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nSUBS=\\\\\\\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\\\\\\\"\\\\n: > \\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nfor s in $SUBS; do\\\\n  bb test:sub:list \\\\\\\"$s\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null | awk \\\\\\\"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\");print}\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n  cat \\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\"$s $(wc -l < \\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\"$OUT/plan.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\ndone\\\\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\\\\nfind test -type f \\\\\\\\( -name \\\\\\\"*_test.clj\\\\\\\" -o -name \\\\\\\"*_test.cljc\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\) | sed -e \\\\\\\"s#^test/##\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\"s#\\\\\\\\.cljc\\\\\\\\?\\\\\\\\$##\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\"s#/#.#g\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\"s#_#-#g\\\\\\\" | sort -u > \\\\\\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nsort -u \\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\\\\\"\\\\ncomm -23 \\\\\\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\necho \\\\\\\"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nrun_shard() {\\\\n  s=$1; f=\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\"; [ -s \\\\\\\"$f\\\\\\\" ] || return 0\\\\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \\\\\\\"$n\\\\\\\"); done < \\\\\\\"$f\\\\\\\"\\\\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \\\\\\\"${args[@]}\\\\\\\" >\\\\\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \\\\\\\"^Ran \\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\\\\\" | tail -1)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n}\\\\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\\\\nprintf \\\\\\\"%s\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \\\\\\\"$PAR\\\\\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\\\\\"run_shard {}\\\\\\\"\\\\necho \\\\\\\"done $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\\\\nset -u\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\"\\\\ncd \\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\nPAR=\\\\\\\"${2:-6}\\\\\\\"\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\neval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\"\\\\nOUT=\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\\\\\\\"; rm -rf \\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\"\\\\nrun_ns() {\\\\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\\\\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \\\\\\\"$n\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \\\\\\\"^Ran \\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\\\\\" | tail -1)\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n}\\\\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\\\\necho \\\\\\\"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nxargs -P \\\\\\\"$PAR\\\\\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\\\\\"run_ns {}\\\\\\\" < \\\\\\\"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\necho \\\\\\\"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\\\\n\\\\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\\\\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\\\\nand `git`.\\\\n\\\\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (server snapshot checked 2026-10-11; repo selection comes from `mise.toml`) |\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version. Activate the repo toolchain before running any lane. |\\\\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | Server snapshot (rechecked 2026-10-29): java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.8 (the stale hand-symlinked 3.41.9 was replaced via `mise install flutter@3.47.8` after it failed to compile `dart:ui`-using cljd namespaces; if the Dart lane dies in the cljd compile, check `mise ls flutter` first). mise was moved off the apt package to the official user-local install (`~/.local/bin/mise`, 2026.11.2, owner action 2026-11-07) so both hosts share shiva's layout; toolchains in `~/.local/share/mise/installs/` were untouched. Tri-host quick tier verified green on 2026-10-29 (see Measured times). |\\\\n\\\\n### Peer dispatch: check the loads, route to the lower-loaded host\\\\n\\\\nshiva and jupiter are **peers**, not a primary and a spare (owner ruling\\\\n2026-11-15, superseding the earlier \\\\\\\"leave jupiter out, use shiva\\\\\\\" routing).\\\\njupiter has the larger core budget (48 vs 24). Before dispatching any\\\\none-host job (an rverify job, a lane, a gate), check both hosts' loads and\\\\nsend the work to the lower-loaded one — measured as load average against\\\\ncore count, not the raw number:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\nfor h in shiva.stigmergy.systems jupiter.stigmergy.systems; do\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\"== $h\\\\\\\"; ssh \\\\\\\"$h\\\\\\\" uptime\\\\ndone\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nA host whose 1-minute load sits well inside its core count is available;\\\\na host at or above its core count is saturated and must not receive new\\\\nwork. If the loads are comparable, prefer jupiter (more cores). Two\\\\nstanding exceptions keep their fixed hosts: the nightly lane pair (below)\\\\nkeeps jupiter=JVM / shiva=node+dart because the lanes are sharded and\\\\nco-ordinated, and a job already running on a host is not migrated mid-run\\\\nfor a marginal load difference — the check happens at dispatch time.\\\\nDocker is usable on both hosts (29.9.3 on each; verified 2026-11-15), so\\\\ncontainerized gates (e.g. the J6f Autobahn suite) are routable to either.\\\\n\\\\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\\\\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\\\\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\\\\n### Gate log hygiene: per-run directories, never shared per-lane logs (2026-11-14 incident)\\\\n\\\\n`bb test:sub` writes its per-lane detail logs to `target/profile/sub-clj.log`,\\\\n`sub-cljs.log`, `sub-cljd.log` inside the checkout — and **each invocation\\\\noverwrites them**. A gate script that runs several suites back-to-back while\\\\nonly appending the summary stream to a shared out file loses every lane's\\\\nper-fixture detail the moment the next suite starts. Measured 2026-11-14\\\\n(yang.php branch-tip gate, jupiter): the yang.php lane failed with\\\\n`yangphp-rc=1`; the dao.stream precondition that ran immediately after\\\\noverwrote `sub-clj.log`, and the failure detail was unrecoverable — forcing a\\\\nfull lane rerun (~2 h).\\\\n\\\\n**Rule: one run, one directory.** Each remote gate run gets its own\\\\ndirectory (per gate, per attempt), and every lane's log is copied there\\\\nbefore the next suite starts:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n# per-gate run dir; the summaries live beside the detail logs\\\\nRUN=~/ci/logs/<gate-name>-\\\\\\\\$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)\\\\nmkdir -p \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\$RUN\\\\\\\"\\\\nbb test:sub yang.php > \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\$RUN\\\\\\\"/yang-php.out 2>&1\\\\ncp target/profile/sub-*.log \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\$RUN\\\\\\\"/          # bank BEFORE the next suite\\\\necho \\\\\\\"yangphp-rc=\\\\\\\\$?\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\$RUN\\\\\\\"/summary.txt\\\\nbb test:sub dao.stream > \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\$RUN\\\\\\\"/dao-stream.out 2>&1\\\\ncp target/profile/sub-*.log \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\$RUN\\\\\\\"/\\\\necho \\\\\\\"daostream-rc=\\\\\\\\$?\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\$RUN\\\\\\\"/summary.txt\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nAlternatively use a fresh `git worktree` (or clone) per gate when the\\\\ncompile caches make reuse expensive — the point is that no later\\\\ninvocation can overwrite an earlier lane's evidence. The failure summary\\\\n(counts, rc) survives in the shared out file; the per-fixture detail\\\\nsurvives only in the run directory.\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n### Kill remote processes by exact PID, never by name pattern\\\\n\\\\nOn these hosts, kill a runaway nightly/lane process by its exact PID\\\\n(`pgrep -af <pattern>` only to LIST, then `kill <pid>`), never with\\\\n`pkill -f <string>` / `killall <name>`. Measured 2026-11-06: `pkill -f\\\\n\\\\\\\"nightly.clj\\\\\\\"` over SSH matched the invoking bash command line itself\\\\n(the pattern appears in `ps` output of the session carrying the kill),\\\\nterminating the SSH session mid-command — exit 255 with the kill\\\\nhalf-done. A name-pattern kill also races concurrent seats: another\\\\norchestrator's delegate process whose arguments embed the same string\\\\ndies with yours. If the PID from `pgrep -af` is ambiguous (several\\\\ncandidates), inspect with `ps -o pid,etime,args -p <pid>` first and kill\\\\nthe exact process; a session that dies under you is a symptom of this\\\\nmistake, not a host outage.\\\\n\\\\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\\\\n\\\\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\\\\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\\\\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\\\\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\\\\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\\\\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\\\\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\\\\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\\\\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \\\\\\\"$URL\\\\\\\";\\\\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\\\\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\\\\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\\\\n\\\\n### A clone is only current if it updated from real master\\\\n\\\\nA scratch clone's `origin/master` ref is only as fresh as the place it fetches\\\\nfrom — and a clone made from another scratch clone inherits that clone's *stale*\\\\nrefs. Measured on shiva 2026-11-02: the `dg` clone was created from the `p4`\\\\nscratch clone, whose local `master` ref had not moved since October 26 because\\\\nevery lane on it ran detached off fetched branches; the `dg` lane's\\\\n`checkout origin/master` therefore produced a commit 436 commits behind real\\\\nmaster, and the G3 Dart leg honestly reported failures (the datagram fix) for a\\\\ntree that did not contain them.\\\\n\\\\nBefore any lane runs in a clone — every time, not just the first:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\\\ngit remote get-url origin          # must be the GitHub URL, never another scratch clone\\\\ngit fetch -q origin \\\\\\\"+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*\\\\\\\"\\\\ngit checkout -q --detach origin/master   # or the lane's branch\\\\ngit merge-base --is-ancestor <the-commit-hash-you-pushed> HEAD && echo CURRENT\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nIf `origin` is not GitHub, repoint it (`git remote set-url origin \\\\\\\"$URL\\\\\\\"`). If\\\\nthe `merge-base` check fails, the clone's bookkeeping lies: refetch, or delete\\\\nand recreate the clone from GitHub directly. Never trust a hash the clone\\\\nreports without comparing it to the hash your push from the laptop printed.\\\\n\\\\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\\\\n\\\\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\\\\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\\\\ninto the clone:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\\\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\\\\nmise install                                    # install versions selected by this checkout's mise.toml\\\\neval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\"                       # puts the repo-selected tools on PATH\\\\nmise current flutter                            # verify the selected Flutter version\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-selected tools\\\\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \\\\\\\"Can't\\\\nfind flutter on PATH\\\\\\\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\\\\n\\\\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\\\\n\\\\n\\\\\\\"One lane set at a time\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\"one Dart lane at a time\\\\\\\" are per-machine rules about\\\\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\\\\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\\\\\\\"on one machine where\\\\navoidable\\\\\\\"). The laptop additionally never runs the slow tier or the nightly\\\\ndriver (see the sharded-lane section) — a full slow tier there reached a load\\\\naverage over 100 on 2026-11-01; the remote hosts own that tier. On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\\\\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\\\\n\\\\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\\\\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\\\\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\\\\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\\\\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\\\\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\\\\n  by the design's own reasoning (\\\\\\\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\\\\\\\"), not yet\\\\n  verified by a measured run.\\\\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\\\\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\\\\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\\\\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\\\\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\\\\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\\\\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\\\\n\\\\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\\\\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\\\\nseparate Dart clone.\\\\n\\\\n**The nightly driver now enforces this by construction.** `src/dev/nightly.clj`\\\\n(2026-11-03, kimi-reviewed) runs a multi-lane invocation as one concurrent\\\\nfuture per lane, each in its OWN clone — jvm keeps the primary clone, node\\\\nand dart get `<primary>-node` / `<primary>-dart`. The pinned SHA is resolved\\\\nonce and checked out in every lane clone; a cache-warm pass\\\\n(`clojure -P -M:test:cljs:clojuredart:cljd`) runs in the primary before\\\\nfan-out so two clones never fetch into the shared `~/.m2`/`~/.gitlibs`\\\\nconcurrently; per-lane summaries are appended serially after the futures\\\\njoin. A single-lane invocation (jupiter's `jvm`) is unchanged: one clone,\\\\none serial lane. Both hosts run the same driver — the lane list on the\\\\ncommand line (shiva: `node,dart` + `--pin-publish`; jupiter: `jvm` +\\\\n`--pin-read --pin-peer shiva...`) is the only difference.\\\\n\\\\n### Scratch-clone first-run gaps (two measured sightings)\\\\n\\\\nA fresh scratch clone needs two things the lane runner does not yet set up\\\\nitself; until the runner heuristic is fixed, do them by hand before the\\\\nfirst lane:\\\\n\\\\n1. **`npm install`** — the Node tests require npm deps (`@noble/hashes` was\\\\n   the measured miss on shiva 2026-11-02: the cljs lane ran but the\\\\n   node-tests bundle died with `MODULE_NOT_FOUND`).\\\\n2. **`bb gen:antlr`** — the `prerequisites` heuristic in\\\\n   `src/dev/affected.clj` decides `:gen:antlr` from the JVM lane's reach\\\\n   only (\\\\\\\"ANTLR classes are JVM only\\\\\\\" — stale: python keeps multi-target\\\\n   parsers), so a cljs/cljd-only selection that reaches\\\\n   `yang.python.antlr.parser` (e.g. `bb test:sub:cljs yang.base` via\\\\n   `yin.repl.frontends`) skips the generation and the shadow build dies on\\\\n   the missing `Python3Lexer.js`. Sighted independently on the p4 lane\\\\n   (PhpLexer ClassNotFoundException) and the yang.base cljs lane\\\\n   (2026-11-02). The lane-runner fix landed 2026-11-03 (eb27fd47).\\\\n3. **`.clojuredart/` compiler cache** — cljd's per-namespace compile cache\\\\n   under `.clojuredart/` survives branch checkouts and re-emits stale test\\\\n   mains, so a dart lane can fail at LOAD with \\\\\\\"Method not found\\\\\\\" for deftest\\\\n   methods whose sources changed or were gated away (measured 2026-11-13 on\\\\n   shiva: 107–133 load failures after checkout until\\\\n   `rm -rf .clojuredart test/cljd-out lib/cljd-out build/cljd-agg*`). Treat\\\\n   `.clojuredart/` like `test/cljd-out/` when proving a checkout took effect.\\\\n   Also: `#?(:clj (deftest …))` is not cljd-exclusive — ClojureDart's reader\\\\n   honors `:clj`, so JVM-only deftests must use the `#?(:cljd nil :default …)`\\\\n   gate shape (4d0d3e70 precedent); two further sightings of the same lesson:\\\\n   the cc3 provenance deftests (dart lane 107–133 load failures, fixed by the\\\\n   `dart-gate-shape-fix` conversion) and the PHP ticks directive parse\\\\n   (5ae34f5c's missing `:cljd` branch crashed the cljd compile of\\\\n   `yang.php.antlr.lower`; fixed 795f8d85).\\\\n\\\\n### Merge verification: run the suite from a fresh clone, not the authoring worktree\\\\n\\\\nA merge whose new code reads a file that was never `git add`ed passes in\\\\nthe authoring worktree (the untracked file is still on disk there) and\\\\nbreaks everywhere else. Measured: the H3-0 landing (1fd7236b) merged\\\\n`kernel-declarations` — which reads\\\\n`yang/latte_oracle/slice9_declarations.edn` via `io/resource` at load —\\\\nwithout the file (untracked in the authoring worktree); master's\\\\nyang.latte-oracle-test died at load until the file was restored\\\\n(2026-11-04). Before landing any merge that adds code reading a new\\\\nresource, or after any merge at all when in doubt:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\ngit worktree add /tmp/merge-verify <merge-commit>\\\\ncd /tmp/merge-verify && <the touched subsystem's focused test>\\\\ngit worktree remove /tmp/merge-verify\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nA fresh clone/worktree has only committed files — a missing untracked\\\\nresource fails there immediately.\\\\n\\\\n### Ledger commit hygiene: never commit a conflict marker\\\\n\\\\nThe append-only ledgers (`docs/agents/known-gaps.md`,\\\\n`orchestrator-logs/*.md`, `docs/agents/routing-status.md`) resolve rebase\\\\nand merge conflicts by the union rule, which means conflicts are common —\\\\nand a conflict marker that reaches a commit silently corrupts the record\\\\n(measured: the 2026-10-30 rebase landed a log with three committed\\\\n`<<<<<<<`/`>>>>>>>` markers and dropped records the union was supposed to\\\\nkeep; caught by the architect four rounds later). Before committing ANY\\\\nchange to a ledger file:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\ngit diff --cached | grep -E '^\\\\\\\\+(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' && echo CONFLICT-MARKERS-STAGED\\\\ngit grep -E '^(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' -- docs/agents/known-gaps.md orchestrator-logs docs/agents/routing-status.md\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nIf either hits, the commit stops until the union is redone. When resolving\\\\na ledger conflict, prefer regenerating the union from both sides' section\\\\nlists over hand-merging, and re-read the result against BOTH parents before\\\\nstaging.\\\\n\\\\n### The jolt J2 lane: run it with `--jobs` on the servers\\\\n\\\\nThe jolt lane's core mode (`bb src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj core [--jobs N]\\\\n[--only re] [--timeout-ms M]`) runs each test namespace as its own child jolt\\\\nprocess (own Chez VM, own native parser library load, own log under\\\\n`build/j2-logs/core/`), so the namespaces cannot corrupt one another — the\\\\nonly thing `--jobs N` overlaps is the waiting. The default is `jobs 1`\\\\n(strictly serial), which on jupiter makes the full ~179-namespace lane a\\\\n5-6-hour run.\\\\n\\\\nOn the servers, run it with `--jobs 16`:\\\\n\\\\n- jolt children are essentially single-threaded (the 16 MiB parse thread\\\\n  only burns during parses) and cost 1-2 GB each, so 16 concurrent children\\\\n  sit far inside jupiter's 48 cores / 62 GB and shiva's 24 / 62;\\\\n- the lane's wall time is dominated by a few long namespaces\\\\n  (`yin.repl.python-antlr-test` has an explicit 3-hour step,\\\\n  `yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test` needs one — see\\\\n  `step-timeout-ms` in `src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj`), so beyond ~16 workers\\\\n  the critical path is the longest child, not the count;\\\\n- **parallel is unproven until checked once**: the recorded lane evidence\\\\n  (J2 closure, the safepoint 60/265,225/0 pass) was all produced `jobs 1`.\\\\n  The first parallel run must be compared per-namespace against a serial\\\\n  baseline; any namespace that flips red gets one serial re-run to\\\\n  attribute before its evidence is trusted. Until that first comparison\\\\n  passes, keep `jobs 1` for anything whose verdict is timing-sensitive —\\\\n  timeout and wall-time measurements, native-memory/leak gates, and the\\\\n  merge-decision qualification runs.\\\\n\\\\nSuites that exceed the lane's default step budget are pinned in the lane's\\\\n`step-timeout-ms` map with their measured wall time; a namespace timing out\\\\nunder the default budget is a reason to measure it serially and add an\\\\nentry, not to re-run under the same bound.\\\\n\\\\n### The full run\\\\n\\\\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\\\\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\\\\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\\\\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\\\\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\\\\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (both hosts run all three lanes; a second clone for Dart is recommended)\\\\nset -u\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\"\\\\nDART=\\\\\\\"${2:-}\\\\\\\"\\\\ncd \\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\neval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\"\\\\nLOG=\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\"$LOG\\\\\\\"\\\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\\\necho \\\\\\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\\\\\\\" | tee \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\\\\\"$LOG/npm.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1               && echo \\\\\\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-node.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\\\\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\\\\\"$LOG/antlr.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1         && echo \\\\\\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-peer.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\\\\\"dart peer built $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \\\\\\\"$@\\\\\\\" >\\\\\\\"$LOG/$name.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\n         echo \\\\\\\"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"; }\\\\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\\\\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\\\\nif [ -n \\\\\\\"$DART\\\\\\\" ]; then                            # its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\\\\n  ( cd \\\\\\\"$DART\\\\\\\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\\\\\\\" 2>&1\\\\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\\\\nfi\\\\nwait\\\\necho \\\\\\\"done $(ts)\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\"\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \\\\\\\"bash -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"'\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\\\\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\\\\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\\\\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\\\\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\\\\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\\\\n(measured 2026-10-11).\\\\n\\\\n### Known differences from the laptop\\\\n\\\\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` on Linux —\\\\n  MEASURED AND FIXED (2026-11-02).** The old entry inferred that \\\\\\\"a second UDP bind on\\\\n  a taken port behaves differently on Linux\\\\\\\"; confirmed, with the cause. Dart's\\\\n  `RawDatagramSocket.bind` defaults to `reuseAddress: true`, and **Linux honours\\\\n  `SO_REUSEADDR` for UDP**, so a second bind to a port a reuse-enabled socket already\\\\n  holds *succeeds* (`strace`: `SO_REUSEADDR=1` on both sockets, no `SO_REUSEPORT`, both\\\\n  kernel `bind()` calls succeed); macOS refuses with errno 48. The seam was correct\\\\n  throughout — given a genuinely exclusive holder (`reuseAddress: false`) it deposits\\\\n  `bind-failed` in ~10 ms with errno 98. The test asserted one host's binding policy\\\\n  rather than the contract, so it now binds its own exclusive blocker\\\\n  (`RawDatagramSocket.bind \\\\\\\"127.0.0.1\\\\\\\" 0 .reuseAddress false .reusePort false`) and\\\\n  points the seam at that port: the failure path is exercised on both hosts. Verified\\\\n  on shiva: the namespace passes 5/5.\\\\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\\\\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\\\\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\\\\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\\\\n\\\\n### The nightly full suite and the branch-testing rule (2026-10-30 owner ruling)\\\\n\\\\nOwner, verbatim: \\\\\\\"since the complete test can take a good 7-8hrs to run\\\\ncompletely, it should be schedule to run once a day at 3am. while\\\\nbranch/worktree test should only run test that are targeting code\\\\nchanges\\\\\\\" — and, clarifying the nightly's target: \\\\\\\"it should not be for\\\\nonly a worktree/branch. the nightly complete test should be for master.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nTwo tiers, by target and purpose:\\\\n\\\\n- **The nightly full suite runs MASTER ONLY** (`~/ci/nightly-cron.sh` on\\\\n  shiva and jupiter, hourly cron plus a 04:00 Vietnam guard and flock,\\\\n  documented under \\\\\\\"Cadence\\\\\\\" above; the driver fetches and checks out\\\\n  origin/master before anything runs). Every lane, across the host pair:\\\\n  the JVM full suite on jupiter, the full Node and Dart suites on shiva.\\\\n  Its results are a\\\\n  post-hoc integrity net for master, NOT a landing gate. Failures it\\\\n  finds route to fix rounds the next morning.\\\\n- **Branch/worktree testing runs only the namespaces that target the\\\\n  changed code** (the diff's own tests plus the surfaces the diff\\\\n  touches), one JVM per namespace, plus one cheap portable lane (Node)\\\\n  when cljc goldens or fixtures moved. Never the full tier on a branch:\\\\n  a branch gate needs the changed code proven, not the whole tree\\\\n  re-proven. A branch is never promoted by its own testing — the merge\\\\n  to master is what puts it under the nightly.\\\\n\\\\nAn orchestrator landing a slice cites the subset runs plus the gate\\\\nsign-off; the nightly catches what the subset could not see, and a red\\\\nnightly owns the follow-up. A gate claim names its tier: fast\\\\n`bb test:sub:clj <sub>` excludes `^:slow` tests, so \\\\\\\"0 failures\\\\\\\" from it\\\\nnever asserts the slow tier ran — when the diff touches slow-tagged tests\\\\n(G2's `--slow` clause), the claim cites the slow-tier or per-test linked\\\\nruns separately (2026-11-17 lesson: three include/const gate claims read\\\\n\\\\\\\"green\\\\\\\" while the slow linked rows were only proven by separate fresh-JVM\\\\nruns).\\\\n\\\\n**The merge precondition (2026-10-30 owner ruling, restored 2026-10-31 —\\\\na dedup pass dropped this section along with a stale duplicate of the\\\\ntwo-tier policy; both stand):** before any merge into master, the\\\\n`dao.stream` tests must run and pass — dao.stream is what everything is\\\\nbuilt on. Concretely: `bb test:sub dao.stream` (the subsystem's tests,\\\\n`--slow` when the slice touches slow-guarded surfaces) is part of every\\\\nbranch gate regardless of which subsystems the diff's own changes\\\\nselect, because a change can break the foundation without touching it.\\\\nA red dao.stream blocks the merge until fixed or diagnosed as\\\\npre-existing by the owning seat.\\\\n\\\\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\\\\n\\\\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\\\\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\\\\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\\\\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\\\\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\\\\nextra cores, and measure before relying on a speedup. (Historical record — the\\\\n\\\\\\\"free laptop\\\\\\\" comparison predates the 2026-11-04 ruling that suite-scale runs\\\\nbelong on the remote hosts.)\\\\n\\\\n### Jupiter verification run (2026-10-29, commit 7c0a078f, scratch clone, sequential lanes)\\\\n\\\\nFirst full tri-host verification of jupiter. Prerequisites (npm ci, Node REPL,\\\\nANTLR via `gen:antlr`, Dart peer) all succeeded; the stale 3.41.9 Flutter was\\\\nreplaced with 3.47.8 before the Dart lane. Results: **JVM quick tier\\\\n(`test:quick-sharded`, 12 workers) rc=0 in 702 s** (cold, every shard green);\\\\n**Node quick tier rc=0 in 4583 s** (all tests pass, but roughly 15x the\\\\nlaptop — route Node lanes to shiva; the laptop comparison is historical and\\\\nsuite-scale Node runs no longer belong there per the 2026-11-04 ruling);\\\\n**Dart quick tier\\\\nrc=1 in 1495 s with the ONLY failure the documented known Linux-only red**\\\\n(`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events`, same\\\\ntaken-port/bind-failed signature recorded above). Routing summary: jupiter\\\\nis the preferred JVM/sharding box (48 cores); Node stays off it when wall\\\\ntime matters; Dart works on either host. Timings are single cold runs, not\\\\nmedians, and two foreign QEMU guests idle on the host.\\\\n\\\\nOne exception to the concurrency above: never run two lanes that both trigger\\\\n`gen:antlr` (the quick `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` tasks) in the SAME worktree at the\\\\nsame time — they race on `build/antlr` and `lib/antlr` and fail with\\\\n\\\\\\\"Patch does not apply\\\\\\\" or \\\\\\\"Generated sources differ from the recorded digests\\\\\\\".\\\\nRun them sequentially, or in separate clones (as the slow-tier harness does).\\\\nThe fix after a collision is `rm -rf build/antlr lib/antlr` and a rerun; the\\\\ngenerator is not idempotent over an already-patched tree. Corroborated twice in\\\\none week (the M3 round and the C3 completion round, 2026-10-18).\\\\n\\\\n### Remote agent execution (delegate CLIs on shiva and jupiter)\\\\n\\\\nOwner relay, 2026-10-24: the delegate agent CLIs — `claude`, `codex`, `glm` —\\\\nare installed and usable **on the remote hosts themselves**, not only from the\\\\norchestrator's laptop. This generalizes this section from \\\\\\\"run tests remotely\\\\nover SSH\\\\\\\" to \\\\\\\"delegate whole work agents remotely\\\\\\\": a heavy implementation or\\\\nverification leg (test lanes, native builds, fault-injection runs, multi-hour\\\\nsuites) can be executed by a delegate agent running on the server, keeping the\\\\norchestrator's laptop unloaded.\\\\n\\\\nThe pattern (composed from the rules above plus the delegate-invocation\\\\nreference):\\\\n\\\\n1. **Land the branch on origin first** (push from the laptop; never rsync) and\\\\n   prepare the scratch clone per \\\\\\\"Get the code there\\\\\\\".\\\\n2. **Start a named tmux session** for the task; run the agent inside it with\\\\n   the toolchain activated *inside* the tmux command (`eval \\\\\\\"$(mise env -s\\\\n   bash)\\\\\\\"` after the `cd` — the non-interactive SSH shell does not have it).\\\\n   A fresh clone hits mise's interactive trust prompt, which blocks the whole\\\\n   session until answered via `tmux send-keys`; pre-empt it with `mise trust`\\\\n   inside the same command.\\\\n3. **The remote agent works in the clone**: implement, build, run lanes,\\\\n   write its findings artifact — the same brief form as any delegate dispatch\\\\n   (role template, bounded file ownership, deliverable path), just executing\\\\n   on the host. Long jobs survive SSH drops because of tmux.\\\\n4. **The orchestrator polls detached files, never the pane** (pane captures\\\\n   carry control characters that make output look empty): rc files, summary\\\\n   logs and `ls *.flag` sentinels written by the run.\\\\n5. **Division of labor**: bookkeeping and polling are orchestrator SSH\\\\n   one-liners; execution belongs to the remote agent. Do not run heavy lanes\\\\n   from the orchestrator's laptop while a remote agent owns the same gate.\\\\n6. **Same trust rules as any delegate**: remote execution does not waive\\\\n   Workflow steps 6–7 of the orchestrator role — artifacts are verified, and\\\\n   review before commit applies to remote-produced work identically.\\\\n   Session-ID capture and resume rules from the delegate-invocation reference\\\\n   apply per host (each host's `~/.claude`, `~/.codex` store is its own).\\\\n\\\\nKnown state: `claude` and `codex` verified working on shiva (2026-10-24, the\\\\njolt canonical-fixes verification ran there); `glm` reported available by the\\\\nowner on both hosts, not yet exercised. Both hosts are toolchain-verified for\\\\nthe full tri-host suite (jupiter verified 2026-10-29 — see Measured times;\\\\nits 3.41.9 Flutter was replaced with 3.47.8, and model availability per\\\\nhost is governed by `routing-status.md`, not this file).\\\\n\\\\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\\\\n\\\\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\\\\n\\\\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\\\\n\\\\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\\\\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\\\\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\\\\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\\\\n\\\\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\\\\n\\\\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\\\\n\\\\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\\\\n\\\\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\\\\n   ```sh\\\\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\\\\n   ```\\\\n\\\\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\\\\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\\\\n\\\\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\\\\n   Common patterns:\\\\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\\\\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\\\\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\\\\n\\\\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\\\\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\\\\n\\\\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\\\\n\\\\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\\\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\\\\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\\\\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\\\\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\\\\n\\\\n### A. Logic & Correctness\\\\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\\\\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\\\\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\\\\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\\\\n\\\\n### B. Security & Credentials\\\\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\\\\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\\\\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\\\\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\\\\n\\\\n### C. Architecture & API Design\\\\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\\\\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\\\\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\\\\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\\\\n\\\\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\\\\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\\\\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\\\\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\\\\n\\\\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\\\\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\\\\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\\\\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\\\\n\\\\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\\\\n2. **Explain the \\\\\\\"Why\\\\\\\"**: Don't just say \\\\\\\"change this\\\\\\\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \\\\\\\"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\\\\\\\").\\\\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\\\\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:23:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_11\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/yang-clojure/AGENTS.md && cat collab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md && python3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target origin/master...HEAD --repo-dir /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-floatkind'\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"# ==============================================================================\\\\n# datom.world – Development Rules & Architectural Foundations\\\\n# ==============================================================================\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.world.md`](./docs/design/datom.world.md) for master architecture, core philosophy, foundational axioms, non-negotiable invariants, code style, development workflow, and operational guides (testing, team roles, agent delegation, website formats).\\\\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-26 12:18:51 GMT\\\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-26 19:18:51 +0700\\\\nCoding-Agent: glm\\\\nSession-ID: 519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a\\\\n\\\\n# Task: fix/float-kind-portability r1 — TDD: lexical preservation of Clojure float literals, per architect ruling 1795693415000\\\\n\\\\nRole: Compiler Engineer (numeric semantics)\\\\n\\\\nImplementers:\\\\n- Status-Event: 2026-11-26 12:58 GMT | Model: glm-5.3 (session 519cc353, darkstar) | Status: superseded | Rationale: owner-directed migration to a remote host (laptop load); in-flight state committed 09401506 before stop\\\\n\\\\n## TDD IS THE REQUIRED METHOD (owner instruction, binding)\\\\n\\\\nWrite the FAILING tests first — RULING 5's seven acceptance gates in test form — run them RED, record the red evidence, THEN implement. Every acceptance gate lands as a test before the code that makes it pass. The red run is itself deliverable evidence (attach logs). Do not implement first and backfill tests.\\\\n\\\\n## Authority (read first)\\\\n\\\\n1. **The ruling (binding, normative)**: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795693415000-architect-float-kind-portability-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md — RULING 1 (impact), RULING 2 (option b: frontend-owned lexical preservation; carrier admission; execution reboxing), RULING 3 (revision bump, migration, legacy custody), RULING 4 (honest portability statement + doc placements), RULING 5 (the complete fix-round contract: file boundaries + seven acceptance gates + re-pin protocol). Read ALL of it.\\\\n2. Your prior probes (from the ruling's Verification section): yang.clojure/read-forms returns Double for 1.0/0.0/1e0 on JVM (kind intact there); cljs.tools.reader AND cljs.reader return the same bare Number for 1/1.0/1e0 (kind lost). Start from that.\\\\n3. The precedent to generalize: yang.typescript lower.cljc:429-440 (token → (cbor/float64 v)); python/php equivalents.\\\\n\\\\n## Where you work\\\\n\\\\nWorktree: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-floatkind, branch fix/float-kind-portability @ origin/master (7f4898f4). Work ONLY there. Commit NOTHING — the orchestrator commits after review. Toolchain: node via /Users/sto/.nvm/versions/node/v26.1.0/bin on PATH; mise shims for bb/clojure; `bb gen:antlr` first (cache hit expected).\\\\n\\\\n## The work, in TDD order\\\\n\\\\n1. **RED — write the seven acceptance gates as portable tests** (new namespace per RULING 5's test boundaries, e.g. test/yang/clojure_float_kind_test.cljc + the corpus/semantic-oracle extensions it specifies). Gate 1 first (real identical source strings on JVM and Node through the frontend source entry + REPL/catalog route: 0.0, 1.0, -1.0, 1e0, 1.5, -0.0, integral floats at/beyond 2^53, small positive float rounding to zero; integer controls). Run RED on JVM + Node, save logs to collab/yang-clojure/<new-ts>-floatkind-evidence/.\\\\n2. **The shared factory FIRST**: a shared numeric-token classifier + carrier constructor under the yang.core shared layer (one implementation; per-language lexeme dialect tables) — per the owner's direction this is SHARED code, not a fourth private copy; ts/php/python adapters move to it (their existing correct boxing is a control — do not rewrite beyond the adapter call).\\\\n3. **The Clojure ingestion stage** (the hard piece — the ruling REQUIRES a lexical-coverage demonstration BEFORE selecting the minimal mechanism): demonstrate coverage of comments/strings/chars/quote/metadata/dispatch/discarded forms/reader conditionals + numeric tokens with the retained-kind mechanism, THEN implement. No regex-over-source, no postwalk of read Numbers, no print-round-trip, no process-wide reader monkeypatch. Scope per RULING 5's file boundaries (src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc + a focused module under src/cljc/yang/clojure/; repl.cljc/frontends.cljc routing; the audit set).\\\\n4. **Carrier admission + execution reboxing** per RULING 2 (forms compiler admits float64 carriers incl. nested/quoted; the portable profile routes floating ops carrier-aware with results reboxed; do not overhaul legacy arithmetic).\\\\n5. **GREEN**: all seven gates green on JVM + Node (laptop single-namespace runs are G0-legal); then Dart + Jolt legs per the gate 6 cross-host matrix (targeted selectors; jupiter scratch clone for Jolt if needed, per-run log dirs). Record exact commands/counts per host.\\\\n6. **Migration per RULING 3**: new Clojure frontend revision + lowering/support profile (retaining the constructed-collection ordering contract in the identity), legacy custody tests (archive pre-fix JS integer-bearing + JVM float-bearing bytes, load/execute under the new system without recompilation), doc placements per RULING 4.\\\\n7. **Re-pin** per RULING 5's protocol — only after the gates explain the change: complete corpus key/C1/provenance references, pre-fix archives preserved, historical records untouched.\\\\n\\\\n## Bounds\\\\n\\\\nRULING 5's file boundaries exactly. Outside them (codec, VM equality, upstream reader): report as dependencies requiring separate architectural decision — do NOT fix incidentally. No unrelated arithmetic redesign, no float-key policy expansion, no new persistent annotation, no global reader monkeypatch. kondo 0 errors on touched files.\\\\n\\\\n## Deliverable\\\\n\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/<new-ts>-engineer-float-kind-portability-r1.glm-5.3.findings.md (worktree mirror + say-so): the RED evidence, the lexical-coverage demonstration, per-gate green evidence with commands/counts, the shared-factory design, the re-pin ledger. Do not end the turn with runnable work remaining. Commit NOTHING.\\\\n\\\\nBegin the final response exactly with:\\\\nCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\\\\nCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\\\\n# Code Review Summary for `origin/master...HEAD`\\\\n\\\\n## Executive Overview\\\\n- **Total Files Changed:** 0\\\\n- **Lines Added:** +0 | **Lines Deleted:** -0\\\\n- **Test-to-Source Additions Ratio:** 0.0 (0 test lines vs 0 source lines)\\\\n- **Overall Change Risk:** `LOW`\\\\n\\\\n## Automated Scan Findings\\\\n✅ No critical security or code quality smells detected by automated scan.\\\\n\\\\n## File Churn & Risk Breakdown\\\\n| File Path | Category | +Add | -Del | Risk |\\\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n\\\\n## Reviewer Focus & Recommendations\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795709372000-architect-floatkind-signoff.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:31:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_15\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=10 jupiter.stigmergy.systems 'cd ~/ci/datomworld-floatkind && git rev-parse HEAD && git status --short && cat collab/yang-clojure/1795706286000-engineer-float-kind-portability-r1.gemini-4-neon.findings.md && cat collab/yang-clojure/1795706698000-reviewer-float-kind-r1.deepseek-v4-pro.findings.md && git diff --stat origin/master...HEAD'\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"6a7fb586ac1f1b35487c88b0fc6108a6aa440664\\\\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-26 15:18:06 GMT\\\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-26 10:18:06 EST\\\\nRole: Compiler Engineer (numeric semantics)\\\\nBranch: fix/float-kind-portability (base `7f4898f4`, resumed from WIP `09401506`, uncommitted working tree per orchestrator rule)\\\\nAuthority:\\\\n- `collab/yang-clojure/1795693415000-architect-float-kind-portability-ruling.gpt-6-astra.findings.md` (normative, RULING 1–5)\\\\n- `collab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability.prompt.md` (round brief)\\\\n\\\\nImplementers:\\\\n- Status-Event: 2026-11-26 12:58 GMT | Model: glm-5.3 (session 519cc353, darkstar) | Status: superseded | Rationale: owner-directed migration to remote host; drafted Gates 1/2/4/5 test file + JVM pre-fix legacy fixture in `09401506`\\\\n- Status-Event: 2026-11-26 15:18 GMT | Model: gemini-4-neon (linux worktree) | Status: completed | Rationale: captured Node pre-fix legacy fixture, locked full 7-gate RED evidence on JVM+Node, implemented shared numeric-token factory + Clojure token-aware reader + carrier admission + scoped numeric primitives + migration/docs, verified GREEN across all four hosts (JVM, Node, Dart, Jolt) + regression oracles + demo build + `clj-kondo` 0 errors\\\\n\\\\n# Findings: `fix/float-kind-portability` r1 — Lexical Preservation of Clojure Float Literals\\\\n\\\\n## 1. Executive Summary\\\\n\\\\nClojure numeric literal kind preservation from original source text to `dao.jing.cbor` / UAST / VM execution is implemented and verified green across all seven acceptance gates of RULING 5 and all four hosts (JVM, Node, Dart, Jolt), strictly inside RULING 5's file boundaries:\\\\n\\\\n1. **Shared numeric-token factory** (`src/cljc/yang/core/numeric_token.cljc`): single portable token classifier and carrier constructor with per-language dialect tables (`:clojure`, `:typescript`, `:python`, `:php`). TypeScript, Python, and PHP ANTLR lowerers were migrated onto it without changing any existing Python/TS/PHP golden addresses, and a new PHP float producer parity test (`test/yang/php/antlr/float_address_test.cljc`) was added.\\\\n2. **Frontend-owned, token-aware Clojure source reader** (`src/cljc/yang/clojure/reader.cljc`): consumes original Clojure source text directly and materializes numeric tokens through `yang.core.numeric-token/materialize :clojure` **at token time**, before collection construction (`{...}`, `#{...}`) or lowering can lose kind or coalesce `1` and `1.0`. Zero ambient mutable state, no regex-over-source, no postwalk over read `Number`s, no print-round-trip, and no global reader monkeypatch. Supports optional `:file`/`:source-positions?` indexing for `yang.io`.\\\\n3. **Carrier admission & scoped execution reboxing**:\\\\n   - `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc` admits `cbor/float64?`, `cbor/decimal?`, `cbor/ratio?`, and `host-int/big-carrier?` in `literal?` and `constant-form?` (direct, nested, and quoted).\\\\n   - `src/cljc/yang/clojure/numeric.cljc` exports carrier-aware Clojure numeric primitives (`+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, kind-strict `=` via `data/equiv`, cross-kind `==`, `!=`) that route float operations through `yin.vm.float` and rebox float results as `data/float64`. Scoped to `yang.clojure/primitives` via `yin.repl.frontends` — global `yin.vm/primitives`, `dao.jing.cbor`, `yin.vm.data`, and `Float64` `:carrier-coercion` policy are untouched.\\\\n4. **Migration, custody, and documentation** (RULING 3 & RULING 4):\\\\n   - `:yang/clojure` manifest bumped to revision `\\\\\\\"c1-3\\\\\\\"`, lowering profile `:yang.clojure/constructed-collections-float-kind-v1`, support profile `:yang.clojure/c1-float-kind`, grammar capability `:source-text` (JVM/Node) vs `:forms-only` (Dart/Jolt, with honest `:unavailable-on-cljd` / `:unavailable-on-jolt` refusals on direct `read-forms`).\\\\n   - Both genuine pre-fix `c1-2` legacy artifacts (JVM float-bearing + Node integer-bearing) archived under `test/resources/yang/clojure/` and verified loaded, decoded, re-encoded, and executed without recompilation on all four hosts.\\\\n   - Documentation updated in `docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md`, `docs/design/yang.antlr.md` (§8.8.1), `docs/design/yang.preludes.md` (§8.1), `src/cljc/yang/docs/yang_implementation.md`, and `test/yang/clojure_collection_order_test.cljc`.\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## 2. Pre-Fix Legacy Custody Artifacts (Captured Before Production Edits)\\\\n\\\\nCaptured from pre-fix `c1-2` (`7f4898f4` / `09401506`) before modifying any production code; one-shot capture test `test/yang/floatkind_legacy_capture_test.cljc` was subsequently removed.\\\\n\\\\n| Fixture | Host | Size | SHA-256 | Manifest Revision | Canonical Root | Runtime Value |\\\\n|---|---|---:|---|---|---|---|\\\\n| `test/resources/yang/clojure/float-kind-legacy-jvm-float.cbor` | JVM (`:jvm-float`) | 1,415 B | `71dc93298c26773dc8383f5d12e03d732840052692a272c16c4aaf4ec90783ec` | `\\\\\\\"c1-2\\\\\\\"` | `:segment/blake3-7cdae9dfee7f1980ccdd9543f279d0d7d20e4a4a4a44dda14f88f54ba9002049` | `float64(1.0)` |\\\\n| `test/resources/yang/clojure/float-kind-legacy-node-integer.cbor` | Node (`:node-integer`) | 1,390 B | `ffb9db68eb953ccadd681b916a8df6d262206baa055d6f91eda461d179174ef2` | `\\\\\\\"c1-2\\\\\\\"` | `:segment/blake3-541ba2ffc6b74327f70008d2069a35279612c2e96c82cc198d7b0b6782501e4c` | exact integer `1` |\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## 3. TDD RED Evidence (Locked Before Implementation)\\\\n\\\\nAll RED logs are preserved unmodified under `collab/yang-clojure/1795702210000-floatkind-evidence/`:\\\\n\\\\n| Stage | Host | Command | Result | Log File |\\\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\\\n| Initial Gates 1/2/4/5 RED | JVM | `clojure -M:test -n yang.clojure-float-kind-test` | 18 tests, 180 assertions, **20 failures, 1 error** (`RC=1`) | `collab/yang-clojure/1795702210000-floatkind-evidence/red-jvm-gates1245.log` |\\\\n| Initial Gates 1/2/4/5 RED | Node | `npx shadow-cljs compile test && node target/node-tests.js --test=yang.clojure-float-kind-test` | 17 tests, 163 assertions, **63 failures, 4 errors** (`RC=1`) | `collab/yang-clojure/1795702210000-floatkind-evidence/red-node-gates1245.log` |\\\\n| Complete 7-Gate RED | JVM | `clojure -M:test -n yang.clojure-float-kind-test` + `yang.clojure-collection-order-test` (`corpus-cross-host-reference-test`, `unmodified-source-integral-floats-test`) | 21 tests / 228 assertions (**19 failures, 1 error**) + 2 tests / 22 assertions (**2 failures, 0 errors**) (`RC=1`) | `collab/yang-clojure/1795702210000-floatkind-evidence/red-jvm-all7gates.log` |\\\\n| Complete 7-Gate RED | Node | `node target/node-tests.js --test=yang.clojure-float-kind-test,yang.clojure-collection-order-test/corpus-cross-host-reference-test,yang.clojure-collection-order-test/unmodified-source-integral-floats-test` | 22 tests, 214 assertions, **75 failures, 5 errors** (`RC=1`) | `collab/yang-clojure/1795702210000-floatkind-evidence/red-node-all7gates.log` |\\\\n\\\\nKey pre-fix failure modes observed in RED:\\\\n- On Node: `\\\\\\\"0.0\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"-1.0\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"1e0\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"9007199254740992.0\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"1e19\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"2.\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"1E5\\\\\\\"` all collapsed into exact integers in `cljs.tools.reader` / `cljs.reader`; `\\\\\\\"{1 :a 1.0 :b}\\\\\\\"` and `\\\\\\\"#{1 1.0}\\\\\\\"` coalesced keys/elements during reading; `1/2` and `1M` were refused or misread; REPL arithmetic returned unboxed JS `Number`s; `\\\\\\\"(inc 1.0)\\\\\\\"` took the integer branch; Gate 3 unmodified-source `\\\\\\\"{0.0 (+ n 1.0) 2e0 -0.0 1 1.5}\\\\\\\"` diverged from JVM.\\\\n- On JVM: explicit `cbor/float64` carriers failed `yang.clojure/compile` (`literal?`/`constant-form?`), `yin.repl` arithmetic returned bare Doubles/reboxing mismatches, `c1-3` manifest/profiles did not exist, and Gate 3 `:integral-floats` had unrecorded pins.\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## 4. Shared Numeric-Token Factory Design\\\\n\\\\nImplemented in `src/cljc/yang/core/numeric_token.cljc` as the single shared numeric-token classifier and carrier constructor:\\\\n\\\\n- **Dialect table (`dialects`)**:\\\\n  - `:clojure` — supports decimal/hex/octal/`Nr` exact integers, `N`-suffixed bigints, binary64 floats (including trailing-dot `2.`, leading-`+`, exponents, symbolic `##Inf`/`##-Inf`/`##NaN`), `M`-suffixed `cbor/decimal`, and `/` `cbor/ratio` (reducing via exact GCD and refusing zero denominator). No underscore separators.\\\\n  - `:typescript` — binary64 floats (decimal/hex/binary/octal) and `n`-suffixed exact BigInts, with `_` separators.\\\\n  - `:python` — binary64 floats (including leading `.5` and trailing `2.` with `_` separators), exact integers (decimal/hex/octal/binary), and imaginary suffix classification (`:imaginary`).\\\\n  - `:php` — binary64 floats (`Real` tokens with `_` separators, leading `.5`, trailing `2.`).\\\\n- **Core API**:\\\\n  - `classify [dialect raw-text]` — pure regex/lexeme classification returning `{:kind ... :text ...}` or throwing structured `:yang.core.numeric-token/invalid-token` refusal.\\\\n  - `materialize-classified [dialect classified]` and `materialize [dialect raw-text]` — constructs canonical carriers (`cbor/float64`, `host-int/demote`, `cbor/decimal`, `cbor/ratio`) portably across JVM, Node, Dart, and Jolt (`#?(:cljd (double/parse norm) :clj (Double/parseDouble norm) :cljs (js/parseFloat norm))`).\\\\n  - `float64-token [dialect raw-text]` — direct binary64 carrier construction for float-only grammar rules.\\\\n- **Adapter migrations (zero address churn)**:\\\\n  - `src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/lower.cljc`: `number-literal` delegates to `numeric-token/classify :typescript` + `materialize-classified`; removed private `host-double`, `radix-double`, `decimal-text`.\\\\n  - `src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc`: `parse-number` delegates to `numeric-token/classify :python` + `float64-token :python`.\\\\n  - `src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc`: `float-literal` delegates to `numeric-token/float64-token :php`.\\\\n  - Added `test/yang/php/antlr/float_address_test.cljc` verifying PHP `Real` tokens (`0.0`, `1.0`, `1e0`, `2.`, `.5`, `1.5`, `1_000.0`, `9007199254740992.0`) lower to canonical `cbor/float64` distinct from `Decimal` `1` on all four hosts.\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## 5. Lexical-Coverage Demonstration & Clojure Source Reader (`src/cljc/yang/clojure/reader.cljc`)\\\\n\\\\nImplemented as a self-contained, portable recursive-descent reader (zero ambient mutable state: no `*data-readers*`, no `*read-eval*`, no evaluation during reading, no global reader mutation). Every numeric token is materialized through `numeric-token/materialize :clojure` **before** any enclosing collection (`{...}`, `#{...}`) is constructed, so `{1 :a 1.0 :b}` and `#{1 1.0}` hold `1` and `Float64(1.0)` as distinct keys/members on both JVM and Node.\\\\n\\\\n| Lexical Construct | Behavior in `yang.clojure.reader` | Gate / Test Coverage |\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\n| Whitespace & comments | Spaces, tabs, CR/LF/FF, commas, `;...` line comments, `#!...` shebang comments skipped cleanly (`\\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\"` inside comments never touched) | Gate 2 (`gate2-lexical-contexts-are-not-floats`) |\\\\n| Strings (`\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"`) | Standard escapes (`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\`, `\\\\\\\\n`, `\\\\\\\\r`, `\\\\\\\\t`, `\\\\\\\\b`, `\\\\\\\\f`, `\\\\\\\\uXXXX`, octal `\\\\\\\\NNN`); numeric text inside strings remains a string | Gate 2 (`gate2-lexical-contexts-are-not-floats`) |\\\\n| Character literals (`\\\\\\\\...`) | Single-char, named (`\\\\\\\\newline`, `\\\\\\\\space`, `\\\\\\\\tab`, `\\\\\\\\backspace`, `\\\\\\\\formfeed`, `\\\\\\\\return`), `\\\\\\\\uXXXX` (surrogates refused), `\\\\\\\\oNNN` (range checked) | Reader unit paths & `yin.repl-test` |\\\\n| Binary64 float tokens | `0.0`, `1.0`, `-1.0`, `1e0`, `1.5`, `-0.0`, `9007199254740992.0`, `9007199254740994.0`, `1e19`, `1.5e-5`, `5e-324`, `1e-400`, `2.`, `+1.5`, `1E5` → `cbor/float64` preserving exact IEEE-754 bits | Gate 1 (`gate1-float-tokens-are-float64-content`) |\\\\n| Exact integer & bigint tokens | Plain, signed (`+7`, `-1`), hex (`0x10`), octal (`017`), radix (`2r101`, `36rZZ`), `N`-suffixed bigints (`9223372036854775808000N`) → exact integer / `big-carrier` | Gate 1 (`gate1-integer-tokens-stay-exact-integers`) |\\\\n| Decimal `M` & Ratio `/` tokens | `1M`, `10.03M`, `-2.5M` → `cbor/decimal`; `1/2` → `cbor/ratio` (reduced, zero-denominator refused) | Gate 1 (`gate1-unsupported-categories-not-relabeled`, `gate1-cross-host-canonical-identity`) |\\\\n| Symbolic values (`##`) | `##Inf`, `##-Inf`, `##NaN` → `cbor/float64` | Gate 1 / numeric-token |\\\\n| Symbols, keywords, booleans, `nil` | `true`, `false`, `nil`, `/`, qualified symbols/keywords, invalid `::` or multi-slash refused | Gate 1, Gate 2, `yin.repl-test` |\\\\n| Collections | `(...)`, `[...]`, `{...}` (even-count & duplicate-key checks), `#{...}` (duplicate-element check), `#:ns{...}` namespaced maps; `{1 :a 1.0 :b}` & `#{1 1.0}` retain both occurrences | Gate 2 (`gate2-equal-valued-occurrences-not-coalesced`), `yin.repl-test` |\\\\n| Reader macros | `'x` → `(quote x)`, `@x` → `(deref x)`, `#'x` → `(var x)`, `^:k`/`^Sym`/`^\\\\\\\"s\\\\\\\"`/`^{...}` metadata (merging onto `IObj`/`IWithMeta`), `~x`, `~@x` | Gate 2 (`gate2-lexical-contexts-are-not-floats`), `yin.repl-test` |\\\\n| Dispatch forms | `#{...}`, `#_` (parses & validates discarded form recursively, incl. nested `#_`), `#'`, `##`, `#\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"` regex, `#(...)` anonymous fn (nested `#()` refused), `#:ns{...}` | Gate 2, `yin.repl-test` (`regex-and-anonymous-fn-forms-read-as-on-the-jvm` now passes identically on Node too) |\\\\n| Explicit refusals | `#?` / `#?@` reader conditionals, `#=` eval, syntax-quote `` ` ``, unknown `#tag`, invalid numbers (`1.2.3`, `08`, `0x`, `1e`, `2r`), odd maps, duplicate map/set keys, unmatched/unclosed delimiters | Gate 1, Gate 2, `yin.repl-test` |\\\\n| Opt-in source positions | When `:file` or `:source-positions?` is passed in `opts` (used by `yang.io/compile-string` & `yang.io/read-source`), attaches `{:file :line :column :end-line :end-column}` via binary-searched line offsets without affecting plain `(read-forms src)` metadata | `yang.clojure-test/indexing-reader-injects-nested-source-positions` + Gate 2 metadata assertions |\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## 6. Carrier Admission, Scoped Reboxing, & Entry-Point Audit\\\\n\\\\n- **`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc`**:\\\\n  - Added `numeric-carrier?` (`cbor/float64?`, `cbor/decimal?`, `cbor/ratio?`, `host-int/big-carrier?`) to `literal?` and `constant-form?`.\\\\n  - Wired `read-forms` (`[source]` / `[source opts]`) to `yang.clojure.reader/read-forms` on JVM/Node while preserving explicit `:unavailable-on-cljd` / `:unavailable-on-jolt` refusals.\\\\n  - Exported `yang.clojure/primitives` delegating to `yang.clojure.numeric/primitives`.\\\\n- **`src/cljc/yang/clojure/numeric.cljc`**:\\\\n  - Scoped carrier-aware primitives (`+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `=`, `==`, `!=`) backed by `yin.vm.float`, `yin.vm.integer`, and `yin.vm.data`.\\\\n  - Mixed int/float and float/float arithmetic unboxes through `yin.vm.float` and reboxes all float results as `data/float64`.\\\\n  - `=` uses kind-strict `data/equiv` (`(= 1.0 1) => false`, `(= [1.0] [1]) => false`); `==` implements Clojure's cross-kind numeric equality (`(== 1.0 1) => true`).\\\\n  - Does **not** mutate global `yin.vm/primitives` or `Float64` `:carrier-coercion` policy.\\\\n- **`src/cljc/yin/repl/frontends.cljc` & `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc`**:\\\\n  - `clojure-binding` installs `:parse yang.clojure/read-forms`, `:lower clojure-lower`, `:lower-forms clojure-lower`, `:primitives yang.clojure/primitives` on JVM/Node, and `:input :forms`, `:parse identity`, `:lower clojure-lower`, `:primitives yang.clojure/primitives` on Dart/Jolt (`:jolt` and `:cljd` ordered before `:clj` in reader conditionals!).\\\\n  - `yin.repl/compile-command-ast` uses `:lower-forms` for form-argument `(compile ...)` commands and `:parse` + `:lower` for string-argument `(compile \\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\")` commands.\\\\n  - `yin.repl/quote-symbols` renders `cbor/float64?` on `:cljs` via `DisplayKey` + `vm-float/format` (preserving `\\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"-0.0\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"##NaN\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"##Inf\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"##-Inf\\\\\\\"`).\\\\n- **Entry-Point & Demo Audit Ledger**:\\\\n  - `src/cljc/yang/io.cljc`: migrated `read-forms` to delegate to `yang.clojure/read-forms` with `{:file file-name}`.\\\\n  - `src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc`: audited — already forwards original source text intact; untouched.\\\\n  - `src/cljs/datomworld/demo/compilation_pipeline.cljs`: migrated from `cljs.reader` to `yang.clojure/read-forms` + `yang.clojure/primitives`.\\\\n  - `src/cljs/datomworld/demo/continuation_stream.cljs`: migrated from `cljs.reader` to `yang.clojure/read-forms` + `yang.clojure/primitives`.\\\\n  - `src/