t \\\\\\\"796d5717fad5b4f5be79e602ba0421480b0544e6522b1c019530b1e3c2852330\\\\\\\"}\\\\n   {:case :map-16,\\\\n    :root :segment/blake3-84d925c00a8849dda46f5e9e528a60fed9362272af386915503febaf1edf9f47,\\\\n    :applications 17,\\\\n    :keys-digest \\\\\\\"9d65bf12420716dfe4efedf656dac18f56af5b77fe4f512227c84589bfc1e0bd\\\\\\\",\\\\n    :c1-digest \\\\\\\"a12317be5d1c0f3e30e34e69c50c45c74efad0d60121706f7216f30d7c76a68f\\\\\\\",\\\\n    :instruction-provenance-digest \\\\\\\"0cd72212d241c5a9d0b9bf796360d09e91bbf98fa3e3cf5aa0a2ce95f93a3440\\\\\\\"}\\\\n   {:case :set-16,\\\\n    :root :segment/blake3-88c6a294f1e93c458d0c8b4b1e87dae7a44ff6c4479d2170af0fefcce670fec6,\\\\n    :applications 17,\\\\n    :keys-digest \\\\\\\"91ccbddf0aa3310fdeba4520c159862badc47a6eb6315d513927c2f6b24e7d05\\\\\\\",\\\\n    :c1-digest \\\\\\\"54f39fe742cea6d93fab4d0e65e01d03e2b09517883d5d93e656f6ab890a7f63\\\\\\\",\\\\n    :instruction-provenance-digest \\\\\\\"ef8c21b6f818383bd097d2d71a6ae85bc3c7f474b5cd733dd4aff169851fa97a\\\\\\\"}\\\\n   {:case :map-32,\\\\n    :root :segment/blake3-b28f6cff1f295481b3ccd747acba0226e9a959f9d46a125f493faca96b979f12,\\\\n    :applications 33,\\\\n    :keys-digest \\\\\\\"7ff031c2af1499aab281b0c1c48114e08d5643e9c5e47f04428a9ba7b66dfb7b\\\\\\\",\\\\n    :c1-digest \\\\\\\"396f336dfe0c7090b516800e4b14aa9b3db1ec01667b2b620098b5d39997a835\\\\\\\",\\\\n    :instruction-provenance-digest \\\\\\\"ff00f6160a89a6dbac3d14134b3b92c407b8426ab8df746d7d6a2e7d172121f3\\\\\\\"}\\\\n   {:case :set-32,\\\\n    :root :segment/blake3-7dcea56d08b933e831ba943dc3359a30f9d56a7a7f85d09d192c3e0064911e71,\\\\n    :applications 33,\\\\n    :keys-digest \\\\\\\"f0ebb492a0d79846a13372fd2c9b7e4d7f4aded9b09c7836862c788c1dbcfab9\\\\\\\",\\\\n    :c1-digest \\\\\\\"1e7173ae4842925197101f4d2309f69afe3d908aa6cc9f168d9ef21409d794f7\\\\\\\",\\\\n    :instruction-provenance-digest \\\\\\\"17d6153febc822c195d8ad62eb52c9102f6246ff60476aadf9377dbdd4fa01d3\\\\\\\"}])\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(deftest corpus-cross-host-reference-test\\\\n  \\\\\\\"B3: every host compiles the broader corpus's canonical constructions to\\\\n   the SAME recorded reference -- root, full C1/key vectors and per-format\\\\n   provenance -- so corpus equality is a cross-host replay gate, not a\\\\n   within-host property. The permutation/representation checks above stay\\\\n   as additional properties.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  (let [actual (into {}\\\\n                     (map (fn [[case snapshot]] [case (dissoc snapshot :case)]))\\\\n                     (concat\\\\n                       (map (fn [[label entries]]\\\\n                              [(keyword (name label))\\\\n                               (compile-snapshot (build-map :array-map entries))])\\\\n                            map-corpus)\\\\n                       (map (fn [[label elements]]\\\\n                              [(keyword (name label))\\\\n                               (compile-snapshot (build-set :vec-set elements))])\\\\n                            set-corpus)\\\\n                       (mapcat (fn [n]\\\\n                                 [[(keyword (str \\\\\\\"map-\\\\\\\" n))\\\\n                                   (compile-snapshot (build-map :array-map\\\\n                                                                (sized-map-entries n)))]\\\\n                                  [(keyword (str \\\\\\\"set-\\\\\\\" n))\\\\n                                   (compile-snapshot (build-set :vec-set\\\\n                                                                (sized-set-elements n)))]])\\\\n                               map-corpus-sizes)))]\\\\n    (is (= (count corpus-reference) (count actual) 18))\\\\n    (doseq [{:keys [case] :as expected} corpus-reference]\\\\n      (testing (str case)\\\\n        (is (= expected (assoc (get actual case) :case case))\\\\n            (str \\\\\\\"cross-host corpus divergence at \\\\\\\" case))))))\\\",\\\"numLines\\\":240,\\\"startLine\\\":1352,\\\"totalLines\\\":1592}},\\\"tool_result_meta\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"call_2ff7bb9770dc46c998ac3360\\\",\\\"permission_decision\\\":{\\\"decision\\\":\\\"accept\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"config\\\",\\\"reason_type\\\":\\\"mode\\\"}}]}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:12971:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"msg_202611262026157dd3ca65361e4652\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"glm-5.3\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"Now I understand the eval flow:\\\\n- `eval-input state line` → probe/bracket-balance → `read-forms combined` (shell reader, cljs.reader/clojure.core) → `eval-parsed*`:\\\\n  - command forms handled\\\\n  - `forms` → `eval-compiled (compile-input state trimmed forms)` — uses the shell's forms.\\\\n  - The `:else` branch (no forms and not forms-input) — for source-input frontends when parse failed? Actually when `forms` is present (parse OK), compile-input. `compile-input` passes `input` (= trimmed string) if binding is not :forms! Wait: `compile-input state input forms` → `((:parse binding) (if (= :forms (:input binding)) forms input))` — so if the binding is source-mode, parse receives the STRING even through compile-input.\\\\n\\\\nSo if I change the Clojure frontend binding to source mode (`{:parse yang.clojure/read-forms :lower clojure-lower}` without `:input :forms`), then eval of \\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\" flows: shell read-forms → 1 (Number, kind lost, only used for command sniffing) → compile-input state \\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\" [1] → parse \\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\" → OUR reader → [carrier] → clojure-lower → compile → literal carrier. The shell's own read is only used for command detection and completeness.\\\\n\\\\nBut careful — `command-line?`/`command-form?` detection uses the shell forms. Commands like `(lang! :yang/python)` are read by the shell reader — unchanged behavior. Good.\\\\n\\\\n`datom-stream?` and `ast-map?` forms — special shell syntax. Unchanged.\\\\n\\\\nWhat about `eval-parsed` branch `(forms-input? state) [state (format-error (:error parsed))]` — for source-mode frontends with a parse error... when read fails, parsed has :error and no :forms → falls to `:else` → compile-source with the bad string → our reader throws → error formatted. OK.\\\\n\\\\nNow — the compile-command-ast path: `(compile-source state arg)` when string? arg — arg comes from `(compile \\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\")` command — the arg is the form the SHELL read! Look at handle-command: `compile [state (render-compile-output state (compile-command-ast state (first args)))]` — args are the read forms of the command line. `(compile 1.0)` — the shell reads `1.0` as Number 1. Then compile-command-ast state 1 (not a string, not forms-input now) → throws \\\\\\\"This compile command expects a source string\\\\\\\". Hmm! So `(compile 1.0)` previously (forms-input) compiled the read form. After my change, non-string args to `(compile ...)` throw. Is that acceptable? The RULING says \\\\\\\"migrate actual compilation callers or explicitly delimit their legacy raw-forms behavior\\\\\\\". The `compile` command preview is a compilation caller. Its string form `(compile \\\\\\\"(+ 1.0 2.0)\\\\\\\")` works through source. Its form form previously compiled the shell-read form — that path loses kind anyway (it's exactly the defect). Options: keep `:input :forms` for the command preview? No — compile-command-ast with arg not string and not forms-input throws. I could make the non-string arg path still work by passing the form through when the frontend is forms-capable... but the honest fix: the `(compile ...)` command expects a source string; a bare pre-read form has no token provenance. Changing behavior: `(compile 1.0)` → error \\\\\\\"This compile command expects a source string\\\\\\\". Let me check what tests use `(compile ...)` command with non-string arg. Search test files for \\\\\\\"'compile\\\\\\\" usage.\\\\n\\\\nAlso demo readers: src/cljs/datomworld/demo/{compilation_pipeline,continuation_stream,equation_plotter}.cljs — audit; they may call read-forms or submit forms. Let me check them.\\\\n\\\\nLet me also check yang.clojure/lower-unit and how read-forms is used there (the linked runtime \\\\\\\"read-forms is the frontend's parser\\\\\\\"). grep read-forms usage in src.\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"f8f41a12ca5b43c1b0a3ac97\\\"}],\\\"stop_reason\\\":null,\\\"stop_sequence\\\":null,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":0},\\\"context_management\\\":null},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"ddb6a714-f920-48fa-a2ad-2a96c22d6c93\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-26T12:26:34.512Z\\\",\\\"thinking_duration_ms\\\":17084,\\\"thinking_display\\\":\\\"updates\\\"}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:13156:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"msg_20261126202646c8443fc3acf740c5\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"glm-5.3\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"The adapter forwards source intact (per the ruling: \\\\\\\"Do not gratuitously change an adapter that already forwards source intact\\\\\\\") — its read is only for command sniffing. Good, unchanged.\\\\n\\\\n`yang.io` — compiles source files/strings using tools.reader directly. This is another source compilation route! The ruling's boundary list doesn't include yang/io.cljc... RULING 5 file boundaries: yang/clojure.cljc, focused module under yang/clojure/, yin/repl.cljc, yin/repl/frontends.cljc, audits of adapter + demo readers, and the audit set. yang.io is NOT in the boundary list. Hmm. But it's a \\\\\\\"source compilation route\\\\\\\". The ruling says boundaries \\\\\\\"exactly\\\\\\\" — outside them report as dependencies. Let me check if yang.io is even used anywhere (maybe it's legacy dead code).\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"512dbaa1d85c4ebfa9f81a04\\\"}],\\\"stop_reason\\\":null,\\\"stop_sequence\\\":null,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":0},\\\"context_management\\\":null},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"bb501eca-83b6-4b32-bd33-78ed636e3706\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-26T12:26:50.110Z\\\",\\\"thinking_duration_ms\\\":3217,\\\"thinking_display\\\":\\\"updates\\\"}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:13284:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"msg_202611262026552994e94459be43e9\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"glm-5.3\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"yang.io is only used in one test (clojure_test.clj, a JVM-only test). It's a utility outside the boundary list. I'll note it as a minor dependency in the findings (its compile-string would still lose kind on JS, but it's not part of the REPL/frontend catalog surface; per RULING 5 boundaries it's outside — report, don't fix). Actually wait — it uses `yang/compile` per form which after carrier admission works for carriers; only its reader loses kind on JS. I'll report it.\\\\n\\\\nNow the demo readers. Let me check them.\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"a66d5a3e38d94a95883f07b9\\\"}],\\\"stop_reason\\\":null,\\\"stop_sequence\\\":null,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":0},\\\"context_management\\\":null},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"aba0afea-2057-45a1-90bd-6a6469139b92\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-26T12:26:59.394Z\\\",\\\"thinking_duration_ms\\\":2398,\\\"thinking_display\\\":\\\"updates\\\"}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:14051:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"msg_2026112620273263522a8e872a4f29\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"glm-5.3\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"`core_impl.cljc` is \\\\\\\"guest operations\\\\\\\" written as quoted forms (source) evaluated in a guest environment where `float/add`, `integer/add` etc. resolve via modules. So `+` in the guest library: where does `+` come from when a Clojure program runs `(+ 1.0 2.0)` through the linked/catalog path? `vm/primitives` binds `+` to host `+`. Under the argument-vector convention, `:native vm/primitive-profiles` — a bare `+` symbol compiles to a direct native call of the host `+` function. On JS, `(+ carrier carrier)` would throw (coercion refusal). RULING 2 execution obligation: the repaired profile must route floating ops carrier-aware. \\\\\\\"Do not change the global legacy primitive table\\\\\\\" — so I need a scoped binding.\\\\n\\\\nHow does `inc` get to `core_impl`'s version? `core-source-name` at yang.clojure.cljc:868 — \\\\\\\"conditional on the outcome/library convention\\\\\\\". Let me read that.\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"cd053cf0ad524d60957d9b81\\\"}],\\\"stop_reason\\\":null,\\\"stop_sequence\\\":null,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":0},\\\"context_management\\\":null},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"fe6d5763-71ff-4698-a537-8642d7c70281\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-26T12:27:37.922Z\\\",\\\"thinking_duration_ms\\\":4166,\\\"thinking_display\\\":\\\"updates\\\"}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:17073:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"msg_202611262028164f0d3f9e421346ee\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"glm-5.3\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"The REPL's primitives = `vm/primitives` + print fns. So `+` in the REPL on JS is raw host `+`. On JS, `(+ (float64 1.0) (float64 2.0))` → carrier coercion refusal. Even on JVM, `(+ (double 1.0) 1)` works via host + but result is Double — fine on JVM; the problem is JS and portability of semantics.\\\\n\\\\nThe ruling (option b execution obligation): route supported floating ops carrier-aware under the repaired portable profile, scoped, no global legacy table change. The natural scoped place: the REPL's primitive table IS composition-level (yin.repl), and the frontend's lowering can route `+` under the REPL route... Hmm, but the REPL compiles with `yang.clojure/compile` (no opts) — free `+` is resolved at runtime from :primitives.\\\\n\\\\nOptions:\\\\n1. Change `make-repl-primitives` to override `+ - * / = == != < > <= >=` with carrier-aware adapters (dispatch on cbor/float64? → float module kernel; else host op). This is composition-scoped (the REPL's own table), not the global `vm/primitives`. This fixes the default REPL route on both JVM and Node, with identical semantics. The linked path (unit-opts, outcomes) routes `+` via native-name? → vm/primitive-profiles (raw host +). Hmm wait — under :outcomes, core-source-name routes only names in core-module/source-names to the library; `+` isn't there, so `+` is native → raw host + → carriers break on JS in the LINKED path too.\\\\n\\\\n2. Add `+ - * = < ...` to core_impl operations (library-level, float-aware) → under outcomes env they become library calls (carrier-aware, like inc/dec). That fixes linked/catalog path. And fix REPL path via option 1 (or by routing).\\\\n\\\\nGate 4 requires: \\\\\\\"execute supported floating +/-/* and comparisons through the actual repaired default/catalog path and linked path, including a non-integral-to-integral result. Record the primitive/profile bindings used.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nSo both routes need repair:\\\\n- Default REPL route: `make-repl-primitives` override (scoped to the shell — this is \\\\\\\"scoped frontend/runtime bindings\\\\\\\", allowed: \\\\\\\"Prefer the existing explicit float/integer modules and scoped frontend/runtime bindings; do not change the global legacy primitive table\\\\\\\").\\\\n- Linked/catalog path: add the numeric operations to `clojure.core.impl` operations table (guest library) — they dispatch on float64? like inc/dec. This changes the clojure.core.impl artifact → new pins for consuming profiles (RULING 3: \\\\\\\"a change to shared clojure.core.impl/clojure.core/clj artifacts requires new pins for every consuming profile whose closure changes\\\\\\\"). That's re-pin work.\\\\n\\\\nWait, but is `+` used inside core_impl's own bodies? `(list '= 'n ...)` uses `=` → resolved as... within the module's compilation, `=` is a free name resolved via the module's registry admission. The impl module requires clj etc. Hmm — adding `+`/`=` to operations changes the module's free-name set? If I define `+` in operations, references to `=` inside other bodies still resolve to the primitive `=`.\\\\n\\\\nCareful scope control: RULING 5 gate 4: \\\\\\\"execute supported floating +/-/* and comparisons through the actual repaired default/catalog path and linked path\\\\\\\". \\\\\\\"supported\\\\\\\" — the set I declare. Minimal honest set: `+ - *` and `= == != < > <= >=`? Comparisons on carriers: raw host `<` on JS with carriers → coercion refusal too. Let me define the supported floating operations: `+`, `-`, `*` (binary at least), and comparisons `< > <= >=`, equality `= ==`. Division `/`? Clojure `/` on floats → checked-divide host op → on JS with carriers breaks; float division exists (float/div). Hmm, include `/`? The ruling's gate says \\\\\\\"supported floating +/-/* and comparisons\\\\\\\" — division not listed! \\\\\\\"Add a test... execute supported floating +,-,* and comparisons\\\\\\\". I'll do +,-,*,comparisons(=,<,>,<=,>=,==,!=) and also `/` if cheap... Actually `float/div` exists and behaves (div-raw handles zero-div like Clojure). Let me include `/` too — hmm, but Clojure's `/` has ratio semantics on JVM for integers! `(/ 1 2)` → 1/2 on JVM, and checked-divide on JS → ? This is integer-semantics territory — the ruling says \\\\\\\"No unrelated arithmetic redesign\\\\\\\". Integer `/` semantics stay as-is (checked-divide primitive). For FLOAT operands, route to float/div. That's exactly parallel to inc/dec's dispatch pattern. I'll implement dispatch: if either operand is float64 → float op; else legacy host op. That preserves integer behavior identically and adds float-awareness. For `/`: if either operand float → float/div (with Clojure's zero-division semantics? float div-raw: 1.0/0 → Inf per div-raw... but Clojure's (/ 1.0 0) throws Divide by zero! div-raw handles: (if (zero? y) (if (or (zero? x) (nan? x)) NaN (signed ... Inf)) — that's IEEE semantics, NOT Clojure's. Clojure throws on / by zero even for doubles. Hmm. To avoid semantic drift, better NOT to touch `/` — keep it out of the supported set this round (report as a boundary). Gate says +,-,* and comparisons. Stick to that exactly.\\\\n\\\\nEquality `=` on floats: Clojure `=` is data/equiv-ish... The library's `not=` uses data/equiv. What's `=` today? Raw host `=` (Clojure =). On JVM (= 1.0 1) → false; (= 1.5 1.5) → true. Carrier-aware `=` should be data/equiv for numeric parts? The ruling gate 4: \\\\\\\"source `=` must be tested against its selected language/profile contract rather than assumed synonymous with data/equiv\\\\\\\". So `=` in the Clojure profile = Clojure's `=` semantics = numeric equality kind-strict (= 1.0 1) false, (= 1.0 1.0) true, but (= 1.0 1.5) false, and NaN: Clojure (= ##NaN ##NaN) → false. data-equiv does exactly this for floats! (and (not nan) (== x y)). For vectors etc. Clojure = is sequential equality — data-equiv also recursive. The library already has `not=` = (not (data/equiv ...)). So `=` → data/equiv is the right Clojure-contract implementation. \\\\n\\\\n`==`? Clojure `==` is numeric equality without kind (== 1 1.0) → true. Hmm! Clojure's `==`: \\\\\\\"Returns non-nil if nums all have the equivalent value (type-independent)\\\\\\\". So (== 1 1.0) is true. Implementing == correctly = compare numeric values across kinds: (== (float64 1) 1) → true. That's float-value comparison when either is float, else integer compare. I can implement: if both exact integers → integer compare; if either float → (== (float-value a) (float-value b)) with NaN → false.\\\\n\\\\n`<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=`: numeric comparisons; with carriers compare float-values; mixed int/float → compare as floats (Clojure does type-promoting compare). Keep it simple: if either is float64 → compare float-value(a-as-float) with float-value(b-as-float) — for the integer side use (data/float-value x)? float-value of a carrier → the double; of a Number → itself... For an exact integer, convert to double — hmm, big integers lose precision, but Clojure promotes to double too in mixed comparisons (actually Clojure compares with ops that use < on doubles for mixed... `(> 9007199254740993 1.0)` → true via bigint compare? Clojure's < uses ops/mixed-compare... it does precise comparison via Categories. I'll do: if both exact-integers → host compare (existing behavior preserved); if either float → compare doubles (float-value coerces ints via double). Edge cases with huge ints are out of scope.\\\\n\\\\nNow — where do these live?\\\\n(a) The guest library (`core_impl` operations): `+ - * = == != < > <= >=` become library ops (dispatch float→float module, int→legacy). Under outcomes/linked, core-source-name routes them. This changes the clojure.core.impl module artifact → re-pin.\\\\n(b) The REPL default: make-repl-primitives overrides the same names with the same adapters (host-function level, no guest compilation). The adapters are plain host fns: (fn [& args] ...) dispatching per arg.\\\\n\\\\nShared implementation: write ONE set of portable numeric adapter functions (e.g. in yang.clojure.prelude or a new focused module?) and install both as REPL primitives and as guest ops. But the guest ops are compiled guest code ('(fn [args] ...)) referencing float/add etc. — they execute inside the guest via module names. The REPL adapters are host fns. They're different mechanisms; sharing a single implementation isn't direct. The guest-library version: bodies written as quoted s-expressions using float/integer module calls (like inc). The REPL version: host functions using yin.vm.float kernels directly.\\\\n\\\\nHmm wait — actually, for the REPL route, maybe better: route `+` etc. through the same dispatch written as a host fn using `yin.vm.float` + `dao.jing.cbor` + `dao.host.integer`. That's straightforward.\\\\n\\\\nBut hold on — is there an existing \\\\\\\"portable adapter\\\\\\\" concept? `yang/outcome-adapter`... and prelude has `library-adapters`. Let me check yang.clojure.prelude for existing numeric helpers and the `admit` function. Also there's `yang.rt.int` (int-rt). Maybe there's a yang.rt.float? Let me check src/cljc/yang/rt/.\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"629ef00169374926b9433b77\\\"}],\\\"stop_reason\\\":null,\\\"stop_sequence\\\":null,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":0},\\\"context_management\\\":null},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"5d600477-5287-43d9-b70b-0c39ccefb63b\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-26T12:29:01.721Z\\\",\\\"thinking_duration_ms\\\":43944,\\\"thinking_display\\\":\\\"updates\\\"}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:18437:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"msg_202611262029050e192c84b414423d\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"glm-5.3\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"Excellent! `yang.python.antlr.clj-runtime` has the exact pattern: `clj.py/add` etc. dispatch on `integer/integer?`/`data/float64?` routing to `integer/add`/`float/add` modules — GUEST-LEVEL adapters written as quoted forms resolved via the module system (`integer/...`, `float/...`, `data/...`, `yrt.int/...`).\\\\n\\\\nThis gives me the model for the Clojure profile's arithmetic: add `+ - * = == != < > <= >=` to `yang.clojure.core-impl`'s operations table, dispatching like clj.py but with CLOJURE semantics:\\\\n- int+int → int op (with int-limit check)\\\\n- any float → float op (to-float-boxed for int side)\\\\n- `=` → data/equiv; `==` → cross-kind numeric equality; comparisons via compare.\\\\n\\\\nBut WAIT. Adding `+` to core_impl's public names changes `source-names` → under outcomes env, `+` routes to the library → the linked path gets carrier-aware `+`. And the REPL default path (compile w/o opts) doesn't route through the library... it resolves `+` from :primitives at runtime. For the REPL route I override in make-repl-primitives with host-fn adapters (dispatch cbor/float64? → yin.vm.float kernels; else host op).\\\\n\\\\nHmm, actually — wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe simpler and MORE uniform: the REPL route could compile with unit-opts? No — the shell's plain `compile` is the \\\\\\\"plain-compile binding\\\\\\\" the ruling mentions; changing the whole REPL to the linked convention is a huge behavioral change. No.\\\\n\\\\nSo two bindings:\\\\n1. REPL default primitives override (host fns) — \\\\\\\"scoped frontend/runtime bindings\\\\\\\" — in yin/repl.cljc make-repl-primitives.\\\\n2. Library ops in core_impl (guest) — for linked/catalog paths.\\\\n\\\\nBoth need the same Clojure numeric contract. The host-fn version can literally be built from yin.vm.float/float-module + integer module + data module fns.\\\\n\\\\nNow — what does `vm/primitives` `+` do on JVM with Doubles today? Host + → Double. After my change on JVM REPL: dispatch — a Double 1.0 is cbor/float64? → float/add → data/float64 result → JVM Double. Same result. Integer 1 → host + → Long... but via integer/add + impl-int-result? On JVM host `+` with small longs is identical to integer/add. Careful: `+` with 0 args → 0; 1 arg → identity; 2+ → variadic fold. The REPL override must preserve variadic arities (Clojure + is variadic). float/add is binary [2]. Fold pairwise.\\\\n\\\\nAlso non-numeric operands: Clojure `(+ \\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\" 1)` throws ClassCastException. Our adapter: if neither float nor integer → fall back to host op (which throws as before). Good — preserves refusal behavior.\\\\n\\\\nComparisons on JVM today: `(< 1 2)` → host <. Mixed `(< 1 1.5)` → host < → true. With adapters: either float → float compare of double values → same. NaN: Clojure `(< ##NaN 1)` → false. float/compare returns ::nan sentinel → lt → (= -1 ::nan) → false. Good.\\\\n\\\\n`=`: host `=` on JVM vs data/equiv — for numbers/vectors/maps/strings/keywords identical outcomes for supported data. (= 1/2 1/2)? Ratio — data/equiv: exact-int? no, float64? no... falls to keyword? symbol? sequential? map? set? :else (= a b) → host =. OK identical.\\\\n\\\\nBUT: overriding `=` in the REPL primitive table changes `=` for ALL languages' REPL sessions (php legacy has its own :primitives; python legacy too; the clojure binding doesn't install :primitives so make-repl-primitives serves all frontends without their own table — PHP/python clojure profiles use :admit clj-runtime/admit with no :primitives?... install-python: legacy? gets :primitives python.repl/primitives; non-legacy gets :admit. So the clojure profile (python clojure-core-v1) ALSO uses the shell's make-repl-primitives!). Changing `+` there affects the Python Clojure profile REPL route too. Is that bad? The Python Clojure profile compiles Clojure-source programs with clojure semantics — its `+` is Clojure's `+`. Carrier-aware dispatch only changes behavior for float64 carriers (previously: raw host op — on JS they'd throw; on JVM Double works same). So the override is behavior-preserving except where it fixes breakage. Good.\\\\n\\\\nHmm, wait — actually for Python-clojure profile, clj.py/add etc. handle `+`? No — that's for Python SYNTAX programs (python.repl lowers python ASTs to clj.py/add calls). The Clojure-profile python frontend compiles Clojure forms via yang.clojure — `+` free name → shell primitives. OK.\\\\n\\\\nNow the linked path: unit-opts `:native vm/primitive-profiles` — `+` native-name? → BUT core-source-name takes precedence (checked first in native-name?): `(not (core-source-name env sym))` — so if I add `+` to core_module/source-names (via public-names), under outcomes env `+` routes to the library callable, not the raw primitive. In the library, `+` body uses integer/float module ops — carrier-aware. \\\\n\\\\nNow — IMPORTANT scope check: does adding `+ - * ...` to core_impl change the clojure.core.impl artifact bytes → module pins? Yes. RULING 3: \\\\\\\"a change to shared clojure.core.impl/clojure.core/clj artifacts requires new pins for every consuming profile whose closure changes; never mutate an existing clojure-core-v1 publication in place.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nWhere are these pins? python.profiles/builtin — descriptors with addresses. Let me look at how the clojure library is pinned in python profiles (the \\\\\\\"clojure-core-v1\\\\\\\" pin) and what re-pinning involves. This could be the heaviest part (gate 7). Let me look at yang.python.antlr.profiles and where module addresses come from.\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"8eeba15675fb4a8ba6156351\\\"}],\\\"stop_reason\\\":null,\\\"stop_sequence\\\":null,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":0},\\\"context_management\\\":null},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"8b9304a1-203c-4a50-b0f0-e396eebc173a\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-26T12:29:32.671Z\\\",\\\"thinking_duration_ms\\\":25992,\\\"thinking_display\\\":\\\"updates\\\"}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:23688:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"msg_20261126202936fe0380ccdf23453b\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"glm-5.3\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"This is the pinning machinery. If I change `clojure.core.impl` (adding numeric ops), its address changes → `module-locks`['clojure.core.impl] and downstream `clojure.core`, `clj.py`?? (clj.py requires clojure.core.impl? profile-modules clojure-id includes clj.py — its module-spec takes pins and requires...). All the locks would need re-derivation, and `revision-locks` for both python profiles. That's the \\\\\\\"re-pin protocol\\\\\\\" — heavy but mechanical: compute new addresses by running the emitters, update the locks. `verify-builtin!` validates at runtime — if addresses mismatch, everything fails. So changing core_impl REQUIRES re-pinning. That's expected by RULING 3 and the task's step 7.\\\\n\\\\nBUT WAIT — do I actually need to add `+ - *` to core_impl? Reconsider: the linked path's `+` currently routes to raw host `+` (native-name? since + not in source-names). The ruling gate 4 requires floating +,-,* through \\\\\\\"the actual repaired default/catalog path and linked path\\\\\\\". Hmm, \\\\\\\"the actual repaired default/catalog path and linked path\\\\\\\". \\\\n\\\\nAlternative that avoids touching core_impl: change `unit-opts`'s `:native` profile map for numeric ops? `:native vm/primitive-profiles` maps name→profile for adapter calls; the actual call is still the raw primitive from the registry. The linker's linked execution resolves `+` to... the linked runtime's primitives table. Where does linked execution get primitives? The composition's VM. In tests (clojure_test?) and repl linked runs — make-repl-primitives again? If linked runs go through the same shell VM, then fixing make-repl-primitives fixes the linked path too!\\\\n\\\\nWhere does \\\\\\\"cold linked execution\\\\\\\" happen? `yang.clojure/lower-unit` produces a module; linked execution via linker... runs on a VM with primitives from the composition. In the REPL require path, the shell VM serves it. So overriding make-repl-primitives covers REPL default + linked-through-REPL.\\\\n\\\\nHmm, but native-name? under :outcomes compiles `(+ a b)` to a direct native call `(primitive + a b)`? The native call goes through the VM's primitive registry — the composition's table (make-repl-primitives). YES. So one fix — the REPL primitives table — covers both REPL default and linked runs driven by the shell.\\\\n\\\\nWhat about `vm/primitives` used directly by tests (e.g. clojure_collection_order_test base-opts :primitives vm/primitives — its float corpus uses non-integral floats and `+` on them... those tests pass today because on both hosts non-integral floats work with host + → but result is unboxed Number on JS! e.g. run-outcome (+ n 1.5) with n=1 → 2.5 Number — non-integral → float content. That's why the corpus only used non-integral floats!).\\\\n\\\\nNow with my fix, source `(+ n 1.5)` where the + is native raw host — on JS operands: n=1 (int), 1.5 (float64 carrier after fix) → host + with carrier → THROWS on JS. So the existing corpus tests with `(list '+ 'n 1.5)` would BREAK on Node after the reader fix, unless the execution path is also fixed. This is exactly the ruling's integration gate (RULING 2 point 9: \\\\\\\"Boxing a literal does not by itself make bare host arithmetic portable\\\\\\\").\\\\n\\\\nFor the collection_order test's own lanes (base-opts :primitives vm/primitives), those tests run (+ n 1.5) via raw +. After the reader fix, 1.5 becomes a carrier on JS → (+ n carrier) breaks the corpus test on Node! Wait — no: the corpus tests BUILD forms programmatically (quoted forms with host doubles 1.5), not through the source reader. `(list '+ 'n 1.5)` — 1.5 is a host Double in the test namespace → on JS, 1.5 is a non-integral Number → float content, host + works (1 + 1.5 = 2.5). On JVM 1.5 Double + works. So corpus tests keep working — they don't go through the reader. Only SOURCE-read float tokens become carriers.\\\\n\\\\nBut REPL route: \\\\\\\"1.5\\\\\\\" read by OUR reader → carrier → (+ 1 1.5) with raw + on JS → carrier coercion refusal. So REPL execution needs the primitive override. OK.\\\\n\\\\nSo the design:\\\\n\\\\n**Execution reboxing — scoped adapters in `make-repl-primitives`** (yin/repl.cljc): override `+ - * = == != < > <= >=` with carrier-aware host fns. Where do the fns come from? A shared implementation — I could put them in `yang.clojure.prelude` (the clj runtime module ns) as host functions? Hmm, \\\\\\\"yang.core shared layer\\\\\\\" is for the classifier. The runtime adapters: put them in... let me think about what the linked/catalog path in TESTS uses. Gate 4 says \\\\\\\"through the actual repaired default/catalog path and linked path... Record the primitive/profile bindings used.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nPlan A (minimal, no core_impl change): \\\\n- REPL primitives override in yin.repl make-repl-primitives (fixes REPL default + shell-served linked runs).\\\\n- unit-opts stays as-is; linked execution resolves + from the serving VM's table.\\\\n\\\\nBut then `clojure.core.impl` unchanged → NO re-pin of python profiles needed → far smaller blast radius. RULING 3: \\\\\\\"Python's py-v1 and already-correct native JS/PHP publications are not automatically re-minted merely because the Clojure source reader changes.\\\\\\\" And the Clojure frontend revision bump handles the reader change.\\\\n\\\\nBut wait — does the ruling REQUIRE the library path (core_impl `+`) to be float-aware? Gate 4: \\\\\\\"In the selected Clojure library profile, inc/dec of floating input must take floating behavior\\\\\\\" — inc/dec already are (existing control). \\\\\\\"Also execute supported floating +/-/* and comparisons through the actual repaired default/catalog path and linked path\\\\\\\" — default/catalog path = REPL + catalog; linked path = linked module execution. Both resolve + from the composition's primitive table → my override serves both IF linked execution uses the shell's table. Need to verify: linked execution in tests (test/yang/clojure_test.cljc? linker tests) — check what primitives those VMs use. The \\\\\\\"cold linked execution\\\\\\\" in gate 7 is about profiles. Let me check how a linked unit executes in tests — search for lower-unit usage in tests.\\\\n\\\\nAlso: what about `=` compiled under outcomes → `=` is in vm/primitives (host =) — under outcomes env, core-source-name? `not=` is in the library; `=` is not (not in public-names? inc/dec/not=/... let me check: operations include not= but not =). So `=` native → host = → with carriers on JS breaks. My override in make-repl-primitives fixes the REPL/linked paths served by the shell.\\\\n\\\\nFor test lanes that build VMs with vm/primitives directly and eval source-read forms — my new tests will use the REPL route (eval-input through a session) or construct VMs with the repaired table. For the corpus semantic oracle, use data module ops.\\\\n\\\\nOK — but one more check: `vm/primitives` itself — is overriding in make-repl-primitives enough for \\\\\\\"the default REPL\\\\\\\"? make-repl-primitives is used at line 855 (make-vm) and 2391 (rebuild). Also embed hosts may construct VMs with vm/primitives directly — \\\\\\\"Any raw-host profile retained unchanged must retain its explicit limitation and immutable identity\\\\\\\" — fine, vm/primitives stays unchanged (legacy raw-host profile), documented.\\\\n\\\\nNow what about `yang.clojure/compile` of `(+ 1.0 2.0)` in a bare test VM with vm/primitives? That's the raw-host profile — documented limitation. Tests for execution use the repaired route.\\\\n\\\\nHmm — wait, actually let me reconsider whether to put the carrier-aware `+` into `vm/primitives` itself... NO — ruling explicitly: \\\\\\\"do not change the global legacy primitive table or Float64 coercion policy incidentally\\\\\\\". Keep vm/primitives untouched. \\\\n\\\\nDecision: Plan A. The repaired bindings:\\\\n1. `yin.repl/make-repl-primitives` — numeric overrides (carrier-aware, both hosts) — this is THE default REPL binding.\\\\n2. Linked/catalog execution through the shell uses the same table.\\\\n3. `clojure.core.impl` untouched → no python profile re-pin for that. \\\\n\\\\nHmm, but hold on: linked modules when run \\\\\\\"cold\\\\\\\" (not through the shell)? E.g. yin.repl.linker publish → execution requires a VM from the composition. In the repo, cold linked execution tests (gate 7) — \\\\\\\"If a shared runtime closure changes, require the impacted profile's descriptor/runtime/derivation pins to reconcile and cold linked execution to pass.\\\\\\\" Since I'm NOT changing the shared runtime closure, that clause doesn't trigger. \\\\n\\\\nBUT — the Clojure frontend revision bump (c1-2 → c1-3?) and lowering profile... The manifest `clojure-manifest` gets revision \\\\\\\"c1-3\\\\\\\" + lowering profile... wait RULING 3: \\\\\\\"new Clojure frontend revision and a revised lowering/support profile... The replacement profile must retain the constructed-collection ordering contract as well as add numeric-kind preservation\\\\\\\". So: revision \\\\\\\"c1-3\\\\\\\", lowering-profile :yang.clojure/kind-preserving-v2? Name it `:yang.clojure/constructed-collections-numeric-kinds-v1`? Something descriptive: `:yang.clojure/lexical-float-kinds-v1`? The ruling: \\\\\\\"exact new names are for the authorized implementation round\\\\\\\" — my choice. I'll use revision \\\\\\\"c1-3\\\\\\\" and lowering profile `:yang.clojure/constructed-collections-float-kinds-v1`... hmm, or keep it shorter: `:yang.clojure/float-kind-preservation-v1`. Let me think: profile names in use: `:yang.clojure/constructed-collections-v1`, support `:yang.clojure/c1`. New: lowering `:yang.clojure/float-kinds-v1`? It must \\\\\\\"retain the constructed-collection ordering contract in the identity\\\\\\\" — meaning the new profile covers both. Name: `:yang.clojure/constructed-collections-float-kinds-v1`. Support profile: keep `:yang.clojure/c1`? The support claim (C1 markers) is unchanged. But \\\\\\\"Do not publish repaired semantics under those unchanged identities\\\\\\\" — the lowering profile changes (new name), revision changes (c1-3), support profile stays :yang.clojure/c1 (unchanged claim). OK.\\\\n\\\\nWait, also `read-forms` docstring updates + capability claims (gate 7: \\\\\\\"correct source/forms capability claims\\\\\\\").\\\\n\\\\nNow — the derivation: \\\\\\\"Source-reader/tokenizer implementation identity belongs in the existing compilation derivation\\\\\\\". The frontend derivation (`python.profiles/derivation` analog for clojure?) — `:derive` in the clojure binding? The standard clojure binding has no :derive — derivations come from... `yin.repl` ast-index etc. Hmm — \\\\\\\"the existing compilation derivation\\\\\\\" — where does yang.clojure compilation record the parser identity? Maybe in `yin.repl`'s derivation of compiled programs (`call-site-tables`?). Let me not over-reach: update docstrings/capability claims; the manifest revision change carries identity.\\\\n\\\\nNow, THE READER. Let me design `src/cljc/yang/clojure/source.cljc` — actually name it `yang.clojure.reader`? The ruling: \\\\\\\"a focused source/token module under src/cljc/yang/clojure/\\\\\\\". I'll write `src/cljc/yang/clojure/source.cljc` with ns `yang.clojure.source`.\\\\n\\\\nBut WAIT — the task says: \\\\\\\"The shared factory FIRST: a shared numeric-token classifier + carrier constructor under the yang.core shared layer (one implementation; per-language lexeme dialect tables) — per the owner's direction this is SHARED code, not a fourth private copy; ts/php/python adapters move to it (their existing correct boxing is a control — do not rewrite beyond the adapter call).\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nSo: `yang.core.numerics` (new file under src/cljc/yang/core/) providing:\\\\n- A classifier: given a numeric token string + dialect → {:kind :integer|:float|:decimal|:ratio|:bigint|...}. Dialect tables: Clojure / TS / Python / PHP lexeme rules.\\\\n- A carrier constructor: parse the float text per host → (cbor/float64 v).\\\\n\\\\nThen:\\\\n- yang.clojure.source uses it (Clojure dialect: distinguishes int/float/ratio/decimal/bigint; ##Inf etc.).\\\\n- TS lower: number-literal currently does its own dispatch (0x etc.) — \\\\\\\"adapters move to it... do not rewrite beyond the adapter call\\\\\\\". TS's number-literal has JS-specific grammar (0x/0o/0b, legacy octal error, underscores). Hmm — \\\\\\\"per-language lexeme dialect tables\\\\\\\" — the shared classifier should accept a dialect describing: float markers (e.g. \\\\\\\".\\\\\\\" or \\\\\\\"e\\\\\\\" present for TS/Python; Clojure: same but excluding M/N suffix forms), radix prefixes, underscore separators. Then TS's `number-literal` becomes `(numerics/float-token? text ts-dialect)` + host-double parse; python's `parse-number` uses the shared classifier for the float/int split; php's float-literal uses it too.\\\\n\\\\nThis must be careful and minimal. Let me design:\\\\n\\\\n```cljc\\\\n(ns yang.core.numerics\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Shared numeric-token classification and float-carrier construction ...\\\\\\\")\\\\n(def dialects\\\\n  {:clojure {...} :typescript {...} :python {...} :php {...}})\\\\n(defn classify [token dialect-key] → {:kind ...})\\\\n(defn float-literal [token] → carrier)  ;; host parse + cbor/float64\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nBut yang.core layer deps: can yang.core.numerics require dao.jing.cbor? Check existing yang.core.* namespaces' deps: yang.core.prelude — let me check its requires. yang.core must stay \\\\\\\"shared layer\\\\\\\" — dao.* is below yang? Let me check yang.core.prelude / uast requires.\\\\n\\\\nThen the Clojure reader module: full tokenizer+reader. Let me now write the reader grammar coverage list (the lexical-coverage demonstration comes first as a RED test? No — the ruling: \\\\\\\"The engineer must demonstrate lexical coverage and occurrence preservation before selecting a minimal mechanism.\\\\\\\" — the demonstration is a deliverable (analysis + tests). I'll write the coverage demonstration as part of the RED tests (gate 1/2 include comments/strings/chars/etc. cases) AND as a findings section explaining the mechanism selection: full-form reader (not postwalk/regex).\\\\n\\\\nReader design (portable cljc, :clj and :cljs only; jolt/cljd never load it — but the FILE is .cljc; reader conditionals guard host-specific parse. Actually the reader is pure Clojure — works on any host in principle; but jolt/cljd lanes: yang.clojure.cljc requires it — jolt loads yang.clojure! The ns yang.clojure on :jolt currently requires treader via #?@(:jolt [] ...) — jolt gets NO treader. So yang.clojure/source.cljc must compile on jolt/cljd too (just not be called). Keep it pure host-neutral Clojure (string ops) — no reader conditionals except where parsing differs (none — regex-free manual scanning, js/BigInt for big ints only :cljs... careful).\\\\n\\\\nNumeric token classes in Clojure grammar (from tools.reader/clj reader):\\\\n- int: `([-+]?)(?:(0)|([1-9][0-9]*)|0[xX]([0-9A-Fa-f]+)|0([0-7]+)|([1-9][0-9]?)[rR]([0-9A-Za-z]+)|0[0-9]+)(N)?`\\\\n- float: `([-+]?[0-9]+(\\\\\\\\.[0-9]*)?([eE][-+]?[0-9]+)?)(M)?`\\\\n- ratio: `([-+]?[0-9]+)/([0-9]+)`\\\\n- symbolic: ##Inf ##-Inf ##NaN\\\\n\\\\nNote: `.5` is NOT a valid Clojure float (needs leading digit). `1.` IS valid (matches [0-9]+(\\\\\\\\.[0-9]*)?). tools.reader's int-pattern requires... \\\\\\\"1.\\\\\\\" → float 1.0. And \\\\\\\"1e5\\\\\\\" float. \\\\\\\"0x1F\\\\\\\" int 31. \\\\\\\"2r101\\\\\\\" int 5. \\\\\\\"017\\\\\\\" octal int 15. \\\\\\\"1N\\\\\\\" bigint. \\\\\\\"1M\\\\\\\" decimal (BigDecimal). \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" ratio.\\\\n\\\\nWhat do CURRENT paths produce?\\\\n- JVM tools.reader: Long/Double/BigInt/BigDecimal/Ratio.\\\\n- JS cljs.tools.reader: match-number in cljs.impl.commons: int-string → if matches int-pattern... For \\\\\\\"1N\\\\\\\": cljs has no BigInt reader? Let me check cljs.tools.reader 1.5.0 source. I recall: cljs match-number: `(cond (re-matches int-re s) (parse-int-ish...) ... float-re (js/parseFloat s) ... ratio-re → (mk-number ...)`. Let me actually read the jar source locally (the ruling inspected it; the jar is in ~/.m2). Let me check.\\\\n\\\\nThen decide M/N/ratio policy for OUR reader. The upstream cljs reader: for \\\\\\\"1N\\\\\\\" it likely returns Number 1 (dropping N)? or js/BigInt? For \\\\\\\"1M\\\\\\\" → Number 1? For \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" → Number 0.5?? That would be a RELABEL of ratio→float on JS — existing divergence. The ruling gate 1: \\\\\\\"Preserve source numeric categories; decimal M, ratio, or other unsupported categories must not be silently relabeled as binary floats to make this gate pass. Retain the existing supported grammar/refusal policy for those categories.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nHonest approach: our reader classifies M as :decimal, ratio as :ratio, N as :bigint. Then materialize:\\\\n- :float → carrier (THE FIX)\\\\n- :integer → host integer (clj: Long/BigInt via existing demote semantics; cljs: Number for safe, BigInt for beyond? what does cljs.tools.reader do for \\\\\\\"9007199254740993\\\\\\\"? Probably js/parseInt → rounded Number → encoder refuses unsafe. Keep same: parse to Number (rounded) — refusal unchanged. Hmm — or produce js/BigInt when beyond safe range? That would CHANGE behavior (today: rounded number → encoder refusal). \\\\\\\"Retain the existing supported grammar/refusal policy\\\\\\\" → keep Number, keep refusal. Hmm, but on JVM \\\\\\\"9007199254740993\\\\\\\" → Long (exact, encodes fine). Cross-host divergence remains for huge int literals — existing, out of scope (ruling: \\\\\\\"use supported exact carriers for large integers\\\\\\\" is a user discipline note).\\\\n- :bigint (1N): JVM → BigInt; JS → today cljs.tools.reader returns? Let me check the source.\\\\n- :decimal (1M) / :ratio: today JVM → BigDecimal/Ratio (encode via tags 4/30); JS → ??? Need facts. If cljs.reader/tools.reader on JS relabels to Number, the categories diverge. Our reader could either (a) reproduce per-host current behavior, or (b) produce the JVM-like carriers on both hosts (dao.host.number has ->Rational and Decimal carriers on JS! cbor/decimal? / ratio? exist — \\\\\\\"BigDecimal on the JVM, the Decimal carrier on JavaScript and Dart\\\\\\\"). So the machinery EXISTS cross-host for decimal/ratio content! Our reader could parse \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" → (cbor/->Rational 1 2)?? On JVM clojure.lang.Ratio, on JS Rational carrier — kind-strict, portable! Same for \\\\\\\"1M\\\\\\\" → BigDecimal/Decimal carrier. That would make M/ratio tokens PORTABLE too... but is that in scope?! \\\\\\\"Preserve source numeric categories; decimal M, ratio... must not be silently relabeled as binary floats\\\\\\\" — producing proper ratio/decimal content preserves categories and is MORE correct. But scope: \\\\\\\"No unrelated arithmetic redesign, float-key policy expansion...\\\\\\\" — ratio/decimal reading is not the float-kind defect; but gate 1 explicitly mentions M/ratio as categories to preserve (not relabel). Options:\\\\n  (a) refuse M/ratio/N tokens with explicit diagnostic (\\\\\\\"unsupported numeric category\\\\\\\") on BOTH hosts — preserves categories (never relabels), uniform, honest. But changes JVM behavior (today 1/2 compiles to ratio content!). Does anything depend on ratio source literals? Prelude sources? The `clj` runtime modules are built from quoted forms, not source strings — the reader isn't involved. Tests? grep \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" in test sources later.\\\\n  (b) produce real ratio/decimal carriers on both hosts (JVM matches current; JS upgrades from relabeled Number to true ratio/decimal) — most faithful \\\\\\\"preserve category\\\\\\\", zero JVM change, fixes JS divergence of those categories too (bonus but adjacent).\\\\n  \\\\n  (b) is tempting but expands the round. The ruling's normative: \\\\\\\"produce Jing float64 Data for every supported binary floating literal\\\\\\\" — the float obligation. For M/ratio: \\\\\\\"Retain the existing supported grammar/refusal policy\\\\\\\" — JVM supports them (grammar reads them; codec encodes them). JS currently relabels (broken). \\\\\\\"Existing policy\\\\\\\" is host-divergent here. Hmm. The minimal-risk choice honoring \\\\\\\"must not be silently relabeled\\\\\\\": on JS our reader cannot reproduce \\\\\\\"existing JVM policy\\\\\\\" without implementing ratio/decimal carriers — which the repo HAS (dao.host.number). Let me check what dao.host.number offers for construction: ->Rational exported; Decimal constructor? Let me look at dao.host.number quickly.\\\\n\\\\nActually — decision: implement (b) for ratio and decimal-M, since the carriers exist and it keeps JVM byte-identical while fixing JS. Wait, wait. Scope discipline: RULING 5 scope: \\\\\\\"preserve Clojure floating literal kind from source to Data/UAST\\\\\\\". Ratio/decimal are NOT floats. Gate 1 controls: \\\\\\\"decimal M, ratio, or other unsupported categories must not be silently relabeled as binary floats to make this gate pass. Retain the existing supported grammar/refusal policy for those categories.\\\\\\\" — the REQUIRED property is: don't relabel them as floats. Both (a) refuse and (b) true carriers satisfy \\\\\\\"not relabeled\\\\\\\". (a) changes JVM behavior (regression for ratio sources). (b) preserves JVM behavior exactly and fixes JS. (b) it is — IF the construction is straightforward. Check dao.host.number for parse helpers: number/decimal? ratio? ->Rational... need a Decimal constructor and BigDecimal parse on JVM (BigDecimal. str) and JS Decimal carrier parse. Let me read dao.host.number.\\\\n\\\\nAlso \\\\\\\"1N\\\\\\\": JVM BigInt; JS: BigInt exists as carrier (exact-integer? js-bigint) — our reader: parse to host big int (clj BigInt/fromBigInteger (BigInteger. s radix...), cljs js/BigInt). Then encoder: int-bytes exact. That PRESERVES category, fixes JS. Good — and gate 1's \\\\\\\"exact-carrier large integers\\\\\\\" control: \\\\\\\"0x...\\\\\\\"/huge ints as BigInt carriers? Gate 1: \\\\\\\"Integer 0/1, exact-carrier large integers, and integer-shaped tokens are controls.\\\\\\\" — \\\\\\\"exact-carrier large integers\\\\\\\" = tokens like 9007199254740993N or plain huge? A plain \\\\\\\"9007199254740993\\\\\\\" on JS currently: rounded Number → refused by encoder. With our reader keeping plain-int → Number (rounded) → refusal retained. The CONTROL in the gate: integer tokens (including large ones WITH exact carriers, i.e. N-suffixed or within-range) must remain integers.\\\\n\\\\nHmm — but hold on, what does cljs.tools.reader currently do with \\\\\\\"1N\\\\\\\"? If it returns js/BigInt... Let me check the actual jar. Also \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" on cljs.tools.reader. Facts first. The jar: ~/.m2/repository/org/clojure/tools.reader/1.5.0/tools.reader-1.5.0.jar — cljs/tools/reader/impl/commons.cljs probably has match-number. Let me extract and read.\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"5a8a9347d6884e858b4b8871\\\"}],\\\"stop_reason\\\":null,\\\"stop_sequence\\\":null,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":0},\\\"context_management\\\":null},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"6aebcc2f-08cd-4b3c-b795-4e6d6145bbc2\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-26T12:31:15.912Z\\\",\\\"thinking_duration_ms\\\":96663,\\\"thinking_display\\\":\\\"updates\\\"}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:29635:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"msg_20261126203137ec99982f1af8443a\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"glm-5.3\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thinking\\\",\\\"thinking\\\":\\\"Confirmed the defect root, exactly as the ruling derived:\\\\n- `match-number`: int-pattern → js/parseInt (Number); float-pattern → js/parseFloat (Number — kind lost); ratio-pattern → `(/ (parseInt num) (parseInt den))` — **ratio silently becomes a Number** (integer when it divides evenly! `1/2` → 0.5, `4/2` → 2 — an INTEGER!). And `10.03M` → parseFloat → Number (float content, non-integral stays float but \\\\\\\"2.0M\\\\\\\" → 2 → integer!). Also `1N` → match-int drops the N (m 8 group ignored — no BigInt!) → js/parseInt → 1.\\\\n\\\\nSo current JS behavior: N suffix dropped (no bigint carrier), M → parseFloat, ratio → division.\\\\n\\\\nCurrent JVM behavior: 1N → BigInt, 1M → BigDecimal, 1/2 → Ratio.\\\\n\\\\nSo for those categories today: JVM encodes bigint→integer bytes (large ok), BigDecimal→tag 4, Ratio→tag 30. JS: 1N→integer Number; 1M→float or integer Number; 1/2→float or integer Number. Existing divergence.\\\\n\\\\nNow, the ruling gate 1: \\\\\\\"Preserve source numeric categories; decimal M, ratio, or other unsupported categories must not be silently relabeled as binary floats to make this gate pass.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nDesign decision for our reader (both hosts, ONE implementation):\\\\n- `1/2`-shaped ratio tokens → construct a RATIONAL carrier: JVM clojure.lang.Ratio, JS dao.host.number Rational carrier. This preserves the category on both hosts and matches today's JVM bytes exactly. Cross-host: JS upgrades from broken Number to true rational — new capability, no regression. Encoder handles both (cbor/ratio? → tag 30).\\\\n- `1M` decimal tokens → JVM BigDecimal, JS Decimal carrier (dao.host.number has decimal? — need constructor; check).\\\\n- `1N` bigint tokens → JVM BigInt, JS js/BigInt (exact-integer? carrier). Encoder int-bytes both.\\\\n- plain int tokens → JVM Long (via Long/parseLong, BigInt when overflow), JS Number via parseInt (retain rounding/refusal behavior for unsafe).\\\\n- float tokens → float64 carrier on JS; JVM: hmm — JVM `1.0` as Double or as `(cbor/float64 1.0)`? cbor/float64 on JVM returns a Double (number/float64-of). So carrier construction is identity on JVM. Use the shared factory → `(cbor/float64 (host-parse text))` — on JVM yields Double, on JS the carrier. ONE code path.\\\\n- ##Inf ##-Inf ##NaN → float64 carrier (Double on JVM).\\\\n\\\\nWait — but what does the JVM currently give for `1e0`? Double 1.0 → encodes float64. Our reader: float token → float64 → Double. Identical. And `-0.0` → Double -0.0 → float bytes preserve sign. JS: carrier with -0.0 value.\\\\n\\\\nNow — RATIO: does anything downstream handle Ratio in the forms compiler? literal? = number? — Ratio on JVM IS a Number → number? true. JS Rational carrier — number? false! So carrier admission must admit the rational/decimal/bigint carriers too (on JS). constant-form? same. compile-literal embeds them; encoder handles them (numeric? true for all four kinds).\\\\n\\\\nCareful: `1N` on JS → js/BigInt — number? false on JS (big-carrier? true). Same admission need. OK — extend literal?/constant-form? with a \\\\\\\"numeric content?\\\\\\\" predicate: (or (number? x) (cbor/numeric? x)) — cbor/numeric? = host-integer? | float64? | decimal? | ratio?. That covers Number, carrier Float64, Rational, Decimal, BigInt. On JVM also Long/Double/BigInt/BigDecimal/Ratio (all number?).\\\\n\\\\nBUT hmm — decimal/ratio in *arithmetic*: (+ 1/2 1/2) with raw host + on JVM works (Ratio + Ratio). On JS with Rational carriers → breaks. But that's beyond the supported float ops; the ruling's execution obligation is floats only. Arithmetic on ratio/decimal tokens = pre-existing category (JVM-only effectively); our JS upgrade makes READING them portable but ARITHMETIC on them is out of scope (host + on Rational carrier on JS would throw — same as Float64 carrier would without the adapter; but we only adapt the float ops). Hmm — wait: is introducing ratio-reading on JS CREATING a new broken path? Source \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" on JS today → 0.5 Number (float, works with +). After our change → Rational carrier → (+ 1/2 1) → carrier in raw + → throws on JS. REGRESSION on JS for ratio arithmetic!\\\\n\\\\nAlternatives: refuse ratio/decimal tokens in OUR reader with explicit diagnostic? Then JS \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" (today: 0.5) → refusal — also a behavior change but an honest one (\\\\\\\"ratio syntax not supported by the portable Clojure frontend\\\\\\\"). And JVM \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" (today: Ratio content) → refusal = JVM regression!\\\\n\\\\nHmm. Options per category:\\\\n(A) Read ratio/decimal faithfully (JVM: unchanged; JS: upgraded reading, broken raw arithmetic) — arithmetic regression on JS.\\\\n(B) Refuse on both hosts — JVM regression (existing valid programs stop compiling).\\\\n(C) Keep per-host legacy behavior for non-float categories (JVM: tools.reader-like Ratio/BigDecimal; JS: the old relabel!) — violates \\\\\\\"must not be silently relabeled\\\\\\\".\\\\n(D) Read faithfully AND extend the carrier-aware adapters to dispatch ratio/decimal through... what ops? There's no add for rationals in the modules (integer module? py runtime uses integer/true-div-boxed for truediv). No rational arithmetic kernels exist. Out of scope.\\\\n\\\\nRuling text again: \\\\\\\"decimal M, ratio, or other unsupported categories must not be silently relabeled as binary floats to make this gate pass. Retain the existing supported grammar/refusal policy for those categories.\\\\\\\" — \\\\\\\"other unsupported categories\\\\\\\" — the ruling CALLS M and ratio \\\\\\\"unsupported categories\\\\\\\"! So in the ruling's view, M/ratio are NOT supported categories of this frontend (even though JVM's reader accidentally passes them through). \\\\\\\"Retain the existing supported grammar/refusal policy\\\\\\\" — the existing policy for unsupported categories is... there is no explicit refusal today; they fall through host readers.\\\\n\\\\nThe safest reading: our reader must EXPLICITLY refuse M and ratio tokens (clear diagnostic: unsupported numeric category — use explicit constructors) rather than relabel. But that regresses JVM \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" → currently compiles. Is \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" used in JVM-only paths that matter? yang.clojure source-string compilation is used by: REPL eval (any host), read-forms (JVM/Node). If someone evaluates \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" in the REPL today on JVM they get 1/2 ratio content. After: refusal. That's a real behavior change on JVM.\\\\n\\\\nHmm, hmm. Let me re-read RULING 1 table row 1: \\\\\\\"Once an external reader has supplied only Number 1, the forms API cannot know whether it originally read 1 or 1.0.\\\\\\\" and RULING 2: \\\\\\\"Integer literals MUST remain exact integers under their existing supported range/carrier rules.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nAnd gate 1: \\\\\\\"Preserve source numeric categories; decimal M, ratio, or other unsupported categories must not be silently relabeled as binary floats to make this gate pass. Retain the existing supported grammar/refusal policy for those categories.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nI think the intent: the FIX must be honest about categories — the test corpus includes M/ratio tokens to ensure the fix doesn't \\\\\\\"fix\\\\\\\" `1.0` by relabeling everything float-ish. \\\\\\\"Retain the existing supported grammar/refusal policy\\\\\\\" = don't change what happens to them beyond that. Existing policy per host differs; the frontend never declared support.\\\\n\\\\nDecision (pragmatic, minimal risk, honest): our reader implements:\\\\n- integer tokens → exact host integer (JVM Long/BigInt; JS Number for safe / js/BigInt for beyond-safe? — hmm: JS currently rounds to Number then encoder refuses unsafe. If I produce js/BigInt for beyond-safe ints, the encoder accepts them (exact) — that CHANGES JS from refusal to success, and diverges from... actually it CONVERGES with JVM (Long/BigInt accepted). \\\\\\\"Integer literals MUST remain exact integers under their existing supported range/carrier rules\\\\\\\" — JS's existing carrier rule: safe-integer Number. Producing BigInt for huge literals expands support. KISS: parse to Number like today (js/parseInt on the decimal digits — radix handling: parse per radix exactly, via reduce like TS radix-int? js/parseInt with radix works: (js/parseInt \\\\\\\"1F\\\\\\\" 16)). For beyond-safe: Number rounds, encoder refuses. UNCHANGED behavior. The N-suffixed token though — today JS drops N silently → Number. If I keep that, I relabel a bigint category to integer Number — that's today's behavior (retain). Hmm, but 1N → integer 1 is a CATEGORY relabel (bigint→int). On JVM 1N → BigInt → integer bytes. Same BYTES on both hosts for values ≤2^53! For huge N values: JVM exact integer; JS rounded→refused. Existing divergence, retained.\\\\n\\\\nHonestly — cleanest within scope: treat N like the JVM does where carriers exist: JS has js/BigInt (an exact carrier!). \\\\\\\"use supported exact carriers for large integers, never a rounded JS Number\\\\\\\" (RULING 4 safe-list guidance!). I'll parse N-tokens to the host big carrier (JVM clojure.lang.BigInt, JS js/BigInt, exact). This is within \\\\\\\"Integer literals MUST remain exact integers under their existing supported range/carrier rules\\\\\\\" — N tokens DECLARE bigint intent; the host has the carrier; using it preserves category. And plain huge ints (no N): keep current per-host (JVM Long/BigInt exact; JS rounded Number → refusal). Fine.\\\\n\\\\n- float tokens → float64 carrier. THE fix.\\\\n- ratio tokens → hmm. FINAL DECISION TIME.\\\\n\\\\nLet me look at what the REPL does when you evaluate \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" today on each host end-to-end. JVM: Ratio content; the value prints \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\". Node: 0.5. With (A): Node gives Rational carrier. Arithmetic (+ 1/2 1/2) JVM Ratio works; Node throws. With the corpus semantics... Actually WAIT — maybe check: does data module / anything have rational arithmetic? `dao.host.number` — let me check what it provides (add for rationals?). If the py truediv produces boxed rationals... integer/true-div-boxed — \\\\\\\"boxed\\\\\\\"? Let me look at yin.vm.integer exports and dao.host.number.\\\\n\\\\nActually, let me step back and bound this: the RULING's normative obligation for THIS round is FLOAT kind. Ratio/decimal handling should be the most conservative: **explicit refusal** in our reader with a precise diagnostic, on BOTH hosts, because:\\\\n1. The ruling calls them \\\\\\\"unsupported categories\\\\\\\".\\\\n2. Any other choice either regresses JVM working behavior (no) or creates new JS behavior whose arithmetic is broken (worse).\\\\n3. A refusal is explicit, honest, and reversible; relabeling is forbidden; silently-broken arithmetic is a trap.\\\\n\\\\nBut JVM regression: \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" compiled on JVM today. Who uses it? Let me grep test/source for ratio literals in Clojure-source strings... The prelude modules are quoted forms (no reader). The REPL corpus (cc4) — check test/yang/cc4_corpus.clj for \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\". If nothing depends on it, refusing is clean.\\\\n\\\\nHmm, wait — actually, hold on. There IS a middle path for ratio/decimal that neither regresses nor breaks: route them through the shared factory to the EXISTING carriers (Rational/Decimal on JS, Ratio/BigDecimal on JVM) for READING (data literals — quoted data, literal positions work fine: encode/decode/equiv all support them via cbor tags 30/4!), and arithmetic on them is out of the supported op set (same as today on JVM: (+ 1/2 1/2) works on JVM host +...). Regression surface on JS only for arithmetic-with-ratio sources that previously got Numbers. How real is that? Source \\\\\\\"(/ 1 2)\\\\\\\" — that's checked-divide → JVM Ratio, JS Number 0.5 — NOT a reader token, unaffected by my change. Source \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" as a literal token on JS: today → 0.5 Number (arithmetic works); after → Rational carrier (arithmetic throws). Narrow.\\\\n\\\\nBUT the corpus/gates compare JVM↔Node bytes: with (A), \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" gives tag-30 rational bytes on BOTH — byte-identical, category preserved. With refusal — both refuse — also identical.\\\\n\\\\nUgh. Decision by scope discipline: the ruling says \\\\\\\"unsupported categories\\\\\\\" for M/ratio. The frontend never declared support. Explicit refusal is the honest \\\\\\\"explicit refusal behavior\\\\\\\" the ruling demands for unsupported syntax (\\\\\\\"Reader support and refusal behavior must be explicit for...\\\\\\\"). I choose EXPLICIT REFUSAL for ratio and M-decimal tokens in the source reader, with a diagnostic naming the category and pointing to explicit constructors. This is defensible, minimal, and honest — and I'll flag in findings that JVM previously passed these through (byte-level note: no existing pinned artifact can contain JS ratio tokens since JS never produced them; JVM artifacts with ratio/decimal literals were compiled from quoted forms/preludes, not source strings... actually could a JVM source-string compilation with 1/2 exist in a pinned artifact? The REPL catalog publications — cc4 corpus programs? Let me check the cc4 corpus sources for ratio/decimal tokens. If the recorded corpus contains none, no legacy custody issue.)\\\\n\\\\nWait, one more consideration — the REPL *is* a user-facing surface where someone may have evaluated \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" on JVM. Legacy artifacts: RULING 3 legacy loading — old artifacts keep their recorded meaning (no change — we don't rewrite old bytes; recompiling \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" now refuses; ruling: \\\\\\\"If source is absent, recovery of intended kind is not generally possible\\\\\\\" / recompilation yields new artifact). Acceptable.\\\\n\\\\nSimilarly `1e5` float, `2r101` radix int (JVM supports; JS drops? match-int handles radixr via (m 7) group — yes supports 2r101 → 5). Our reader: support radix ints both hosts (exact within carrier rules).\\\\n\\\\n`0x10` → 16 both. `017` octal → 15 both (JVM yes; cljs match-int group 5 octal yes).\\\\n\\\\n`+5` → 5. `-5` → -5. `--5` invalid token → error.\\\\n\\\\nNow ##Inf: JVM tools.reader reads ##Inf → Double/POSITIVE_INFINITY. cljs.tools.reader: dispatch ## → symbolic value? cljs supports ##Inf? cljs.tools.reader reads ##Inf as ##Inf Number (js Infinity — a Number, float content since not integer). Our reader: ##Inf/##-Inf/##NaN → float64 carrier both hosts. JVM: Double Inf — same as today.\\\\n\\\\nChars: \\\\\\\\a \\\\\\\\1 \\\\\\\\newline \\\\\\\\space \\\\\\\\tab \\\\\\\\u0041 \\\\\\\\o101 — JVM tools.reader returns Character. JS: cljs returns... \\\\\\\\a → \\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\" (string of length 1)? cljs.tools.reader read-char*: returns (.charAt ...)? cljs chars are strings of length 1. Our reader: :clj Character, :cljs 1-char string. Keep per-host (compile-literal embeds; encoder encodes strings — JVM Character? cbor encode of Character — hmm! Does the current system encode Character literals? number-bytes... value-bytes dispatch: string? etc. Character on JVM — is it handled? (compile \\\\\\\\a) → literal Character → encoder: probably refuses or encodes as string? This is existing behavior — don't change; just reproduce per-host char representation (JVM Character / JS string). Check quickly what cbor does with \\\\\\\\a on JVM... Actually tools.reader on JVM gives \\\\\\\\newline → \\\\\\\\newline char. Existing corpus tests use chars? Not sure. Keep per-host representation identical to today: :clj Character via (char i); :cljs single-char string.\\\\n\\\\nStrings: with escapes; JVM String, JS string. Same as tools.reader. Also verify `\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"`, escapes \\\\\\\\t \\\\\\\\r \\\\\\\\n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\b \\\\\\\\f \\\\\\\\uXXXX \\\\\\\\oXXX? tools.reader supports \\\\\\\\377 octal in strings? Clojure string escapes: \\\\\\\\uXXXX (4 hex), \\\\\\\\oXXX (3 octal, ≤0377), standard escapes. Implement those.\\\\n\\\\nKeywords/symbols: token read then parse-symbol rules (ns/name split; validation). ::alias/kw — auto-resolve: current path? tools.reader with no alias-map resolves ::foo against *ns* — in our context *ns* is whatever the REPL process ns is — effectively 'user'? cljs.tools.reader resolves ::foo → \\\\\\\"Invalid token ::foo\\\\\\\"? Hmm. What does the CURRENT yang.clojure/read-forms do with \\\\\\\"::foo\\\\\\\" on each host? JVM: tools.reader/read with default — ::foo resolves against *ns* (user) → :user/foo. cljs.tools.reader: rt/*ns*? cljs resolves against cljs.user? To be conservative: REFUSE :: tokens in our reader with explicit diagnostic (\\\\\\\"auto-resolve keywords are not supported by this frontend; use explicit :ns/kw\\\\\\\"). Wait but that regresses JVM behavior (::foo → :user/foo today — an accidental ns-dependent behavior!). ns-dependent reading is non-deterministic across call sites — refusing is HONEST and portable. Do the corpus/tests use ::? grep later. Clojure ::kw appears in... the frontend test sources are quoted forms mostly. OK refuse `::`.\\\\n\\\\nHmm wait, careful — `::foo` in tools.reader JVM: (read) with no opts — *ns* is user → :user/foo. But if yang.clojure/read-forms is called from another ns, ::foo → :that-ns/foo — call-site dependent! Definitely refuse for determinism.\\\\n\\\\nMetadata: ^:key form, ^{:m 1} form, ^String form (type symbols — read as meta :tag). tools.reader attaches meta to the NEXT form read (which must be IObj — collections/symbols; applying meta to 5 → \\\\\\\"Metadata can only be applied to IMetas\\\\\\\"). Implement: read meta then read next form, merge meta, attach if IObj else error. Keep same semantics.\\\\n\\\\nDispatch #: #{ set }, #{} — set; #\\\\\\\"regex\\\\\\\" → Pattern object on JVM (java.util.regex.Pattern), JS RegExp? cljs.tools.reader #\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\" → js/RegExp. Compile-literal of a Pattern → encoder refuses? Current: probably unsupported downstream. Our reader: read regex — produce... JVM Pattern / js RegExp (per-host, as today). Hmm — regex reading is \\\\\\\"dispatch forms\\\\\\\" coverage: \\\\\\\"Reader support and refusal behavior must be explicit for ... dispatch forms\\\\\\\". I'll read #\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\" into a per-host regex object like today (JVM re-pattern, JS re-pattern via cljs.reader? js/RegExp. str). What does cljs.tools.reader do? It returns (js/RegExp. pattern flags?) — actually it returns an #\\\\\\\"-reader object... In cljs.tools.reader, #\\\\\\\"regex\\\\\\\" → (re-pattern s) → js/RegExp. And downstream compile of a literal RegExp → literal? number? no... string? no... → \\\\\\\"Cannot compile unknown form type\\\\\\\". Same on JVM (Pattern → unknown form). So regexes refuse at compile anyway. Fine — read them (to not error during reading of surrounding structure? or refuse at read?). Simpler: refuse at READ time with explicit diagnostic \\\\\\\"regex literal not supported\\\\\\\". Hmm — but that changes read behavior vs today (today reading succeeds, compiling fails). Both end in refusal; explicit-at-read is cleaner. But \\\\\\\"explicit for dispatch forms\\\\\\\" — I'll READ #\\\\\\\"...#\\\\\\\" structurally (skip the string correctly) and let the compiler refuse as today. NO wait — simpler and honest: our reader reads the regex token and throws the same class of \\\\\\\"unsupported literal\\\\\\\" the compiler gives today? KISS: read it into a per-host pattern (re-pattern) — compile refuses \\\\\\\"Cannot compile unknown form type\\\\\\\" exactly as today. Per-host re-pattern: clj (clojure.core/re-pattern s) → Pattern; cljs re-pattern → RegExp. Both hosts already have re-pattern. OK.\\\\n\\\\n#() anonymous fn literal — tools.reader expands #(f %) into (fn* [p1__x] ...). This is READER macro expansion with gensym — non-deterministic names? tools.reader produces p1__N auto-names. Does the current system support #()? If the corpus uses it... The expander/compiler — (fn* ...) — does yang.clojure handle fn*? special-form? set has 'fn not 'fn*. So #() would read to (fn* ...) → compile \\\\\\\"Cannot compile unknown form type\\\\\\\"? Actually fn* unknown operator → application of variable fn* → runtime unresolved. Hmm. Today via tools.reader: #(...) → (fn* [p1__120] ...) — compiles as application of fn* variable → runtime failure. Effectively unsupported. Our reader: refuse #(...) explicitly (\\\\\\\"anonymous-fn reader macro not supported\\\\\\\"). Cleaner refusal vs today's obscure failure. Explicit per ruling.\\\\n\\\\n#' var-quote: #'x → (var x) special. tools.reader reads #'foo as (var foo). Our reader: same → (var x). compile of (var x)? special-form? no 'var → application of 'var → fails today too. Just produce (var x) same as tools.reader.\\\\n\\\\n#_ discard: skip next form (recursively). Must handle nested discards.\\\\n\\\\n#? / #?@ reader conditionals: current tools.reader without :read-cond → throws \\\\\\\"Conditional read not allowed\\\\\\\". Match: refuse with same style diagnostic.\\\\n\\\\n#< ... > unreadable: refuse.\\\\n\\\\n'quote → (quote x); ` syntax-quote → tools.reader EXPANDS syntax-quote (with resolved symbols!). Current behavior through read-forms: `x expands to (quote x)? Full syntax-quote resolution against ns... Our reader: how to handle `? Current system: tools.reader expands it. Supporting full syntax-quote in our reader is a big chunk (resolution, unquote splicing, gensyms #). Does anything use it? The prelude is quoted forms. REPL users could type `(+ 1 2) → expands to (clojure.core/+ 1 2)? then compile: clojure.core/+ ns-qualified symbol → core-source-name handles \\\\\\\"clojure.core\\\\\\\" ns → + ... under outcomes. Without outcomes: application of variable 'clojure.core/+ → unbound at runtime.\\\\n\\\\nHonest scope: our reader supports ' (quote), ~ unquote/~@ ONLY inside syntax-quote (else error like tools.reader: \\\\\\\"unquote (~) outside of syntax-quote\\\\\\\"), and for ` itself — hmm. If I refuse `, I regress JVM/Node REPL backtick usage (which half-works today). If I implement syntax-quote without symbol resolution (plain structural quote + gensym for # suffix), it differs from tools.reader (which resolves). Given the ruling's insistence on lexical coverage demonstration but ALSO \\\\\\\"The ruling does not pre-authorize a full parser rewrite\\\\\\\"... syntax-quote is a reader MACRO with semantic resolution — I'll implement the structural subset: `form → (quote form') where form' has unquotes resolved: actually syntax-quote returns the form with unquote splicing — `(a ~b c) → (seq (concat (list 'a) (list b) (list 'c)))?? tools.reader produces that expansion. That expansion is what compiles today. Reproducing tools.reader's exact expansion is risky.\\\\n\\\\nDecision: implement ` ` ` as producing (quote <structure-with-unquotes-as-is>)? NO — that breaks semantics badly.\\\\n\\\\nAlternative decision: our reader THROWS on syntax-quote with explicit diagnostic \\\\\\\"syntax-quote is not supported by this frontend's source reader\\\\\\\". Regression vs today (today `(+ 1 2)` works through expansion). Hmm. How much do I care? The corpus doesn't use it (check). The REPL demos? Check demo sources for backtick. equation_plotter default source? Let me grep for \\\\\\\"`\\\\\\\" in demo sources and tests later. If unused, refuse honestly.\\\\n\\\\nHmm, wait — maybe implement syntax-quote WITHOUT resolution but with proper unquote/splicing expansion into concat/list/seq Application tree? What does tools.reader produce for `(a ~b): ((clojure.core/seq (clojure.core/concat (clojure.core/list (quote a)) (clojure.core/list b))))? For symbols: `a → (quote clojure.core/a)?? Only if resolvable. Unresolvable symbols stay plain quoted. Since our frontend has NO ns resolution notion, producing (quote a) for `a and structural expansion for collections matches \\\\\\\"unresolvable\\\\\\\" behavior — which is exactly tools.reader's behavior for unresolvable symbols! When you syntax-quote an unresolvable symbol in Clojure: `foo → (quote foo) (stays unqualified when it can't resolve... actually in Clojure `foo resolves to the current ns if it's a local... no: unresolvable+not-local → qualified? Let me recall: user=> `foo → user/foo? NO — `foo in user ns → foo (unqualified)? Testing memory: in Clojure, `foo when foo is unbound → (quote user/foo)? I believe unresolvable symbols DO get ns-qualified with *ns*: user=> `bar ⇒ user/bar. Hmm no... Actually: `bar in user → user/bar? I genuinely recall `unresolvable` → user/unresolvable? Let me test on JVM quickly later. cljs: `foo → foo?\\\\n\\\\nThis rabbit hole is deep. PRAGMATIC: check actual usage of syntax-quote in repo source-string corpora; if none — refuse with explicit diagnostic. The REPL's demo default sources might use it (they're Clojure teaching demos!). equation_plotter source default: \\\\\\\"(plot ...)\\\\\\\"? Check.\\\\n\\\\nAlright — @ deref: @x → (clojure.core/deref x) via tools.reader. Our reader: (deref x)? tools.reader produces (clojure.core/deref x)? For cljs: (cljs.core/deref x)? Host-dependent! Our portable choice: (deref x). Under compile: deref is a variable application → fails at runtime unless bound. Today JVM: clojure.core/deref → core-source-name maps ns \\\\\\\"clojure.core\\\\\\\" → deref... under outcomes. Without: unbound. Eh. Produce (deref x) portable. Slight change from today's host-qualified expansion, but more portable and equally unsupported at runtime. Fine — document.\\\\n\\\\n@ in metadata position? No.\\\\n\\\\nOK. Let me now bound the reader implementation and get moving — time is limited and there's a LOT to do. Let me check the demo default sources + test corpora for reader features actually used (backtick, #(), ::kw, regex, ratio, chars).\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"6b1cb9d7ba8b4cd8a75bb6f0\\\"}],\\\"stop_reason\\\":null,\\\"stop_sequence\\\":null,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":0},\\\"context_management\\\":null},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"76b8206f-1454-4b38-9bf4-eb23598f4b2f\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-26T12:33:43.904Z\\\",\\\"thinking_duration_ms\\\":123006,\\\"thinking_display\\\":\\\"updates\\\"}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability-r1.glm-5