ent 0 mantissa 1 → BigDecimal 1 scale 0 — tools.reader BigDecimal(\\\\\\\"1\\\\\\\") → unscaled 1 scale 0. Match.\\\\n\\\\nSo the shared factory (yang.core.numerics) gets:\\\\n- `float64-of-text` (host parse + cbor/float64)\\\\n- `bigint-of-text` (radix-aware exact parse → host big carrier: clj BigInt/BigInteger, cljs js/BigInt, cljd BigInt)\\\\n- `ratio-of-text` (split / → number/ratio-of)\\\\n- `decimal-of-text` (split . → number/decimal-of)\\\\n- `classify-token` with dialect tables.\\\\n\\\\nBut WAIT — yang.core.numerics requiring dao.host.number + dao.jing.cbor — fine (yin.vm requires those; yang.core.uast requires yin.vm).\\\\n\\\\nHmm — actually, hold on. One design question remains for the reader on JVM: tools.reader returns `1N` → clojure.lang.BigInt; my bigint-of-text on :clj → BigInt/fromBigInteger — same. `0x10` → Long 16. My int-of-text: :clj Long/parseLong; overflow → BigInt? tools.reader: int-pattern match → match-int → if > Long range → BigInt? JVM tools.reader match-int: `(if (nil? (m 2)) ... (let [s (...) ^String s (if negate? (str \\\\\\\"-\\\\\\\" s) s)] (try (Long/parseLong s radix) (catch Exception _ (BigInteger. s radix))))` roughly → BigInteger on overflow. My parse: same (Long try, catch → BigInt). cljs: js/parseInt with radix; NaN → invalid.\\\\n\\\\nClojure radix \\\\\\\"2r101\\\\\\\": digits \\\\\\\"101\\\\\\\" radix 2 → Long/parseLong(\\\\\\\"101\\\\\\\",2)=5. JS js/parseInt(\\\\\\\"101\\\\\\\",2)=5. Match.\\\\n\\\\nOctal \\\\\\\"017\\\\\\\": JVM Long/parseLong(\\\\\\\"17\\\\\\\",8)=15. JS js/parseInt(\\\\\\\"17\\\\\\\",8)=15. Match.\\\\n\\\\n\\\\\\\"08\\\\\\\" → int-pattern: 0([0-7]+) fails for 8 → falls to 0[0-9]+ alt → matches \\\\\\\"0[0-9]+\\\\\\\" group? The last alternative `0[0-9]+` with no group → match-int :else [nil nil] → nil → then float? \\\\\\\"08\\\\\\\" no → invalid number error. JVM tools.reader: \\\\\\\"08\\\\\\\" → Invalid number. Match behavior.\\\\n\\\\nFloat \\\\\\\"1.\\\\\\\" → valid float (1.0). \\\\\\\"1.e5\\\\\\\"? float-pattern: [0-9]+(\\\\\\\\.[0-9]*)? then ([eE]...)? — \\\\\\\"1.e5\\\\\\\": after \\\\\\\"1.\\\\\\\" comes \\\\\\\"e5\\\\\\\" — (\\\\\\\\.[0-9]*)? matches \\\\\\\".\\\\\\\", then ([eE][-+]?[0-9]+)? matches \\\\\\\"e5\\\\\\\" → VALID float 1e5. OK my classifier: float = ^[-+]?digits (\\\\\\\\. digits*)? ([eE][-+]?digits+)?$ with at least one of (. or e present) — careful \\\\\\\"1e5\\\\\\\" valid without dot. Trailing \\\\\\\"M\\\\\\\" → decimal (text before M must match float body). Ratio: ^[-+]?digits+/digits+$.\\\\n\\\\nNow ##Inf: symbolic — dispatch ## then read token Inf/-Inf/NaN → float64-of: cbor/float64 needs a host double: :clj Double/POSITIVE_INFINITY, :cljs ##Inf. Fine.\\\\n\\\\nNOW the reader implementation. Sizeable but tractable. Let me also check: does the REPL shell's `bracket-balance` probe matter? clojure has no :probe in binding → bracket-balance heuristic governs multi-line. Unchanged.\\\\n\\\\nNext — CRITICAL pre-implementation step: capture LEGACY artifacts (gate 6) from the CURRENT (broken) system BEFORE any code changes:\\\\n- Pre-fix JS integer-bearing bytes: compile \\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\" (or a small float program) on NODE with current build → artifact bytes show INTEGER content. I need the Node test lane running the CURRENT code to mint these. Node lane needs: npm ci, antlr gen, yin-repl build, then a small node script that requires the built main? How do I run ad-hoc Node code against the project? The node-tests.js path compiles test namespaces. I can write a tiny probe namespace under test/ (temporary? no — keep it as the evidence generator... hmm). Better: write the legacy-mint as part of my RED test namespace guarded to run only when an env var is set? Complicated.\\\\n\\\\nAlternative: use `target/yin-repl.js` (the compiled REPL) — it's a node-script with -main. Not a library for ad-hoc eval... Actually shadow node-script sets up the module; I could `node -e \\\\\\\"require('./target/yin-repl.js')\\\\\\\"` — runs -main (REPL loop