iled single literal's projected bytes via encoder/source-envelope like corpus does, or just (jing/canonical-bytes value)? The custody test loads bytes, decodes, executes without recompilation. A literal 1.0 → pre-fix JS artifact = integer 1 content. Decode on new system → integer 1 (historical meaning kept). New compile → float64 (distinct). The payload should be an executable artifact — use the encoder envelope like legacy-c1-1 fixture (rows + root) so it can be vm-loaded and run. I'll reuse `project-batch`-like envelope: (encoder/project (encoder/source-envelope :cc4/replay \\\\\\\"floatkind-legacy\\\\\\\" [ast])) → tree {:root :rows} → cbor/encode that map → fixture bytes. Loader: decode + vm-load-rows + run (like legacy-fixture-custody-test).\\\\n- fixture B (JVM pre-fix, float-bearing): same source \\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\" on JVM → Double 1.0 → FLOAT64 content artifact. Under the new system loads as float64, executes to 1.0 float, and NEW JVM compile produces the same float content but... \\\\\\\"new compilation produces a distinct corrected JS artifact\\\\\\\" — the JS NEW artifact must differ from the JS OLD artifact (integer→float64). JVM old vs new: both float64 1.0 — same bytes? RULING 3: \\\\\\\"Correct JVM float literal bytes ... should remain unchanged\\\\\\\" — so JVM old==new for the literal row (expected); the distinctness assertion is on the JS side. Fine.\\\\n\\\\nAlso the fixtures record :manifest-revision? The envelope — let me check encoder/source-envelope shape. I'll look when writing.\\\\n\\\\nNOW — let me also confirm how `eval-input` flows to compilation once binding is source-mode... one more look at `compile-input` callers with forms for the AST/datom-stream special forms: `eval-parsed*` (and form (ast-map? form)) — those come from SHELL reading \\\\\\\"(yin.repl/...)\\\\\\\"? datom-stream? — vec form of [op & args]? These still work from shell forms. OK.\\\\n\\\\nWhat about `handle-command` `compile` with non-string arg — after source-mode, `(