datoms?). vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast → {:root ...} tree of rows. Let me check what shape vm-load-rows takes: (select-keys payload [:root :rows]) — the semantic bytecode tree: rows map root→row. In corpus `digest-snapshot`: `(:root tree)` where tree = (first (:yin/batch p)) — the projected batch member is the SEMANTIC tree {:root :rows}? linearize/project-rows tree nil rows — takes tree. OK so encoder/project members are semantic trees. For the fixture I'll store the semantic tree (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast) + manifest revision + expected value, CBOR-encoded via jing — matching the c1-1 pattern enough for vm-load-rows.\\\\n\\\\nLet me look at how the c1-1 fixture was created... it's checked in at test/resources/yang/clojure/constructed-collections-legacy-c1-1.cbor with fields :manifest-revision :root :rows :old-value :old-operand-roots. I'll mirror that.\\\\n\\\\nNOW — let me design the full RED test namespace. Given the enormous scope, I need to be pragmatic about what fits. The deliverable demands ALL SEVEN gates as tests. Let me enumerate concrete deftests:\\\\n\\\\nNamespace: `test/yang/clojure_float_kind_test.cljc` (ns yang.clojure-float-kind-test).\\\\n\\\\nGate 1 — real-source identical strings JVM+Node:\\\\n- deftest source-numeric-kinds-through-frontend-entry: for each token in [0.0 1.0 -1.0 1e0 1.5 -0.0 9007199254740992.0 2e-324 (rounds to 0? \\\\\\\"4.9e-324\\\\\\\" is min subnormal; \\\\\\\"1e-400\\\\\\\" underflows to 0.0)] compile via (yang/read-forms s) → yang/compile → check :value is float64? content (cbor/float64?) AND canonical bytes equal jing float64 tag encoding, AND data/float64? true. Controls: \\\\\\\"0\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"1\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"1N\\\\\\\" big \\\\\\\"9223372036854775808000N\\\\\\\"? (bigint carrier control), \\\\\\\"0x10\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"017\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"2r101\\\\\\\" integers; \\\\\\\"+5\\\\\\\"/\\\\\\\"-5\\\\\\\". Category preservation: \\\\\\\"1/2\\\\\\\" ratio content (cbor/ratio?), \\\\\\\"1M\\\\\\\"/\\\\\\\"10.03M\\\\\\\" decimal content, not relabeled float.\\\\n  Cross-host byte identity: compare against recorded JVM reference digests (jing/content-hash of canonical bytes) — a recorded table so Node compares to JVM-recorded values. THAT's the cross-host comparison mechanism within one test file (like corpus-reference).\\\\n- deftest source-numeric-kinds-through-repl-catalog-route: (shell/eval-input (frontends/create-state {:vm-type :semantic}) \\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\") → run → check the RESULT value is float64 carrier... how to get the value? output-elements state → :repl/result :value. Assert (data/float64? value). And text rendering \\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\". And \\\\\\\"1\\\\\\\" → integer, value 1 integer. Node+JVM same assertions.\\\\n\\\\nGate 2 — structural preservation:\\\\n- top-level, binding (let [x 1.0] x), vector [1.0 2.0] literal (constant), dynamic vector [(identity 1.0)], quoted '[1.0 1] → compiled literal payload: first elem float64? second integer; nested quote, map values {:a 1.0}; metadata ^:m [1.0]; repeated equal tokens at distinct positions: source \\\\\\\"(+ 1.0 1.0)\\\\\\\" → two operands each float; \\\\\\\"[0.0 0.0 0.0]\\\\\\\".\\\\n- comments/strings/chars/discarded: source with \\\\\\\"; 1.0 comment\\\\\\\" / \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\" string / \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\1\\\\\\\" char? (\\\\\\\\1 char literal) / \\\\\\\"#_1.0\\\\\\\" discard / \\\\\\\"(str 1.0)\\\\\\\" — the numeric-looking text inside comments/strings/chars/discards must not become floats: compile \\\\\\\"(do (quote \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\") 1.0)\\\\\\\"... simpler: read-forms of \\\\\\\";; 1.0\\\\\\\\n42\\\\\\\" → [42]; \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\" → [\\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\"] string; \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\1\\\\\\\" → char \\\\\\\\1; \\\\\\\"#_ 1.0 7\\\\\\\" → [7]; \\\\\\\"#_{1.0} 8\\\\\\\" → [8]; \\\\\\\"#{1.0} 9\\\\\\\"?? set literal with float — set constant #{1.0} → literal set with carrier — hmm set as data. Also reader-conditional refusal: \\\\\\\"#?(:clj 1 :cljs 2)\\\\\\\" → throws.\\\\n- macro-generated numeric Data: defmacro producing a float constant — \\\\\\\"(defmacro m [] 1.0) (m)\\\\\\\" → expansion yields float64 1.0. Hmm — the macro's BODY is source-read → 1.0 carrier → the defmacro fn returns the carrier → expansion → compiled? The expander runs the macro fn in the guest → returns carrier → the result form contains carrier → compiled by... the expander's compile? Macro expansion returns forms that then compile — carrier in the expanded tree hits compile-form → literal? must admit (carrier admission). Good test.\\\\n- host map/set occurrence preservation: source \\\\\\\"{1 :a 1.0 :b}\\\\\\\" → both entries present (2 entries) — cross-host. And \\\\\\\"#{1 1.0}\\\\\\\" → 2 elements. On JVM today: {1 :a 1.0 :b} → Long/Double both. On JS pre-fix: coalesced {1 :b}! On JS post-fix: carrier → both. RED evidence on Node!\\\\n- float-key refusal both hosts: dynamic \\\\\\\"(assoc {} 1.0 :a)\\\\\\\" → runtime policy refusal — through REPL route, expect Error (same text both hosts?). The refusal message via clj/perform-policy :coll/unsupported-key → unhandled → error. Assert both hosts raise.\\\\n\\\\nGate 3 — corpus restore (in collection_order_test): add integral-float rows via explicit carriers (data/float64) in map/set corpus + NEW source-string corpus rows through read-forms + extend corpus-reference with new recorded entries (post-fix JVM-recorded). Also the corpus comment fix (r2's narrowed comment → rewrite per RULING 4 item 4). RED: pre-fix, the new integral-float rows FAIL ON NODE (0.0 in test code = integer on JS... wait — explicit carriers make programmatic rows host-independent; the SOURCE-string rows fail on Node pre-fix). Also on JVM pre-fix: source-string row via read-forms gives Double 1.0 → float — passes on JVM. So corpus RED = Node only. That's the honest RED (the defect is JS-side). JVM red comes from gates 1-2 REPL/admission assertions (carrier compile fails pre-fix: read-forms gives Double → compile literal Double fine on JVM... hmm — on JVM PRE-fix most gate-1 assertions PASS (kind intact)! The RED on JVM: carrier-admission gates (compile of explicit carriers fails), REPL route text (\\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\" eval → on JVM works?), execution reboxing ((+ 1.0 1) via REPL → works on JVM), the corpus restore rows (pass JVM), gate 5 prelude audit (JVM audit finds integral doubles in quoted prelude? we'll see), gate 6 legacy (post-fix only), gate 7 revision (assert c1-3 → RED pre-fix since manifest says c1-2!). So JVM RED = gates 5/7 + carrier admission + snapshot digests of source rows recorded post-fix... careful: recorded references must be recorded POST-fix (green), so those tests are RED pre-fix trivially (no reference yet — I record during green). The honest RED core: Node source-kind loss (gate 1/2/3), JVM carrier admission (gate 5), REPL catalog route on Node, revision assertions (gate 7).\\\\n\\\\nGate 4 — runtime semantics oracle (data-module ops + actual execution):\\\\n- data/equiv(float64(1),1) false; nested vectors; float/float true; content= signed zero/NaN distinctions — through the data module registry (like corpus outcome-modules) AND via REPL: \\\\\\\"(= 1.0 1)\\\\\\\" → false both hosts; \\\\\\\"(= [1.0] [1])\\\\\\\" false; \\\\\\\"(= 1.5 1.5)\\\\\\\" true; \\\\\\\"(inc 1.0)\\\\\\\" → float (data/float64? result) value 2.0; \\\\\\\"(inc 1)\\\\\\\" → integer 2; \\\\\\\"(dec 2.0)\\\\\\\" float 1.0; \\\\\\\"(float64? 1.0)\\\\\\\"?? — guest has no float64? fn... core_impl has int?; does it have float?... not in the operations list I saw (int? yes). Predicates \\\\\\\"Check float64?/integer predicates\\\\\\\" — via host-side assertions on RESULTS (output value), plus data module direct calls. OK: assert on returned values with cbor/float64?/exact-integer? host-side.\\\\n- (+ 1.5 1.5) → 3.0 float (non-integral→integral result!); (+ 1.0 2.0) → 3.0 float; (* 2.5 2.0) → 5.0 float; (< 1.0 2) true; (< 2.5 2.5) false; (= 1.0 1.0) true.\\\\n- mutation/control: \\\\\\\"a test mutation/control that dropping boxing fails the semantic oracle as well as the digest gate\\\\\\\" — a test that with raw Number literals (constructed programmatically WITHOUT the reader — i.e. compile (yang/compile 1.0) with a host Number 1.0... on JVM Double — hmm. The control: compile the form with an UNBOXED integral number on JS (identity of the defect) and assert the oracle FAILS/differs — i.e. assert (data/equiv (value-of (compile-and-run 1.0-unboxed-js)) ...) differs from carrier run. Concretely on JS: (yang/compile 1.0) → literal Number 1 → run → 1 → (data/equiv result 1) true (integer!) vs source-read → carrier → equiv 1 false. Write as: the unboxed control run yields a value data/equiv to integer 1, proving the oracle distinguishes. Host-conditional: on JVM 1.0 is Double → equiv 1 false — the control only \\\\\\\"fails to distinguish\\\\\\\" on JS... The point: prove the ORACLE catches dropped boxing. Do: value-unboxed = run (yang/compile 1.0-host-literal); value-boxed = run (compile (first (read-forms \\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\"))); assert (data/equiv value-unboxed value-boxed) is FALSE on every host? On JVM: 1.0 Double vs carrier(from reader→ also Double!) — read-forms JVM post-fix gives (cbor/float64 1.0)=Double → same → equiv TRUE. Hmm. On JVM both are Double. On JS: Number 1 vs carrier → equiv false. So cross-host the assertion differs... The control's real home is JS (the defect host). Gate: \\\\\\\"Add a test mutation/control that dropping boxing fails the semantic oracle as well as the digest gate.\\\\\\\" — a control demonstrating that WITHOUT the reader's boxing, the oracle would fail: on JS assert (not (data/equiv (run-of-unboxed) 1))?? I'll implement: on :cljs, compile the host-literal 1.0 form (unboxed) and assert its result IS data/equiv to 1 (the collapsed behavior) and NOT float64? — i.e. the control shows what the fixed path must not do; plus assert digest difference between the two compilations. On :clj the control asserts the reader path equals the host-double path (both float). Fine — write it host-conditionally with clear docstring.\\\\n\\\\nGate 5 — API/build boundary:\\\\n- compile explicit Float64 forms directly on Node nested+quoted: (yang/compile (cbor/float64 1)) → literal carrier; [(cbor/float64 1) 2] vector; '(quote (cbor...))? quoted DATA containing carriers: (yang/compile (list 'quote [(cbor/float64 1)])) → literal payload preserved; execute via walker → value; serialize (jing/canonical-bytes) → float64 tag bytes; integer controls stay integers.\\\\n- prelude emission pinned across hosts: the pinned python clojure profile / clojure.core module emission — assert module addresses match the pins (profiles.cljc locks) on both hosts — actually that's existing machinery (verify-builtin!) — the gate wants \\\\\\\"Exercise actual prelude emission and pin its contents across hosts\\\\\\\" + \\\\\\\"A JVM-side audit must detect forbidden integral Double constants in host-quoted prelude producers before JS can erase them\\\\\\\". The AUDIT: scan the quoted prelude forms (clj numeric table, core_impl operations, etc.) for integral Double constants — a JVM test walking the quoted forms (source of the prelude modules) asserting NO bare integral Double appears where a float is intended... i.e. assert: every Double constant in the quoted prelude data is non-integral OR explicitly boxed. Let me check whether any integral doubles exist in prelude quoted forms: (cbor scan of core_impl operations data...). If none → audit passes (control). The audit test: walk impl/operations + prelude numeric + py runtime tables on JVM (where Double is distinct), collect integral Double leaves, assert empty (or exactly the known sanctioned set). RED-pre-fix? It passes pre-fix if none exist — fine, it's a control gate.\\\\n- float-producing operation result crossing snapshot/continuation: run a program computing (+ 1.5 1.5) → 3.0 float, snapshot/continue the VM, resume, read value → still float. Which snapshot API? yin.repl has continuation/pending runs; simpler: the stream_eval/continuation machinery — check stream_eval_test for the pattern... This might be heavy. Alternative honest scope: continuation capture via `yin.vm` continuation API (%capture)? Let me check stream_eval_test briefly. If too heavy, do: queue two programs in a session (eval \\\\\\\"1.5\\\\\\\" then \\\\\\\"(+ *1 1.5)\\\\\\\")... hmm *1 history? Actually simpler continuation crossing: use tu/make-observer-session + queue-ast! with a program that computes float then blocks? Time-box: do value-through-session-ledger: eval \\\\\\\"(+ 1.5 1.5)\\\\\\\" then \\\\\\\"inc\\\\\\\" applied via *1? Eh. Look at stream_eval_test for a continuation pattern I can reuse cheaply.\\\\n\\\\nGate 6 — cross-host artifacts + legacy custody:\\\\n- decode/re-encode/execute repaired float artifacts across hosts: fixture bytes (produced post-fix on JVM) embedded base64 in the test → each host: decode → kind/value assertions → re-encode → byte-identical → execute via vm-load-rows → value float. Runs on JVM/Node/Dart (+Jolt if feasible).\\\\n- compilation from explicit carrier forms on forms-capable hosts (JVM/Node/Dart? read-forms throws on cljd/jolt — forms compilation works everywhere): (yang/compile (cbor/float64 1)) on all four hosts → same root.\\\\n- legacy custody: the two archived fixtures (JS integer-bearing pre-fix; JVM float-bearing pre-fix) → decode, old meaning, execute without recompilation, new compile distinct (JS). \\\\n- \\\\\\\"do not label Dart/Jolt execution of relayed source as native source parsing\\\\\\\" — capability claims: read-forms on :cljd/:jolt throws (assert the diagnostic) — a jolt/dart-leg assertion.\\\\n\\\\nGate 7 — profiles and revisions:\\\\n- assert (frontends/clojure-manifest) revision \\\\\\\"c1-3\\\\\\\", lowering profile new name, support :yang.clojure/c1; jing/bytecode/C1 versions unchanged (call-site-version 1 still; compare vm constants?); old pins immutable: python profiles verify-builtin! still passes (locks unchanged) — assert (python.profiles/builtin clojure-id) addresses match the recorded constants (i.e. my changes didn't disturb them).\\\\n- ts/php/python producers retain artifacts: e.g. TS float lowering still float64 (compile a TS number token? heavier — use numerics unit tests + existing float_address_test as oracle — just RUN those test namespaces as regression).\\\\n\\\\nCross-host recorded references (gate 1/3): recorded JVM digests embedded — like corpus-reference. Record during green.\\\\n\\\\nOK. Also \\\\\\\"test/yang/clojure/stream_eval_test.cljc as appropriate\\\\\\\" — check it for source reading... grep read-forms there: none (only eval-input). stream_eval uses eval-input with source strings! It'll exercise the new route automatically — regression coverage. And repl tests exercise heavily.\\\\n\\\\nNow, before writing tests — decide the fixture format & write the LEGACY CAPTURE namespace (needed BEFORE code changes; Node lane building now). Legacy capture:\\\\n- Program: \\\\\\\"(def f (fn [] 1.0)) (f)\\\\\\\" — compiled via read-forms+compile-program? For loader-execution simplicity use single form program: the value 1.0 alone, or (identity 1.0)? identity is a lib call... Keep the artifact a plain literal program: forms [\\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\"] → clojure-lower → (yang/compile 1.0-Double-on-JVM / 1-Number-on-JS) → ast {:type :literal :value}. Semantic bytecode → rows. Execute → value. Pre-fix JS artifact: literal INTEGER 1 (the defect, archived). Pre-fix JVM: literal FLOAT64 1.0. Payload fields: {:yang.clojure.floatkind/manifest \\\\\\\"pre-fix@7f4898f4\\\\\\\" :host :jvm/:node :source \\\\\\\"1.0\\\\\\\" :root ... :rows ... :old-value ...}.\\\\n  BUT rows with literal value — semantic rows encode the VALUE into the row bytes; vm-load-rows needs the row map. vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast → {:root :rows}? Let me confirm shape — corpus: (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast) → tree; legacy test: (ast-walker/vm-load-rows vm (select-keys payload [:root :rows]) vm/ast-contract). And digest-snapshot uses (:root tree). So tree = {:root r :rows {r row...}}? I'll mirror the c1-1 payload: {:manifest-revision :root :rows :old-value}. Write via cbor/encode → file.\\\\n  \\\\n  Wait — is the VALUE inside rows as DATA (in-memory Clojure data with Double/carrier) — cbor/encode of the rows map must handle the value: pre-fix JS Number 1 → integer bytes; JVM Double → float64 bytes. Decode on any host: integer 1 / Float64. \\\\n\\\\nLet me now write the legacy capture namespace and run it on JVM NOW (yin-repl build still running for node). Then write the main test namespace.\\\\n\\\\nOne more check — vm/ast->semantic-bytecode output shape:\\\",\\\"signature\\\":\\\"a23d5d2e86f94dfa83fe8ea6\\\"}],\\\"stop_reason\\\":null,\\\"stop_sequence\\\":null,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":0},\\\"context_management\\\":null},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"ff3fea0a-c6da-419f-8464-d9b39b325251\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-26T12:43:19.191Z\\\",\\\"thinking_duration_ms\\\":85622,\\\"thinking_display\\\":\\\"updates\\\"}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1795695531000-engineer-float-kind-portability-r1.glm-5.3.stdout.log:49371:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"message\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"msg_2026112620432879711a4be8ae411b\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"message\\\",\\\"role\\\":\\\"assistant\\\",\\\"model\\\":\\\"glm-5.3\\\",\\\"content\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool_use\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"call_e9f74cb97ceb42d39ff85a2d\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"Write\\\",\\\"input\\\":{\\\"file_path\\\":\\\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-floatkind/test/yang/floatkind_legacy_capture_test.cljc\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"(ns yang.floatkind-legacy-capture-test\\\\n  \\\\\\\"ONE-SHOT pre-fix evidence capture (float-kind portability r1, RULING 5\\\\n   gate 6): mints the legacy custody fixtures from the CURRENT compiler —\\\\n   the JVM float-bearing artifact and the Node integer-bearing artifact of\\\\n   the SAME source — and writes them under test/resources/yang/clojure/.\\\\n\\\\n   Run once on each host against the UNREPAIRED tree, then delete this\\\\n   namespace. The fixtures it leaves behind are the genuine pre-fix bytes\\\\n   the custody test loads and executes without recompilation afterwards;\\\\n   an artifact synthesized by the repaired compiler would not be legacy\\\\n   evidence.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is]]\\\\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\\\\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\\\\n            [yang.clojure :as yang]\\\\n            [yin.vm :as vm]))\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(def ^:private legacy-source\\\\n  \\\\\\\"The custody witness program: a float literal under a closure call.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  \\\\\\\"((fn [] 1.0))\\\\\\\")\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(defn- write-bytes!\\\\n  [path payload]\\\\n  (let [bytes (cbor/encode payload)]\\\\n    #?(:clj (clojure.java.io/make-parents path)\\\\n       :cljs (js/require \\\\\\\"fs\\\\\\\"))\\\\n    #?(:clj (with-open [w (clojure.java.io/output-stream path)]\\\\n               (.write w ^bytes bytes))\\\\n       :cljs (.writeFileSync (js/require \\\\\\\"fs\\\\\\\") path bytes))\\\\n    bytes))\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(deftest capture-legacy-float-kind-artifact\\\\n  (let [ast (yang/compile-program (yang/read-forms legacy-source))\\\\n        tree (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast)\\\\n        host #?(:clj :jvm :cljs :node :default :other)\\\\n        payload {:yang.clojure.floatkind/legacy true\\\\n                 :yang.clojure.floatkind/host host\\\\n                 :yang.clojure.floatkind/source legacy-source\\\\n                 :yang.clojure.floatkind/pre-fix-ref \\\\\\\"7f4898f4 c1-2\\\\\\\"\\\\n                 :manifest-revision \\\\\\\"c1-2\\\\\\\"\\\\n                 :root (:root tree)\\\\n                 :rows (:rows tree)}\\\\n        path (case host\\\\n               :jvm \\\\\\\"test/resources/yang/clojure/float-kind-legacy-jvm-float.cbor\\\\\\\"\\\\n               :node \\\\\\\"test/resources/yang/clojure/float-kind-legacy-node-integer.cbor\\\\\\\"\\\\n               nil)\\\\n        bytes (when path (write-bytes! path payload))]\\\\n    (when path\\\\n      (println :floatkind-legacy-capture host\\\\n               :bytes (count bytes)\\\\n               :sha256 (jing/content-hash {:bytes (vec bytes)}))\\\\n      ;; the captured literal keeps the HOST's current kind: JVM float64\\\\n      ;; content, Node whatever cljs.tools.reader produced (pre-fix, an\\\\n      ;; integer). Both facts are the evidence; neither is corrected here.\\\\n      (is (pos? (count bytes))))))\\\\n\\\"}}],\\\"stop_reason\\\":null,\\\"stop_sequence\\\":null,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":0},\\\"context_management\\\":null},\\\"parent_tool_use_id\\\":null,\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"519cc353-4ebf-4ee9-a727-f6a51287dd1a\\\",\\\"uuid\\\":\\\"13e3a592-6da4-42e5-9b97-8d07ade14fe9\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":\\\"2026-11-26T12:43:48.441Z\\\",\\\"wire_tool_inputs\\\":{\\\"call_e9f74cb97ceb42d39ff85a2d\\\":{\\\"file_path\\\":\\\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-floatkind/test/yang/floatkind_legacy_capture_test.cljc\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"(ns yang.floatkind-legacy-capture-test\\\\n  \\\\\\\"ONE-SHOT pre-fix evidence capture (float-kind portability r1, RULING 5\\\\n   gate 6): mints the legacy custody fixtures from the CURRENT compiler —\\\\n   the JVM float-bearing artifact and the Node integer-bearing artifact of\\\\n   the SAME source — and writes them under test/resources/yang/clojure/.\\\\n\\\\n   Run once on each host against the UNREPAIRED tree, then delete this\\\\n   namespace. The fixtures it leaves behind are the genuine pre-fix bytes\\\\n   the custody test loads and executes without recompilation afterwards;\\\\n   an artifact synthesized by the repaired compiler would not be legacy\\\\n   evidence.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is]]\\\\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\\\\n            [da